Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Memphis TN

559 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 558 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025

 

- A slight warming trend will begin on Wednesday with afternoon

  highs in the low to mid 90s through the end of the week.



- The Mid-South will remain predominantly rain-free through the

  next week. Low (up to 30%) chances for showers return by next 

  Tuesday.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

(This afternoon through next Tuesday)

Issued at 1210 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Weak rex blocking continues across the eastern half of the

continent, with the Mid-South underneath the weak upper low 

in the blocking pattern's southern periphery. This low has 

managed to keep the regions highs around our climatological 

average (upper 80s) due to weak cold air advection, barring areas

that have seen persistent cloud cover. This pattern will continue

today, but the upper blocking should begin to weaken through the 

end of the week. Upper heights will begin to rise with 

temperatures following suit thereafter. By Friday, much of the 

region will see highs return to the mid 90s with heat indices 

attempting to climb into the triple digits across portions of 

northwest Mississippi along the Mississippi River. This pattern

shift will continue into the weekend with triple digit heat index

values becoming more widespread each day. Only after Monday do we

see values that may require heat headlines. However, locations

that manage to get to 105+ F still look to be scattered,

potentially diminishing confidence in any products. Regardless,

temperatures will rise and become uncomfortably warm again

through

the period.



PoPs remain low within the forecast period as well, thanks to dry

mid-levels that have entrained most attempts at deeper convection

over the last few days. Looking upstream, upper moisture appears 

to be at or below climatology, meaning that these layers will 

remain dry for some time. GFS/ECMWF guidance provides support to 

this idea with PWATs remaining at or below 1.75" until next week,

even in the presence of 2m dew points in the 70s. Therefore, 

afternoon showers seem unlikely, and isolated at best, until 

moisture from lower latitudes can return. The earliest chance at 

this occurring appears to be next week when upper ridging to our 

east brings back southerly mid-level flow. NBM has followed this 

trend as well with PoPs remain 30% or higher relegated to 

northeast Mississippi until Tuesday where higher afternoon storm 

coverage seems possible.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 558 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Patchy fog may develop again at MKL overnight resulting in

temporary MVFR visibilities. Otherwise, VFR conditions and light

winds are expected to prevail through the period.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 1210 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



No fire weather concerns this period as light 20ft winds and

MinRH 

values above 50 percent persist.



&&





.MEG WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

AR...None.

MO...None.

MS...None.

TN...None.

&&



