041

FZAK80 PAFC 062301

ICEAFC



Sea Ice Forecast for Western and Arctic Alaskan Coastal Waters 

National Weather Service - Alaska Sea Ice Program 

301 PM AKDT Wednesday 6 August 2025



FORECAST VALID...Monday 11 August 2025



ANALYSIS CONFIDENCE...High



SYNOPSIS...A low over the Bering Sea moves east into the Gulf of 

Alaska through the weekend while high pressure follows in tow. High 

pressure builds over the Chukchi and Beaufort Seas through Thursday. 

A low drops southward from the Pole into the Beaufort Sea on 

Thursday and Friday and then weakens through the weekend. By Monday, 

high pressure builds across the Chukchi and Beaufort Seas. 



-BEAUFORT SEA-



PKZ505-Central US Arctic Offshore-

PKZ510-Eastern US Arctic Offshore-



Ice covered.



PKZ813-Point Franklin to Cape Halkett out to 15 nm-

PKZ814-Cape Halkett to Flaxman Island out to 15 nm-

PKZ815-Flaxman Island to Demarcation Point out to 15 nm-

PKZ859-Point Franklin to Cape Halkett from 15 to 75 nm-

PKZ860-Cape Halkett to Flaxman Island from 15 to 80 nm-

PKZ861-Flaxman Island to Demarcation Point from 15 to 75 nm-



The main ice edge in the Beaufort Sea extends from near 70 12’N 145 

25’W to 70 38’N 140 18’W to 69 59’N 135 1’W and continues into 

Canadian waters. The ice edge is open water. 



From land-based points in Alaska, the main ice edge in the Beaufort 

Sea extends from 35 nm west of Kaktovik to 70 nm northeast of 

Kaktovik to 125 nm northeast of Demarcation Point and continues into 

Canadian waters. The ice edge is open water. 



FORECAST FOR THE BEAUFORT SEA (DAYS 1 through 5)...Forecast 

confidence is moderate. Near the coast, winds will remain light and 

variable through Friday and then shift light northerly from Saturday 

through Monday. Expect sea ice to mainly move with local tides and 

currents and drift 5-10 nm/day with the Beaufort Gyre. The low 

pressure dropping into the area will bring some light to moderate 

westerly winds (across the pack ice farther north away from the 

coast) which will counteract this movement over the weekend. Expect 

the ice free area from the Mackenzie River Delta to continue to push 

westward into Alaska waters. Overall, expect continued slow melt of 

the existing sea ice. 



-CHUKCHI SEA-



PKZ500-Western US Arctic Offshore-

PKZ505-Central US Arctic Offshore-

PKZ813-Point Franklin to Cape Halkett out to 15 nm-

PKZ859-Point Franklin to Cape Halkett from 15 to 75 nm-



Ice covered.



PKZ858-Cape Beaufort to Point Franklin from 15 to 85 nm-



The main ice edge in the Chukchi Sea extends from near Utqiagvik to 

71 31’N 166 27’W to 69 54’N 169 21’W to 70 45’N 171 42’W to 71 28’N 

169 12’W to 71 35’N 174 13’W and continues into Russian waters. The 

ice edge is open water. 



From land-based points in Alaska, the main ice edge in the Chukchi 

Sea extends from near Utqiagvik to 180 nm west of Utqiagvik to 125 

nm west of Point Lay to 180 nm northwest of Point Lay to 155 nm 

north-northwest of Point Lay to 240 nm northwest of Point Lay and 

continues into Russian waters. The ice edge is open water. 



FORECAST FOR THE CHUKCHI SEA (DAYS 1 through 5)...Forecast 

confidence is high. Winds will be generally northerly through the 

forecast period, however periods of northeasterly and northwesterly 

winds will develop at times. Expect winds to bring the ice edge 

southward 10-15 nm/day while the ice pack continues to degrade. 





&& 

Fenrich



