[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.7577","issue":"2025-08-06T23:01:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KUNR","awipsid":"AFDUNR"}]
=================================================



052

FXUS63 KUNR 062301

AFDUNR



Area Forecast Discussion For Western SD and Northeastern WY

National Weather Service Rapid City SD

501 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot Thursday, with high temperatures in the 90s and low 100s.



- Cooler for Friday and the weekend.



- Isolated to scattered thunderstorms will continue into early

  next week.



&&



.DISCUSSION...(This Evening Through Wednesday)

Issued at 237 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The upper ridge will continue to build through Thursday, before a 

trough moves from the Pacific Northwest into the Northern Plains by 

Friday night. The upper trough will bring a cold front through the 

forecast area Thursday night and Friday. 



While under the influence of the ridge, impulses will continue to 

advect along the northern periphery of the ridge tonight and 

Thursday, supporting isolated thunderstorm chances across most of 

the area. There is the potential for isolated strong to severe 

storms given sufficient CAPE and shear in place. 



The thermal ridge becomes centered over the forecast area on 

Thursday, resulting in high temperatures reaching the 90s to low 

100s. With dew point temperatures in the upper 60s to near 70s

degrees, a heat advisory may be needed. However, there is still

some uncertainty on how far east the dry air will make it, as

dew points may only be in the low 60s. Therefore, have decided 

against any headlines at this time and will let the next shift

re-evaluate based on the latest guidance. 



The warm air in the lower levels should result in a strong cap

over most of the area. However, there are some signals that deep 

mixing could occur on the backside of the thermal ridge, which 

could support a few very high based cells in portions of 

northeastern Wyoming into far southwestern South Dakota, while 

other storms develop over northwestern South Dakota as the cold 

front begins to drop southward into the area. Speaking of 

northeastern Wyoming and southwestern South Dakota, fuel 

conditions have become critical in southern Campbell and western 

Fall River Counties. These critical fuels combined with the hot 

temperatures, gusty winds, and low relative humidity has prompted 

the issuance of a fire weather watch for fire weather zones 315 

and 322. 



The cold front begins to pass through the forecast area Thursday

night, before moving through the entire area on Friday. With the

support of the upper trough and the cold front, northwestern 

South Dakota could see isolated to scatted storms Thursday night

and Friday. The strongest dynamics look to be over North Dakota,

but with plenty of CAPE and shear, any storm that makes it into

northwestern South Dakota will be strong to severe. As the cold

front continues to push through Friday, temperatures behind the

front will be cooler and there will be chances for scattered showers

and storms.



Models continue to keep chances for isolated storms in the

forecast through early next week as the upper trough moves east

and the ridge over the western CONUS begins to rebuild. However, 

the forecast area will be in northwest flow, so overall, drier

conditions should prevail.



&&



.AVIATION...(For the 00Z TAFS Through 00Z Thursday Evening)

Issued At 501 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Isolated thunderstorms will be possible through this evening. 

Otherwise, VFR conditions are expected through the TAF period.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued At 237 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



An approaching cold front on Thursday will bring dry air and gusty

west to southwest winds into the area from the southwest. High

temperatures will reach into the 90s to low 100s while relative

humidity falls below 15 percent over portions of northeastern

Wyoming and far southwestern South Dakota. West to southwest winds

of 12 to 18 mph and gusts of 20 to 30 mph will result in critical

to elevated fire weather conditions for portions of the area.

Additionally, there is a small chance for an isolated dry

thunderstorm in this area. Therefore, a fire weather watch for

Thursday has been issued for fire weather zones 315 and 322

(southern Campbell County WY and western Fall River County 

SD) where fuels have become critical.



&&



.UNR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

SD...Fire Weather Watch from Thursday afternoon through Thursday 

     evening for SDZ322.

WY...Fire Weather Watch from Thursday afternoon through Thursday 

     evening for WYZ315.

&&



$$



DISCUSSION/KEY MESSAGES...Smith

AVIATION...Wong

FIRE WEATHER...Smith



