[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.7579","issue":"2025-08-06T23:02:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KBGM","awipsid":"AFDBGM"}]
=================================================



054

FXUS61 KBGM 062302

AFDBGM



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Binghamton NY

702 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Hazy and smokey skies will continue along with a few scattered 

showers and thunderstorms. Seasonable temperatures are forecast 

tonight, with valley fog possible. Hazy conditions continue on 

Thursday, otherwise sunny skies are expected. Dry and warm 

weather then continues Friday and Saturday, before hot weather 

returns Sunday through at least Tuesday next week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/...

130 PM Update



Key Messages: 



-Smoke and haze will remain in place across much of the area

 through at least Thursday afternoon or evening. Air Quality 

 Alerts in effect from NY and PA DEC/DEP.



-Pulse thunderstorms will be around portions of Central NY this

 afternoon and early evening. Low shear values point to these

 storms not turning particularly strong or severe. Irregardless

 storms will produce cloud to ground lightning, locally heavy

 downpours and wind gusts up to 35 mph at times. Storms

 completely dissipate by late evening.



We are seeing a mix of several different cloud layers today, with 

scattered cumulus development, a broken layer of mid to high level 

clouds and a layer of smoke/haze as well. This is all adding up to 

mainly cloudy conditions persisting into this evening. Surface solar

radiation is down quite a bit (400-700 W/M2) midday compared to

what would normally be expected if skies were clear (around 1000

W/M2). This just means that temperatures will be held in check 

today, as highs reach the mid-70s to lower 80s...except some 

mid-80s for the I-90 corridor and Mohawk Valley area. Latest 

GOES-East visible loop shows thick cirrus clouds racing into the

Twin Tiers, NE and Central PA at this time. These will push 

into the Syracuse/Utica/Rome area by mid to late afternoon. 

There are also already some pop up showers and isolated t'storms

forming near and just south of I-90 in Central NY. Expect more 

isolated to scattered t'storms to pop up and slightly expand in 

coverage late afternoon to early evening. These storms will not 

be severe, but will be slow moving with some breezy outflow 

winds and local downpours possible.



Quiet and dry weather returns tonight with haze and smoke aloft 

still over the area. Valley fog will also be possible late tonight 

into early Thursday morning. Seasonable lows  in the upper 50s to 

mid-60s expected.



On Thursday we will not have the mid and high level clouds to 

contend with. There is still likely to be haze and smoke layers over 

the area though. Overall, much more in the way of sunshine is 

expected for the forecast area heading into Thursday afternoon. It 

will be mainly dry, but cannot rule out a stray late afternoon 

shower or t'storm over the Catskills and Mohawk Valley regions. With 

more sunshine, it will be a little warmer with highs 78-88 expected.



According to the latest 12z HRRR smoke model data, the smoke layer 

lifts north and eventually dissipates for Central NY and NE PA 

Thursday night. Otherwise, there will be clear skies, light winds 

and patchy fog over the Catskills and Pocono valleys. Cooler lows in 

the mid-50s to lower 60s expected (except mid-60s Syracuse metro and 

northern Finger Lakes region).



&&



.SHORT TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH SATURDAY NIGHT/...

130 PM Update



Key Message: Prolonged period of dry and seasonably warm weather 

will continue through this short term timeframe. Smoke and haze look 

to disperse and fade, at least somewhat.



Southerly flow continues on Friday on the western periphery of an 

Atlantic High. Overall it is looking dry, mostly sunny and warm with 

highs in the upper 70s to mid-80s for most locations. There could 

still be some light haze or smoke aloft of the area, but the HRRR 

vertically integrated smoke product is showing lower concentrations. 

Upper level heights rise heading into Saturday with more dry and 

sunny weather expected. Warming trend starts with highs reaching 

well into the 80s over the region. Dew points (55-62F) and humidity 

levels (35-50% in the afternoon) remain low. Surface high settles 

over the area Saturday night bring clear skies and mild overnight 

temperatures in the upper 50s to upper 60s.



&&



.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

130 PM Update



Key Message: Hot weather is expected to return for much of this long 

term period...at least Sunday through Tuesday. Confidence is now 

moderate to high in this occurring. Daytime highs in the upper 80s 

to lower 90s are forecast. It does look to be a dry heat initially, 

with dew points and humidity slowly rising by Tuesday/Wednesday.



A strong 500mb ridge builds over the region Sunday into Monday, 

before slowly breaking down by Tuesday and Wednesday next week. 

850mb temperatures look to reach +19.5C Sunday afternoon and +20.5C 

Monday and Tuesday afternoons. This supports likely high 

temperatures in the upper 80s to lower 90s areawide. Humidity only 

slowly creeps up, with dew points 60-65F Sunday/Monday, and perhaps 

mid-60s by Tuesday afternoon. So although it is certainly looking 

hot, it will not be overly humid...and therefore, at this time, it 

appears heat indices may be held in check. Dry weather persists 

Sunday and Monday, with a low chance for a shower or storm entering 

into the forecast by late Tuesday. Height falls begin next Wednesday 

as the upper level trough approaches from the central Great 

Lakes...this could also bring a surface front through the area with 

increasing chances for showers and t'storms. Details and timing 

remain uncertain this far out in time. Temperatures start to 

gradually come back down by the middle of next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Mostly VFR haze should continue through most of the TAF period 

with some MVFR visibility restrictions possible at ITH/ELM. Haze

should start to clearing out later in the day tomorrow with

winds turning southerly.  



Outlook...



Thursday night through Monday...Mainly VFR besides some early 

morning valley fog possible at KELM.



&&



.BGM WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

PA...Air Quality Alert until midnight EDT tonight for PAZ038>040-

     043-044-047-048-072.

NY...Air Quality Alert until midnight EDT tonight for NYZ009-

     015>018-022>025-036-037-044>046-055>057-062.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...MJM

NEAR TERM...MJM

SHORT TERM...MJM

LONG TERM...MJM

AVIATION...MPK



