095

FXAK67 PAJK 062304

AFDAJK



Southeast Alaska Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Juneau AK

304 PM AKDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SHORT TERM...

As of Tuesday afternoon, the remnants of Tropical Storm Krosa 

merged with a broad closed low over the Bering Sea. While these 

systems dance in the west, clearing skies from a building ridge is

present over the Panhandle. Warm and dry will be the theme for 

the next 24 hours or so before an occluding front moves into the 

Gulf. Mariners planning the Cross Sound/PWS transit should be 

aware of gale force easterly winds forming Thursday afternoon into

Friday morning, with strong breezes from Cape Fairweather towards

Cape Edgecumbe. 11 to 14ft southeast seas with a period less than

10 seconds will form from this fetch, highest significant heights

west of Cross Sound towards Cape St. Elias. Winds relax Friday 

before another system moves into the coast; more details in the 

long range below.





.LONG TERM...

Key messages: 

- A low pressure system makes its way toward the panhandle

  Saturday through the weekend.

- Expect times of moderate to heavy rain during the weekend,

  mainly over the central and southern panhandle. 

- Winds will increase along the coast and inner channels as the low 

  moves into the panhandle. 



Details: To start the weekend, on Friday, a front will continue to 

bring light to moderate precipitation across the panhandle. This 

rain will continue into Saturday before a stronger low makes its way 

across the gulf. The stronger low will reach the panhandle Saturday 

bringing moderate to heavy rain for many areas, but mainly focused 

on central and southern areas. Over a 24 hour time span, there is a 

60% chance of the central to southern panhandle receiving 1.5 inches 

of rain. Some locations could see up to 2 to 2.5 inches. Currently, 

IVT values are reaching greater than 750 kg/m*s for over 48 hours. 

This event will be more about the duration of the precipitation 

rather than the amount. Confidence has slightly increased on the 

area of greatest precipitation due to guidance showing ensembles 

slowly coming into agreement. The limiting factor continues to be 

the lack of forcing that help to bring the heaviest precipitation. 



As the low reaches the gulf, winds will increase along the coast and 

inner channels. The strongest winds along the gulf will occur 

Saturday late morning to afternoon. Southeast winds will build to 

strong breezes of 25 to 30 kts on Saturday. For the inner channels, 

winds are expected to reach moderate to strong breezes up to 25 kts 

Saturday afternoon into evening as the low pushes into the panhandle 

increasing the pressure gradient.



&&



.AVIATION...

Main aviation concern continue to be MVFR CIGs this afternoon for

Yakutat and redevelopment of FG and low CIGs Wednesday night into

Thursday morning.



Widespread predominate VFR flight conditions across the panhandle

this morning as an upper level system shifts eastward out of the 

panhandle. Yakutat has seen some slight improvement in CIGs from a

thermal gradient picking up winds in the surround areas. Looking

to see Yakutat break out slightly with a SCT layer at around 1500

ft in the coming hours with moderate confidence. Not expecting

this VFR trend to last long as the marine layer looks to make a

resurgence shortly after. Winds should remain around 10kts or 

less through the afternoon, going near calm and variable Wednesday

night into Thursday morning. Strongest winds expected at Skagway 

through the afternoon up to 15kts. Increasing forecast confidence 

of patchy dense FG and low CIG development by 12z Thursday under 

clear skies, saturated marine layer, calm winds and ridging moving

in aloft.



&&



.MARINE...

Outside: Current sea state is dominated by southerly swell 3ft at

15 to 18 seconds originating from the south pacific and a west- 

northwest wind wave 5ft at 6 to 7 seconds. Expect northwest wind 

waves to diminish as the moderate breezes along the Panhandle

coast decreases tonight. Thursday anticipate easterly gale force 

winds along the northern coast, with southeasterly strong breezes 

for elsewhere. Sea state will be dominated by southeasterly fresh 

seas of 10 to 14 ft, period less than 10 seconds, with highest 

seas west of Cross Sound towards Cape Suckling/St. Elias. 



Inside: 

Beautiful Wednesday to throw some gear in the water, this

meteorologist wishes he was stalking large schools of Coho with a

speargun 40ft deep in kelp forests along Pt. Retreat. Winds will 

follow sea breeze regime Wednesday afternoon into the evening, 

with fresh breezes (17 to 21 knots) anticipated for northern Lynn 

Canal and Pt. Couverden. Expect some fog/low-clouds along the 

coast Thursday morning.



As an occluding front pushes into the Gulf Thursday, southerly 

winds will build and become more widespread across the north/south

facing inner channels into Friday, current forecast confidence 

reflects moderate to fresh breezes (13 to 21 knots) with isolated 

spots seeing strong breezes (22 to 27 knots).



&&



.AJK WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

PUBLIC...None.

MARINE...Gale Warning for PKZ652-672. 

     Small Craft Advisory for PKZ642>644-651-661>664-671. 



&&



$$



