[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.7660","issue":"2025-08-06T23:07:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KILN","awipsid":"AFDILN"}]
=================================================



136

FXUS61 KILN 062307

AFDILN



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Wilmington OH

707 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

A relatively quiet pattern remains in store through the end of the

working week and into the weekend with isolated afternoon

thunderstorms possible each day. Temperature and humidity values 

both slowly increase over the next several days.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

The weak shortwave that has been providing the subtle lift needed for

our afternoon thunderstorms moves off to the east Wednesday evening.

ILN will remain situated in between two broad areas of ridging and

quiet conditions continue overnight. 



Some CAMs suggest patchy fog development, particularly along and west

of I-75 where afternoon showers dominated. Have added this signal to

the grids during the late overnight/early morning hours for these

areas. Not currently anticipating much fog development in central

OH due to a broad area of surface high pressure over New England

pushing easterly flow and subsidence driven dry air into the Ohio 

Valley from the Appalachians. 



Overnight lows fall to the mid/upper 60s.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT/...

CONUS continues to be dominated by two broad areas of ridging: one

anchored in the southwest near the Four Corners region and the other

over New England. The ILN CWA (and most of the Midwest) remains

sandwiched between these two features under relatively weak large

scale flow. As such, there is relatively little forcing to drive any

sensible weather changes and the forecast remains mostly quiet. 

Cannot rule out an isolated pop up summertime thunderstorm, but most 

areas will remain dry on Thursday. We'll begin to notice a subtle 

uptick in temperatures/moisture and the daytime highs will rise to 

the mid/upper 80s. 



Thursday night remains similarly quiet, though with warmer overnight

lows falling to the mid/upper 60s.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Similar to midweek, a persistent inactive stretch is expected to 

continue through the end of the week and into the weekend as the 

region remains stuck in near normal PWATs and lackluster forcing. 

The ridge intensifies a bit through the weekend, supporting high 

temperatures into the upper 80s and lower 90s, but low-level 

moisture values are not high enough to anticipate heat advisory 

criteria. Still can't rule out a few pop-up showers and 

thunderstorms during the afternoon hours as sufficient moisture and 

some instability are in place. This activity would decrease into the 

evening hours with dry overnights.



Monday through Wednesday are expected to be a bit more active with a 

trough descending into the upper Midwest and Great Lakes regions. 

Increasing moisture ahead of the cold front supports growing 

rainfall chances Monday, but especially Tuesday and Wednesday as the 

front moves closer to the local area. There are some differences 

across the ensembles regarding how far south the front moves through 

midweek, but there is at least some potential for a brief dry period 

(Thursday & Friday) following the active pattern.



&&



.AVIATION /23Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Outside of a patchy fog signal near the Ohio River, VFR conditions

are likely to continue through the TAF period with light east to

southeast winds. Latest HRRR and also the 12Z HREF offer spotty 

showers Thursday afternoon with much less coverage than the past 

couple of days. Therefore, have opted to keep this very low potential

out of the TAFs for now.



OUTLOOK...No significant weather expected.



&&



.ILN WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

OH...None.

KY...None.

IN...None.



&&



$$



