...New Aviation, Short Term... 



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A warming trend will continue through the rest of the week,

  increasing the risk for heat-related illnesses, especially

  Thursday and Friday.



&&



.SHORT TERM... /NEW/

Update:



Temperatures will gradually fall into the 70s tonight, followed 

by a hot Thursday with highs ranging from 95 to 103. Maximum heat 

index values will top 105 across much of North Texas Thursday

afternoon, and a Heat Advisory remains in effect for areas 

generally near and north of the I-20 corridor. Otherwise, another 

orange, hazy sunset will be upon us in about an hour as smoke from

Canadian wildfires lingers overhead. 



30



Previous Discussion:

/Through Thursday/



An upper level ridge centered over New Mexico will continue to 

strengthen today and Thursday, resulting in a warming trend across

North and Central Texas along with no chances for rain. High

temperatures will peak in the mid to upper 90s today, with

isolated 100+ degree temperatures in western North Texas.

Afternoon highs in the mid to upper 90s are expected Thursday

afternoon, with triple digit heat becoming slightly more

widespread across North Texas. While we should see dew points mix

out a bit each afternoon, it won't be enough to keep us below 

Heat Advisory criteria tomorrow. Therefore, a Heat Advisory has 

been issued for most North Texas as well as Navarro and Henderson

counties Thursday afternoon, with heat index values expected to 

be as high as 105-109 degrees across the advisory area. Heat index

values will be between 100-104 degrees in Central Texas.



Barnes



&&



.LONG TERM... /Issued 1238 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025/

/Thursday Night Onward/



The heat will continue to intensify on Friday, with widespread 

triple digit temperatures in North Texas and highs in the mid to 

upper 90s in Central Texas. Thursday's Heat Advisory will likely

continue into Friday, as heat index values will climb above 105 

degrees once again. It's possible the advisory will also need to 

be expanded to include more of Central Texas, but these decisions

will be made either tonight or tomorrow. 



Hot weather is expected over the upcoming weekend, but

temperatures will actually begin trending downward as an inverted

upper level trough moves westward from the Mississippi River 

Valley. This will help weaken upper level ridging and subsidence

across North and Central Texas, which will shave at least a few

degrees off of high temperatures. The NBM continues to advertise 

the return of daily low rain chances across our far southeastern 

counties Sunday through the middle of next week. The rest of the 

region has a higher probability of remaining rain-free through 

the period. However, quite a few ensemble members are generating 

at least some low QPF towards the middle of next week. Therefore, 

there is at least a low potential for some rain chances to return 

to the rest of the region during this time period, but this will 

largely depend on how our upper level pattern evolves over the 

weekend and early next week.



Barnes



&&



.AVIATION... /NEW/

/00Z TAFs/



It looks like another round of one-line TAFs is in order this

evening as VFR continues along with south winds around 10 kt.



30



&&



.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...

Spotter activation is not expected at this time.



&&



