[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.7684","issue":"2025-08-06T23:09:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KMFL","awipsid":"AFDMFL"}]
=================================================



160

FXUS62 KMFL 062309

AFDMFL



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Miami FL

709 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.UPDATE...

Issued at 306 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025

 

With a northeast wind flow, the sea breeze pushing inland has

generated showers and thunderstorms producing rain accumulations

and gusty winds across the interior areas of South Florida. From

these, outflow boundaries can be seen on radar and their collision

could generate new storms through the afternoon. As the afternoon

progresses, the western counties will see the development of new

storms as these move to the southwest.



Similar scenario is expected for South Florida on Thursday due to

the weak pressure pattern over the area. Showers could arrive by 

mid-morning or earlier to the eastern coastal areas and move

inland. Coverage of afternoon storms will be greater with a

progression of development from northeast to southwest.

Temperature wise, highs will be a few degrees cooler with heat

indexes staying generally below 105F. So probabilities of needing

a heat advisory are low. No major changes were made to the

forecast.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Today through Thursday)

Issued at 320 AM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Not a lot of changes expected for the short term overall synoptic

scenario. Consensus among models remains fairly good, keeping a 

decaying front lingering around the Florida panhandle area, 

drifting a little southward at times. At the upper levels, a weak 

ridge across central Florida will gradually erode as an area of 

disturbed weather over the west Atlantic approaches the SE CONUS. 

The ridge axis begins migrating westward and into the central Gulf

region, but still remaining the dominant feature of SoFlo's 

synoptic scenario. 



Pressure gradients across SoFlo remain very weak, with possible 

periods of light and variable winds even during the afternoon hours. 

Therefore, expect sea breezes to again dominate convection 

development, and combining with moisture advection to generate 

scattered to numerous thunderstorms each day. Highest POPs will 

again reside over interior and west areas of SoFlo. NBM and 

ensembles have pushed POPs into the 80-90 percent range this 

afternoon for those areas, and into the 50-60 percent range for the 

Atlantic metro areas. 00Z MFL and model soundings keep PWATs around 

2 inches each day as moisture advection from the Atlantic continues. 



The prevailing weak synoptic flow will allow for the east coast sea 

breeze to again dominate this afternoon, placing the deepest 

convection over interior-western areas. However, a few late morning 

showers/storms are possible around the Atlantic coast, then pushing 

inland with the sea breeze. Main hazards will be frequent lightning 

strikes, damaging wind gusts, and increased potential for large 

hail. 



High temperatures will hit the low-mid 90s, and some interior-

western areas may be in the upper 90s. Heat index values will likely 

reach the 105-110 range this afternoon, especially over western 

SoFlo. Therefore, a heat advisory will be in effect today from 11am 

to 7pm. Nighttime lows will remain in the mid-upper 70s inland, and 

in the low 80s closer to the coasts.  



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Thursday night through Tuesday)

Issued at 320 AM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The weather pattern for the weekend will continue to be dominated by 

the diurnal heating cycle, with sea breezes becoming the dominant 

mechanism for lifting and deep convection. Meanwhile, model 

ensembles and long range globals begin to gradually lift the frontal 

boundary over northern Florida further north and away from the area. 

At the same time, two areas of low pressure seem to develop, one off 

the Georgia coast, and a another over the east Gulf waters. The 

moisture field associated with these features will further enhance 

showers and thunderstorms over SoFlo through early next week. The 

bulk of convective activity is expected during the afternoon hours 

and into the early evening hours each day. 



POPs/Wx coverage should will steadily increase with 60-80 percent 

chances each afternoon, even up to 90 percent possible in some 

locations. Model PWATs of 2 inches or higher may result in localized 

heavy showers, especially with slow-moving thunderstorms. Therefore, 

the unsettled weather pattern will prevail through much of the long 

term. Main hazards will again be frequent lightning strikes, 

damaging wind gusts, and increased potential for large hail. 



Afternoon high temperatures remain in the low 90s near the coasts, 

and mid-upper 90s for inland and western areas. Nighttime lows 

should remain in the mid 70s inland to around 80 near the coasts.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 650 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Collision of boundaries could generate additional storms through

07/01z for some of the eastern terminals and VCSH for APF for a

few hours this evening. Otherwise, VFR conditions for tonight. 

Short term models indicate early showers for the eastern terminals

and causing visibility reductions, lower ceilings and gusty winds

through the afternoon. APF could see these after 07/17z.



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 320 AM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Generally easterly winds today in the 5-10 mph range today across

the coastal waters. Only exception will be with Gulf breezes 

bringing a shift to west-southwesterly flow winds over the west 

coast each afternoon. Seas should remain around 1-2 ft, except 

around thunderstorms, which could produce brief periods of rough 

seas and gusty winds.



&&



.BEACHES...

Issued at 320 AM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Rip current risk will remain moderate across the West Palm Beach 

coastal waters for the next few days.



