=================================================



257

FXUS63 KDTX 062310

AFDDTX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Detroit/Pontiac MI

710 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Daily chances for isolated to scattered thunderstorms through the 

  end of the work week.  



- Humidity increases into the weekend with heat index values in the 

  low to mid 90s Saturday and Sunday.



&&



.AVIATION...



Late afternoon showers and thunderstorms are quickly exhausting 

instability that remains during early evening, and now mainly west 

of the terminal corridor. That leaves mid and high level convective 

debris to linger for VFR conditions into the late night hours. Light 

surface gradient wind will have difficulty holding up against the 

stabilizing effect of radiational cooling, even under lingering 

patches of mid and high clouds, so the inclusion of MVFR fog is 

maintained in the forecast through early morning. Boundary layer 

moisture available for fog gives way to cumulus development Thursday 

afternoon and another round of showers and thunderstorms in or near 

the terminal corridor.  



For DTW/D21 Convection... As evening convection fades, the best 

chance for any new thunderstorms now holds off until Thursday 

afternoon.  



THRESHOLD PROBABILITIES...



* Low for thunderstorms Thursday afternoon.



&&



.PREV DISCUSSION...

Issued at 335 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



DISCUSSION...



Weak trough tracks trough the area with mid level vorticity 

advection from the west today. South the southeasterly winds has 

lead to a plume of higher 850-700mb theta-e with contributions from 

Lake Erie and better dewpoints into the 60s drawn in from the south. 

Diurnal heating is yielding mean MUCAPE of 500-1000 J/kg or greater 

with the RAP showing the best CAPE density focused generally along 

and west of the I-75 corridor where best convergence along inland 

advancing lake boundaries and subsequent outflow boundaries have been

enough to trigger isolated to scattered showers and isolated 

thunderstorms with the available instability. Lack of shear should 

keep convection disorganized and more pulse type, though any stronger

storm that is able to develop will be capable of strong downburst 

winds of 40-50 mph through the rest of the afternoon. Locally heavy 

rain may accompany these thunderstorms as well given the slow N-NW to

occasionally stationary storm motion with up to 1 inch or greater 

possible in localized areas in a short period of time. Activity 

expected to wane with the loss of daytime heating towards the 

evening, likely ending by 8-9 pm.



South to southwest flow in place into tomorrow will usher in even 

better theta-e in the 850-700 mb layer offering yet another chance 

at scattered showers and thunderstorms along any surface convergence 

zones. Slightly better mid-level lapse rates begin to creep into 

central Michigan in the afternoon while both instability and shear 

improve across southeast Michigan. The 0-6km bulk shear values do 

improve to 20-30 knots with MLCAPE values climb to around 1500 J/kg 

per RAP for tomorrow afternoon. This leads to a slightly better 

thunderstorm environment for tomorrow afternoon with gusty downburst 

winds and locally heavy rain as the main concerns. Thunderstorm 

chances in the forecast again for Friday as remnants from a decaying 

MCS that develop late Thursday in Wisconsin move into Michigan 

providing a focal point for lift within an already moist and 

unstable environment.



Aside from the daily thunderstorm chances through the remainder of 

the work week, temperature and humidity trends will be the other 

notable forecast item. Temperatures through the work week are 

forecast to top out around the mid to upper 80s, though dewpoints 

will be on the climb with 70 degrees achievable by Friday. Amplifying

upper ridge will draw in even warmer air for the weekend with 850 mb

temperatures forecast to reach around 20C at 850 mb. This will aide 

in daily high temperatures both Saturday and Sunday in the upper 80s 

to low 90s. Dewpoint levels hold around 70 degrees during this time 

and will result in heat indices in the low-mid 90s. The presence of 

the ridge will limit the chance for precipitation over the weekend. 

However, cannot completely rule out the potential for at least some 

isolated shower/thunderstorm potential given the amount of moisture 

and daily instability. Low confidence is these chances this far out 

will preclude any mention at this time.



MARINE...



Influence of high pressure continues to wane this evening-tonight as 

it drifts towards the Canadian maritimes. More uniform southeasterly 

flow develops by late evening and gradually turns to more southerly 

over the course of Thursday. Only a modest increase in strength 

occurs over that window with peak gusts 15-20kts over north-central 

Lake Huron by Thursday afternoon. Rain chances both this evening and 

daytime Thursday are generally confined towards the nearshore waters 

with the bulk of shower/storm activity occuring overland. Better 

chances to see scattered thunderstorms over the open waters of Lake 

Huron comes late Thursday into Friday as a weak warm front crosses 

the upper Great Lakes.



&&



.DTX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MI...None.

Lake Huron...None.

Lake St Clair...None.

Michigan waters of Lake Erie...None.

&&



$$



AVIATION.....BT

DISCUSSION...AA

MARINE.......KDK





You can obtain your latest National Weather Service forecasts online

at www.weather.gov/detroit.



