=================================================



284

FXUS63 KSGF 062312

AFDSGF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Springfield MO

612 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A dying complex of thunderstorms diving south from KCMO will

  push an outflow boundary through our area this afternoon.

  Expect the next couple of hours to be breezy with southerly 

  winds gusting up to 30 mph as this complex continues to 

  diminish. 



- Hot and humid conditions build into the area today and linger

  into the weekend. Afternoon temperatures in the lower to

  middle 90s, with heat index values approaching 100 degrees.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH TONIGHT/...

Issued at 231 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A dying MCV has pushed its way south into the area this

afternoon and has brought a few showers and thunderstorms. This

activity wasn't captured well by any of the models but has yet

persisted longer than anticipated. The outflow from this complex

has caused gusty winds to occur over in eastern KS/western MO. 

Most observations show southerly wind gusts up to 30 mph. Once 

the gust front pushes through, expect gusty winds to hang around

for the next couple of hours and die down by sunset. It's 

uncertain how far south the rain will go. Right now, have the 

highest chances for rain north of I-44. There is a bit of dry 

air over the MO/AR border that may cause the showers to 

dissipate as the complex continues to move away from the 

favorable environment. Again, the main hazards with the MCV 

this afternoon is lighting, wind gusts up to 30 mph, and brief

heavy rainfall. 



Due to the cloud cover from this MCV, have lowered the daytime

highs by a couple of degrees. It'll still be warm, but expect

higher clouds to filter into southern MO this afternoon. Even

with the cloud cover, highs will still reach the lower 90s

today. Skies will start to clear out by tonight and highs will

be in the upper 60s to lower 70s.



&&



.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 231 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Another shortwave will move over the ridge and through 

northern/eastern MO on Thursday bringing a chance (10-15%) for 

showers and thunderstorms to areas east of Highway 63 and north 

of I-44. No severe weather is expected with that activity that 

may occur tomorrow afternoon.



Thursday-Saturday: Hot and humid conditions look to persist in 

the later part of the week and upcoming weekend, with daily 

highs approaching middle to upper 90s by Friday. The highest 

temperatures look to remain focused in the same area, along and 

west of Highway 65. This pattern will feature minimal rain 

chances through the weekend as the ridge remains stagnant. With

heat index values reaching or exceeding 100 for portions of the

area, heat headlines may be warranted in future forecasts. 

Confidence is increasing that Friday and Saturday will be the 

hottest days of the week. Forecast high temperatures are around 

5 degrees above normal for early August.



Sunday-Early Next Week: By Sunday into early next week, 

ensemble guidance diverges a bit on solutions, though the 

general trend supports a bit "cooler" temperatures with a slight

breakdown in the ridge over the central CONUS. Though, these

daytime highs would around 90 degrees which is average for this

time of year. POPs will be at 15-20% for central MO on Sunday

with no severe weather expected at this time.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 607 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions will prevail through the entire TAF period.

Surface winds continue to diminish at KSGF and KJLN, and will 

continue to do so during the remainder of this evening. 

Southerly winds will then become gusty again after 15Z primarily

for KJLN. Confidence in gusts occurring at KSGF/KBBG was too 

low to include in the TAFs, however cannot rule out future

additions if guidance comes into more agreement.



&&



.SGF WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

KS...None.

MO...None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...Soria

LONG TERM...Soria

AVIATION...Melto



