[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8019","issue":"2025-08-06T23:13:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KPAH","awipsid":"AFDPAH"}]
=================================================



498

FXUS63 KPAH 062313

AFDPAH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Paducah KY

613 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Light to calm winds and clear skies may result in patchy fog

  again tonight.  



- Dry and seasonable conditions will continue on through the 

  weekend.



- Isolated showers and thunderstorms are possible Monday, with

  better chances late Monday night through Wednesday.  



- Temperatures and humidity will increase a little for the first

  half of next week with south to southwest winds.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 613 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Updated Aviation discussion for the 00z TAF issuance. 



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 201 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A few very small light showers/sprinkles are popping up this

afternoon. They are very brief and will fizzle out between

22z-00z with loss of heating.



Our region will remain sandwiched between a weak upper level trof to 

our east and a stronger upper level high over the southwest U.S. 

through the end of the week. The upper trof will push off the 

coast this weekend, allowing upper level ridging to build over 

the lower/middle Mississippi and Ohio valleys into the Great 

Lakes region. These features will keep the weather across our 

region fairly benign tonight through Sunday. With light to calm

winds and clear skies again tonight, we may see some fog 

develop in the 2am-8am time frame, and patchy dense fog is 

possible. Temperatures will remain seasonable along with pretty

typical humidity levels for early August. 



By early next week, models show a surface low pushing across 

southern Canada, with a frontal boundary extending southwest through 

the Great Lakes region into the Central Plains by 12z Monday. Models 

show the frontal boundary potentially moving into the middle 

Mississippi and lower Ohio valleys by Tuesday.  Along and ahead of 

the front will be chances of scattered showers and thunderstorms. 

Based on the current timing, this would be late Monday night through 

Wednesday for the PAH forecast area, with just isolated chances on 

Monday. Precipitation timing overall remains in question based on 

just how far south the front can progress, competing with the high 

pressure already in place across our region.  Increased southerly 

flow ahead of the front will push both temperatures and dew points 

up a couple of degrees.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 613 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



High pressure centered over the region will bring calm or light

and variable winds throughout the forecast period. Predawn fog

development is expected again, with periods of MVFR vsbys

forecast at all terminals in the 09-13z window. Skies will be 

generally clear overnight, with diurnal CU development expected 

to begin around 13-15z, with bases around 5000ft. 



&&



.PAH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

IL...None.

MO...None.

IN...None.

KY...None.



&&



$$



UPDATE...DWS

DISCUSSION...RST

AVIATION...DWS



