[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8025","issue":"2025-08-06T23:14:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KTAE","awipsid":"AFDTAE"}]
=================================================



504

FXUS62 KTAE 062314

AFDTAE



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Tallahassee FL

714 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.NEAR TERM...

(Through Thursday)

Issued at 235 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Flood watch remains in effect through 8 PM ET for portions of

southeast Alabama and southwest Georgia. Flash flood guidance is

fairly low and with ongoing showers and storms through early

evening, these areas are susceptible to flooding concerns. 



A fairly active afternoon of showers and storms will gradually 

decrease into the evening hours. A weak frontal boundary will 

continue to meander around the Gulf coast or just offshore into 

Thursday. The axis of best low/mid level moisture shifts further 

south as a mid level trough also sinks south into the northern Gulf. 

CAMs are hinting of a few showers or storms developing overnight 

along I75 in the vicinity of the front then shifting south and east 

towards daybreak. 



Rain chances Thursday will follow a more typical summer regime with 

the best chances in the seabreeze favored zones of the Florida 

counties and adjacent southern Georgia. Drier air will filter into 

the northern and northwestern zones, which is welcomed with the 

recent heavy rainfall of the past few days. Chances in these areas 

will range from 20-40%. 



Highs Thursday will mainly be in the upper 80s with lower 90s south 

of I10.



&&



.SHORT TERM AND LONG TERM...

(Thursday night through Tuesday)

Issued at 235 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Mid level trough gradually shifts south and west while the weak 

boundary eventually moves south and washes out this weekend. This 

will allow a tropical wave to transition across the Florida 

Peninsula Friday and into the eastern Gulf Saturday. Rain chances 

Friday will virtually be a repeat of Thursday. This weekend, with 

the added moisture from the tropical wave, rain chances plus back up 

to 60-80% across the CWA. On the heels of the tropical wave moving 

west into the western Gulf, the Atlantic ridge builds in from the 

east. Monday will be a transition day to gradually lower pops from 

high rain chances over the weekend as low/mid level dry air is 

impinging into the southeast US. Beyond Monday, rain chances start 

to decrease to a more typical footprint favoring south of I10 and 

along I75. With the reduction in rain chances, the heat comes back 

with highs progged for the mid to upper 90s and heat indices pushing 

back into the advisory levels of 108F+.



&&





.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 713 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Isolated showers/thunderstorms may occur during the evening and 

overnight hours. However, VFR conditions should prevail through 

08Z/09Z. A deck of low stratus clouds extending from the east will

bring our ceilings down to MVFR/IFR affecting all terminals 

except ECP. Ceilings are expected to return to VFR by around 16Z. 

Another round of showers and thunderstorms is expected tomorrow 

afternoon and evening. Storms will be moving inland from the coast

through the day, affecting TLH, ECP, and VLD terminals with 

MVFR/IFR conditions in heavier storms.



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 235 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Surface winds will clock around to the northeast Thursday as a 

meandering front oozes southward. In the meantime, a tropical wave

will enter the northern Gulf this weekend. Scattered showers and

thunderstorms are expected each day favoring the overnight into

morning periods. Heading into next week, high pressure builds

through the southeast US with winds becoming east to southeast and

an overall decreasing trend to the rain chances. Accompanying

heavier storms, brief higher winds and seas can be expected.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 235 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A return to a more typical summer pattern is expected heading 

into late week. Scattered late morning to mid evening showers and 

storms is anticipated, favoring the seabreeze zones of the Florida

counties and adjacent southern Georgia counties. Rainfall chances

will decrease into southeast Alabama and the Flint River area of 

southwest Georgia. Low dispersions will occur around the coastal 

sections of the Florida Big Bend while favorable dispersions 

elsewhere. Erratic and gusty winds will accompany thunderstorms 

through the end of the forecast period.



&&



.HYDROLOGY...

Issued at 235 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Flood watch continues through 8 PM ET tonight and should be

allowed to expire. A transition to typical summer time regime is

forecast Thursday and Friday with slightly drier air and lower

rain chances in the flood watch area. Better rain chances will shift

south into Florida and adjacent southern Georgia. Rain chances 

increase this weekend as a tropical wave adds additional moisture 

with rain chances bumping back up. WPC keeps a marginal risk 

(level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall going into our Florida 

Panhandle, Big Bend, and adjacent southern Georgia into the 

weekend. Next week, high pressure builds in with an overall 

decreasing trend to rainfall chances. 



Despite the lowered rain chances the next few days across AL/GA

where recent heavy rains have occurred, flash flood guidance is

low so any heavy rains in those areas could lead to quick 

localized flooding.



Concerning rivers, action stage is expected or occurring on the 

Saint Marks, Sopchoppy, Kinchafoonee, Flint, and Spring Creek.

None are expected to reach flood stage at this time.



&&



.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...



Spotter activation is not requested. However, spotters are always 

encouraged to safely report significant weather conditions when they 

occur by calling the office or tweeting us @NWSTallahassee.



&&



