[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8074","issue":"2025-08-06T23:18:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KGJT","awipsid":"AFDGJT"}]
=================================================



553

FXUS65 KGJT 062318

AFDGJT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Grand Junction CO

518 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Widespread critical fire weather conditions will continue

  through the remainder of the week with Red Flag Warnings in 

  place.



- High temperatures will remain above normal.



- Winds are expected to diminish some Saturday, but critical 

  fire weather conditions are expected to linger.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 355 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A couple of brief shots of mid level moisture have sparked a few 

showers and thunderstorms across southwest Colorado this afternoon. 

A more monsoonal plum is responsible for the activity across the 

southern CWA, while the persistent Pacific plume streaming through 

the past couple of days is producing some cloud cover along the I-70 

corridor. All of this activity is beneath the persistent boundary 

located along the perimeter of the resident high. Winds within the 

mixed layer have been strong enough to keep critical fire weather 

conditions around the region this afternoon. Low pressure working 

ashore in the PACNW will knock down isoheights tonight and Thursday, 

while also packing the pressure gradient more. This will bring 

surface winds up substantially Thursday afternoon, leading to 

critical fire weather conditions across most of the CWA. Red Flag 

Warnings are in place for Thursday and Friday, as this regime 

continues.



Tonight, shower activity will taper around dark, but a few nocturnal 

showers across the San Juans are not out of the question. Thursday 

will return hot and dry, with area temperatures in excess of 5 

degrees above normal. Leftover moisture could produce an isolated 

thunderstorm or shower across our eastern San Juans, with the main 

threat just gusty outflow winds and/or dry lightning in the 

continued hot and dry conditions. The overnight lows will be a 

couple degrees warmer thanks to the higher dewpoints working through 

on the weak moisture.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 355 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



On Friday a low pressure system will track eastward along the 

US/Canada border with the trough extending southward over Wyoming. 

The jet associated with this low will pass over our area, which 

looks to increase the winds overall. Wind gusts of 25-40 mph will be 

common in the afternoon. This coupled with a lack of moisture keeps 

the critical fire weather conditions going. The pressure gradient 

relaxes somewhat on Saturday, but winds are still projected to reach 

red flag criteria for portions of the area. Limited moisture may 

linger across the southern CWA on Sat and Sun, but confidence is not 

high. Northwest flow becomes well established by Monday, so any 

moisture around will likely leave the area. By mid week the high 

builds back in overhead and gradually slides eastward. Moisture 

pooling in Arizona and New Mexico could circulate back northward and 

into the area on the west side of the high. Until then warm, dry and 

sometimes windy looks to be the main story.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 516 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A few light rain showers will continue to move over the higher

terrain of the southern and central Colorado Divide, but will

taper off over the next 3 or so hours. Skies will clear

overnight, with the gusty southwesterly winds diminishing and

becoming light and terrain driven. These southwesterly winds

will pick up again tomorrow afternoon, with gusts of 20-30 

knots expected. Smoke from regional wildfires will continue to 

produce reduced visibility at certain airports, with KHDN seeing

the greatest impacts.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 355 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Hot, dry, windy conditions will persist. Wetting rains are not 

expected. A passing disturbance is producing some dry lightning 

and gusty outflow winds across southwest Colorado this afternoon. 

Winds increase Thursday and Friday, continuing widespread critical 

fire weather conditions for the majority of the region. Winds 

diminish after Saturday and likely reduce critical fire weather 

conditions.



&&



.GJT WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...



CO...Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for COZ200>203-

     290.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Thursday for 

     COZ200>203-205-207-290-292>294.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Friday for COZ200>203-

     205-207-290-292>294.

UT...Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for UTZ485>487-

     490.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Thursday for 

     UTZ485>487-490-491.

     Red Flag Warning from 11 AM to 8 PM MDT Friday for UTZ485>487-

     490-491.



&&



$$



