FXUS65 KTFX 062320

AFDTFX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Great Falls MT

520 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Aviation Section Updated.



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A few showers and thunderstorms are expected through tonight.



- Thunderstorm activity increases during the afternoon on

  Thursday, with a few severe storms possible over the eastern

  portions of the CWA.



- It turns cooler for Friday, with temperatures below freezing in

  higher valleys of Southwest MT.



- It is looking to be mostly dry and warm for much of next week.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

/Issued 245 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025/ 



 - Meteorological Overview:

A weak upper level disturbance will move northeast through the CWA

tonight. This disturbance will produce some showers/thunderstorms

across the CWA through the night.



On Thursday, a stronger upper level trof approaches from the west,

with the potential for a few stronger storms during the late

afternoon and evening hours. The main impact from any severe

storms will be gusty winds per the HREF model, but some large 

hail can't be ruled out. The most likely timing for any severe 

storms will be from about 3 PM to 11 PM Thu.



On Friday, as the upper level trof exits the area, cooler

temperatures will move into the CWA for a short time. Afternoon

temperatures will be close to 15 degrees below normal for this

time of year. There will continue to be a chance for mostly rain

over the far northern portions of the CWA, with the heaviest

rainfall expected in Hill/Blaine counties, where rainfall could

exceed a half inch on Friday.



Drier air starts to move back into the CWA on Saturday, with

generally dry conditions then expected into the middle of next

week. Even though an isolated shower will still be possible

Sat/Sun in the Havre/Harlem areas, the probability for any precip

is quite low. Afternoon temperatures warm back up to near 80

degrees on Sunday, then continue warming into the mid 80s for 

early next week.



 - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:



The main concern is the thunderstorm potential in the short term.

The HREF model continues to prog a few storms producing wind gusts

around 50 to 60 mph later tonight and then again on Thursday. The

eastern portions of the CWA are the most favored areas.

Additionally, some brief heavy rainfall is possible with the

stronger storms, but overall, these heavier rain producing cells

should be more isolated. Brusda



&&



.AVIATION...

07/00Z TAF Period 



Broken mid-level clouds and showers develop early this evening 

and track northeast across the area overnight with some gusty 

winds accompanying showers. The risk for thunderstorms increases 

as these showers progress east across central MT during the 

overnight period with KLWT and KHVR potentially seeing a 

thunderstorm after 06z. Showers and thunderstorms exit the area 

early Thursday morning with a greater coverage of showers and 

thunderstorms likely to develop Thursday afternoon through the 

evening. Surface winds will shift to the west or northwest with 

the passage of the showers this evening/overnight. Hoenisch



-Wilson



Refer to weather.gov/zlc for more detailed regional aviation 

weather and hazard information.



&&



