=================================================



584

FXUS63 KFSD 062320

AFDFSD



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Sioux Falls SD

620 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Scattered showers with isolated thunderstorms will be 

  possible tonight mainly east of I-29. However, severe weather 

  is not expected.



- More warm and muggy conditions are ahead with heat index 

  values between 95-105 degrees likely on both Thursday and 

  Friday. While heat headlines will likely be needed, heat 

  indices will be driven by oppressive humidity rather than 

  dangerous heat. 



- Severe weather risks continue in the region but remain very

  uncertain in coverage. Greatest risks are associated with a

  passing front on Friday night and Saturday.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 250 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



CURRENTS-TONIGHT: Another warm day continues! Taking a look across 

the area, isolated showers and thunderstorms continue this afternoon 

along the I-90 corridor mainly in response to a weak perturbation and 

about 35 kts of bulk shear. While there is just enough instability 

(200-500 J/kg) for an occasional stroke of lightning with some of 

this activity, should see things gradually taper down over the next 

few hours as the mid-level perturbation weakens. From here, mostly 

quiet conditions should return from the evening onwards as 

temperatures likely decrease into the upper 60s to lower 70s for the 

night. Lastly, could see some scattered redevelopment overnight 

mainly east of I-29 as a nocturnal LLJ strengthens. While severe 

weather is not expected, can't completely rule out an isolated 

thunderstorm in northwestern IA closer to daybreak.



THURSDAY & FRIDAY: Looking into the late week, mostly quiet 

conditions will return by Thursday as a weak mid-level ridge moves 

over head keeping most of our precipitation chances at bay. With 

building mid-level warm air advection (WAA) aloft and southerly to 

southeasterly surface winds; expect conditions to continue trending 

warmer into the weekend with highs in the 80s and 90s expected 

through Friday. With this and higher dew points (upper 60s to mid 

70s) in play due to peak corn sweat season, heat indices will likely 

top out in the mid to upper 90s and low 100s across parts of the 

area each afternoon. While heat headlines will likely be needed for 

both day, decided to hold off for now as we fine tune details 

regarding humidity values since this will be the main driver of the 

headline decision this time around instead of temperature. 

Otherwise, the main concern will be the increasing precipitation 

(30%-40%) chances from Friday night onwards. 



Looking aloft, a closed upper-level low (ULL) will rotate into the 

Northern Plains by Friday with its associated cold front rotating 

through our area between Friday night and Saturday morning promoting 

better chances for organized convection. While some uncertainty 

remains with the severe weather risk, the overall environment will 

be characterized by 2000-4000 J/kg of instability and 35-45 kts of 

bulk shear. However, with the better instability offset from the 

better shear; there are still a few question on where exactly things 

initialize and when. Nonetheless, when things start to develop; 

stronger storms will be capable of large hail up to Hen egg size (2 

inch) and damaging winds up to 65 mph. With this in mind, SPC 

continues to highlight most of the area in a Day 3 Slight Risk 

(Level 2 of 5) so continue to monitor the forecast ahead. 



SATURDAY ONWARDS: Heading into the extended period, an active 

pattern continues aloft as southwesterly flow lifts multiple waves 

into the area through Monday leading to additional shower and 

thunderstorm chances. While the severe weather risk is uncertain at 

this point, machine learning guidance has started to introduce some 

low probabilities for our area between Day 4 to Day 5 (Saturday 

through Sunday) so this will likely be the next period to watch for 

any organized convection. Temperatures will likely sit around our 

seasonal normals with daily highs in the low to mid 80s.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 619 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Three layers of clouds continue over the region this evening. A

low-lvl CU field will gradually dissipate this evening, while

mid-lvl ACCAS grows and slides east towards MN/IA. Further

aloft, cirrus will remain in place into Thursday.



Tonight, we'll watch for isolated to scattered shower and

thunderstorms to develop along and east of I-29, focused mostly

in MN/IA counties. Further west, if skies clear and winds turn

light and variable, then some daybreak patchy fog may be

possible. 



&&



