=================================================



592

FXUS64 KLCH 062321

AFDLCH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Lake Charles LA

621 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Expect a return to typical summertime through the end of the 

  week. Moisture pooling in lower Acadiana may result in higher 

  rain chances in this area compared to everyone else. 



- Rain chances increase a bit over the weekend as a disturbance

  moves across the northern Gulf Coast and provides extra support

  for convection.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Tonight through Friday night)

Issued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A weak frontal boundary continues to meander near the I-10 corridor 

today, with scattered shower activity ongoing south of the boundary 

at this time. There is no significant difference in the airmass 

north/south of the front temperature/dewpoint wise, with the only 

real difference being the ongoing convection. CAMs guidance shows 

isolated convection continuing near/south of the boundary this 

afternoon, as it continues to meander north and wash out. Aloft, 

broad ridging is centered over the desert SW/west TX, with the 

eastern extent of the ridge overhead. This should help to keep 

convection in check today, with drier air overhead aloft.  



Tomorrow and Friday, upper ridging lingers to our west and begins to 

weaken as a weakness develops aloft over the northeastern Gulf. This 

will result in slightly higher rain chances across Acadiana/south LA 

through the next couple of days, with lower chances across our 

northern most counties/parishes. Otherwise, temperatures warm into 

the low to mid 90s each afternoon, while lows fall into the low to 

mid 70s overnight. 



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday through next Tuesday)

Issued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Moving into the weekend, a weakness aloft will be overhead by 

Saturday. From there it will slowly meander westward, eventually 

moving into Texas by late Sunday. At the surface, low pressure will 

be meandering off the East Coast along a stalled front. This 

boundary will be draped back towards the CWA through the weekend. 

The combination of the weakness aloft and the weak boundary will 

result in elevated rain chances across much of the northern Gulf 

Coast through the weekend. Saturday and especially Sunday, rain 

chances become elevated across Acadiana and the southern half of the 

CWA in general, with lower chances inland. 



Moving into the work week, stalled boundary continues to meander 

around the northern Gulf, gradually washing out by the mid-week. 

The weakness aloft lingers across the Southern Plains for a bit, 

before ridging begins to overtake it by the mid-week. NBM paints 

elevated POPs each afternoon, particularly across the southern half 

of the CWA. Still think this POPs are a bit high, but we will see 

how things progress through the next few days. Temperature wise, 

near normal highs/lows continue throughout the long term. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 618 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Mainly VFR conditions this evening with lower CIGs in the vicinity

of the lingering showers and storms. Carried VCTS for the rest of

the evening as a result of the scattered convection, however this

will continue to taper over the next few hours. With exception to

some areas of patchy fog, tonight should remain quiet at all the 

TAF sites. Tomorrow afternoon we will see another round of diurnal

activity, mainly across the acadiana area and coastal terminals. 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A light onshore flow with low seas will continue through end of

the week as weak high pressure nudges into the area from the 

east. A few nocturnal showers and storms will be possible each 

morning. Over the weekend a weakness aloft will move east to west

across the Gulf. Winds will increase just slightly in response

while remaining east to southeasterly, while coverage of showers 

and storms will become more scattered to widespread.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Isolated to scattered showers and storms will be most likely near

the coast and over Acadiana this afternoon, with minimal rain

chances elsewhere. With the week boundary now lifting inland,

moisture has returned, with minRH values forecasted in the 50-65

range this afternoon. A similar pattern continues through the end

of the week. Rain chances increase a bit over the weekend as a

disturbance moves overhead.



