614

FXUS64 KLUB 062322

AFDLUB



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Lubbock TX

622 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 621 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



 - Hot and dry conditions the rest of the week, with temperatures

   climbing into the triple digits.

 

 - Heat advisory level conditions possible off the Caprock Friday

   with widespread triple digit heat. 



 - Precipitation chances return this weekend with slightly cooler

   temperatures. 



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Thursday)

Issued at 1220 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Water vapor imagery this afternoon shows the center of the 

persistent 600 dam upper high spiraling over the Desert Southwest, 

with the associated upper level ridge extending into the Texas 

Panhandle. This slight increase in height values, along with 

increased thickness values over the region, will influence even 

warmer temperature today compared to yesterday with temperatures 

already 4 to 5 degrees warmer than what we saw 24 hours ago. 

Thankfully, the center of the high as I mentioned earlier remains 

parked over central New Mexico which will help temperatures stay 

below Heat Advisory criteria. Despite this, a hot one is still 

expected this afternoon with highs in the upper 90s to lower triple 

digits. Dry conditions are still expected this afternoon, however 

any moisture trapped beneath the upper ridge may interact with minor 

disturbances wrapping around the eastern side of the upper high 

which may allow for an isolated thunderstorm or two across the 

Caprock. If any storms develop, the severe threat will remain low, 

however an occasional sub-severe gust cannot be completely ruled out 

given inverted-v nature of forecast soundings. Dry and warm 

conditions are expected to continue overnight with lows in the upper 

60s to mid 70s. The upper level high will weaken ever so slightly as 

it makes a subtle shift west, this will lead to a slight decrease in 

heights Thursday afternoon. Despite this, persistent southwesterly 

flow from the surface to H7, along with similar thickness values 

will lead to daytime highs similar to what we see today in the upper 

90s to lower triple digits. One uncertainty with tomorrows 

temperature forecast is if areas off the Caprock see more widespread 

Heat Advisory level temperatures (>105 degrees), as of right now 

these conditions look to be localized with 850 mb temperatures 

around 28C to 31C suggesting highs may remain just below criteria. 

However, this cannot be ruled out entirely and heat safety should be 

practiced by limiting time outdoors if possible and drink plenty of 

water to avoid heat related illnesses. Although confidence still 

remains low at this time, there is a chance for isolated storms 

across the far southwestern Texas Panhandle Thursday evening as 

storms develop off the higher terrain in eastern NM and track into 

the area.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Thursday night through next Tuesday)

Issued at 1220 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



By Friday the upper level ridge and area of associated high pressure 

will begin to retreat back west, slightly weakening from 600 dam to 

around 596 dam. Given a more westward progression in recent runs 

compared to yesterday, which will allow height values to slowly 

decrease across the FA. Despite this, thickness values will still 

remain, which in combination with breezy southwest surface winds and 

clear skies will allow temperatures to soar into the triple digits 

area-wide Friday. Thankfully, heat advisory conditions do not look 

to be widespread, remaining confined to areas across the far 

southeastern Texas Panhandle and northern Rolling Plains, while 

elsewhere temperatures remain just shy of criteria around 100F to 

104F. Cooler conditions, back in the 90s, are then expected to begin 

as early as Saturday as the upper level ridge continues to move back 

west, as an upper level low translates through the Canadian 

Provinces over the weekend and into the NE CONUS by early next week. 

This will influence northwest flow aloft across much of the region, 

as the upper ridge flattens across western CONUS. This synoptic 

pattern in place should allow for these "cooler" temperatures to 

remain locked in place through early next week with highs forecasted 

in the upper 80s to mid 90s. Ensemble guidance suggest the return of 

precipitation to the area by the weekend as the upper high becomes 

displaced off to the west, and the base of the trough to our north 

extends into portions of the Southern Plains. An axis of monsoonal 

moisture looks to set up through the weekend, stretching across 

portions of the FA, which combined with minor perturbations tracking 

through the base of the trough, will allow for daily chances for 

showers and thunderstorms each afternoon through the weekend into 

mid-week. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 621 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions are expected through the TAF period. Gusty

southerly winds are possible mainly during daylight hours. Check

density altitude. 



&&



.LUB WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.

&&



$$



SHORT TERM...12

LONG TERM....12

AVIATION...07



