626

FXUS64 KMRX 062323

AFDMRX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Morristown TN

723 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 103 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Key Messages: 



1. Slightly below normal temperatures continue through at least 

Friday, followed by a gradual return to more normal temperatures. 



2. Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms expected through 

the forecast, mainly during the afternoon hours. 



Discussion:



We start the period with a weak upper trough over our region with 

ridging over the desert SW and in the western Atlantic. Meanwhile 

strong surface high pressure centered over the northeastern US and 

nosing down east of the Appalachians is keeping an easterly 

component to the low level flow over our area. This set up will 

continue to keep our temperatures a bit below normal through the 

remainder of the work week. While synoptic forcing will be lacking, 

some modest instability will allow for isolated to scattered 

convection especially during the afternoon hours each day. 



The trough will give way to upper ridging over our area by the 

weekend,  although the surface high over the northeast will continue 

to keep the easterly component to the low level flow over our area 

which will preclude a rapid increase to the heat.  We will see a 

slight warm up over the weekend into early next week, although just 

to near normal for this time of year.  We will continue to see 

isolated to scattered convection around each day through at least 

Monday. By the Tuesday/Wednesday time frame our coverage of showers 

and storms may increase although models do not agree on the impact 

for our area of an upper trough moving through the Great Lakes into 

the northeast US as well as how far south the surface cold front 

will make it during that period.   



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 716 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Showers and storms are becoming more isolated and should end

completely shortly after sunset. Tomorrow afternoon will bring

similar coverage of showers and storms with fairly low chances at

each terminal. MVFR CIGs and vis will be possible at all terminals

in the early morning hours but confidence is low since none of 

the terminals got rain today.



&&



