=================================================



646

FXUS64 KSJT 062324 AAA

AFDSJT



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service San Angelo TX

624 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot and dry conditions through this weekend.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Thursday)

Issued at 200 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Strong upper ridging, centered over New Mexico, will continue to 

bring hot, dry conditions. High temperatures Thursday rise a degree, 

with ranges of 99 to 103 in the Big Country and Concho Valley, and

mid and upper 90s farther south. Southeast to south winds will be

mainly in the 5 to 15 mph range, with a few higher daytime gusts 

of 20 mph in the Big Country and Concho Valley.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Thursday night through next Wednesday)

Issued at 1247 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Upper level ridge to continue to be the main weather story for

West Central Texas, largely keeping hot and dry conditions in

place through the weekend. Afternoon highs will creep up towards

the 105 degree mark across the western and northwest Big Country,

closer to the center of the upper ridge. Will need to take a good

look at possible Heat Advisory conditions starting for at least

Friday and Saturday.



Models do suggest a break down of the upper level ridge sometime

during the early/mid portions of next week. Model blends are

dropping temperatures down into the mid and upper 90s and this

seems reasonable for now. Quick peek at some of the statistical

analysis shows a high (60-80%) chance of temperatures above the 

100 degree mark across the Big Country on Friday afternoon/evening

dropping down to a low (10-20%) chance by next Wednesday so that 

shows the model trending down in temperatures nicely for next 

week.



Models aren't showing much in the way of POPs but given the 

pattern, a return of some isolated storms not out of the question.

Not going to add POPs this far out at this point, but will be 

keeping an eye on it as we head into the weekend. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 622 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions and clear skies are expected through the next 24

hours. Southeast winds are somewhat gusty this evening, but will

decrease by 02Z-03Z. Winds will veer to the south by late tonight.

During the day Thursday, south or south-southwest winds will

increase to 6-8 knots by mid-to-late morning at our southern 

terminals, and 9-11 knots at KSJT/KABI. Higher gusts to near 18 

knots are expected at times from late morning through the 

afternoon hours, mainly at KABI and KSJT. 



