=================================================



649

FXUS63 KLOT 062324

AFDLOT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Chicago/Romeoville, IL

624 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Potential (20% chance) for isolated to widely scattered

  showers and thunderstorms Thursday afternoon and night, mainly

  north of I-80.



- A gradual increase in heat and humidity will continue this

  week with peak afternoon heat indices of near to possibly a

  bit above 100F Friday-Sunday.



- A more active pattern returns by early next week with 

  increasing shower and storm chances.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 244 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Through Friday Morning:



A broad area of high pressure continues to reside over the 

eastern Great Lakes which has been maintaining our rain-free and

mild conditions locally. While there continues to be some haze 

from lingering wildfire smoke, it appears the heavier surface 

smoke concentrations have exited the area and what lingering 

smoke there is should do the same tonight into Thursday morning.



That said, we are keeping a close eye on a remnant MCV that is 

moving across southeast IA this afternoon. The earlier shower 

and thunderstorm activity associated with this MCV has largely 

dissipated aside from some spotty showers in eastern IA, but as 

the circulation moves into the humid and modestly unstable 

airmass in central IL this evening additional showers and 

thunderstorms are likely to develop. Given the current 

trajectory the thinking is that any redeveloping showers/storms 

will stay south and west of our area, however, there is a low 

chance (~15%) that a stray shower could get as far east as a 

Peru, IL to Paxton, IL line through midnight. Therefore, have 

maintained a slight chance mention of showers in the forecast 

during this period with otherwise dry conditions expected for 

tonight. 



While we enjoy a night of tranquil weather, another shortwave 

trough is expected to pivot into northern IA resulting in the 

development of another thunderstorm complex. Similar to today, 

the storm complex is forecast to weaken as it nears the 

Mississippi River and encounters some drier air and weaker 

instability. However, the shortwave is expected to pivot across 

northern IL Thursday afternoon where forecast sounding show 

sufficient moisture and instability to be present. Thus, suspect

that isolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms 

should develop Thursday afternoon and persist through the 

evening. Given that the overall forcing with the wave will be 

somewhat modest, confidence on the coverage of any 

showers/storms remains rather low and thus have opted to just 

introduce some slight chance (20-30%) POPs mainly for areas 

north of I-80 during this period. With the hit and miss nature 

of these showers/storms suspect many areas may remain dry 

especially those south of I-80. Outside of the showers/storms, 

expect a mix of sun and clouds with high temperatures in the 

upper 80s to near 90 Thursday afternoon. 



Heading into Thursday night, any ongoing showers/storms should 

gradually taper through the night as the shortwave ejects to the

east and the atmosphere loses the diurnal instability. Though, 

if storms are of broader coverage then a few could linger into 

early Friday morning. For now have opted to keep the 20-30% slight

chance POPs going through Friday morning mainly near the IL-WI 

line and north of I-80 in the off chance any showers/storms 

persist. Otherwise, expect Thursday night to mild with lows in 

the 70s areawide.



Yack



Friday Afternoon through Saturday:



The main item of note will be the heat and humidity as the mid-

upper level ridge builds across the region. If lingering cloud 

cover from any decaying convection Friday morning (see previous

section for details) doesn't modestly limit afternoon heating,

highs in the upper 80s to around 90F/low 90s paired with dew

points in the 70s would translate to peak afternoon heat indices 

on Friday in the ~95-100 degree range. Regarding the heat index 

forecast, there's also some uncertainty on specific dew point 

values during peak heating. A warm and humid Friday night,

especially in the core of the Chicago urban heat island, will 

also provide limited relief from the heat.



Saturday looks to feature the highest heat indices of the upcoming

stretch, as 925 mb temps top out in the +26 to +28 Celsius range.

Crop evapotranspiration this time of year typically results in a

slightly lower temps but higher dew points outside of the heart of

the Chicago metro in the farming areas vs. warmer temps and

slightly lower dew points in and near Chicago. Barring earlier

afternoon convection affecting how warm temps get, Saturday's

temps aloft generally point toward highs solidly in the lower 90s

for most locales, and localized mid 90s in the heart of the

Chicago urban heat island. Our current forecast peak heat index

for Saturday is in the ~97-103F range, just below local criteria

(105+F) for heat advisory issuance. That said, if temps end up

close to the latest forecast and dew points overperform a bit,

can't rule out the possibility of some of our counties being

included in a Heat Advisory. All in all, for the Friday-Saturday

period, again while we're not currently forecasting heat advisory

criteria heat indices, those sensitive to the heat will want to 

take proper precautions to avoid heat illness.



Late Saturday through Wednesday:



Despite the presence of strong ridging aloft (500 mb heights of

~590-592 DaM locally), later Saturday into the evening and

overnight hours, capping may be sufficiently eroded for some

convective potential towards and after sunset. Our area won't be

too far removed from short-waves rounding seasonably deep

troughing over southern Manitoba, and as such an isolated

thunderstorm is plausible near/north of I-80 in the early evening

and then in the northwest 1/3 to 1/2 of the area in the late

evening and overnight.



Sunday could end up another very warm to hot and humid day with

highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s, especially if any convection

(and associated effects on temps) holds off until late. Then from

later Sunday onward through early next work week, while there 

remains a fair amount of uncertainty in the details, there have 

been consistent signals in the ensemble guidance for the upper 

ridge to gradually break down across the Upper Midwest. This would

place the local area in a more favorable position to see multiple

rounds of convection as various waves round the ridge. Blended 

20-40% chances for showers and storms seem appropriate at this 

range for the Sunday evening/night through Tuesday timeframe. A 

period of somewhat cooler and less conditions may then follow mid

to late next work week, coinciding with the start of the 2025-2026

school year for parts of the area. 



Castro/Petr



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 06Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 624 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Forecast concerns include...



Low chance (20%) of thunderstorms Thursday afternoon/evening.

Southerly wind directions Thursday.



A lake breeze has moved just west of ORD/MDW early this evening

and will dissipate with sunset. Winds behind the lake breeze 

will become light southeast this evening and then winds 

everywhere will become light southerly later this evening and 

possibly light south/southwest overnight. South/southwest winds 

will increase after sunrise, possibly in the 10-12kt range but 

directions may begin to favor south/southeast by early/mid 

afternoon and likely will be south/southeast by late afternoon. 

Overall low confidence for specific wind directions and timing, 

with changes possible with later forecasts.



There is a small chance for isolated thunderstorms Thursday

afternoon into Thursday evening. Confidence is too low for any

mention with this forecast. There appears to be a better chance

of thunderstorms late Thursday night into early Friday morning,

beyond the end of all of the current tafs but some mention may 

be needed for both time periods as trends emerge. cms



