656

FXUS61 KBOX 062324

AFDBOX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Boston/Norton MA

724 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure crests over New England this afternoon and overnight 

bringing dry and fair weather. More high pressure on Thursday with 

seasonable high temperatures and cool low temperatures. Other 

than a spot shower over western New England today and tomorrow,

dry weather with temperatures running around or slightly cooler

than normal through the end of the week. Warming trend this 

weekend with 90+ degree heat Sunday into early next week along 

with increasing humidity.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Key Messages:





* Smoke concentrations decrease from east to west through the 

  afternoon



* Mainly dry and cooler this afternoon and overnight





Rest of the Afternoon 



GOES True-Color band shows a dense layer of smoke over most of the 

CWA. Greatest concentrations of smoke are currently found across 

western portions of our CWA where PM 2.5 concentrations are between 

100 and 140 ug/m3. Further east, a developing onshore flow has 

brought improvement and webcams near Boston are showing blue skies 

for the first time in over a day! HRRR vertically integrated smoke 

forecasts show continuing improvement through the rest of the day. 

Outside of improving visibility and air quality, a few high clouds 

will continue to filter sunshine and keep most of the area 

noticeably cooler. Highs rise into the upper 70s near eastern MA and 

the Cape and Islands. It will be warmer across the interior with 

temps around 80 by peak heating. The other detail to watch for the 

rest of the day will be the eastward extent of shower and 

thunderstorm activity. Most CAMs show a cluster of showers and 

storms developing across the southern Greens and pushing south 

towards northern MA around sunset. Any precipitation would be 

limited in coverage as activity quickly wanes with the loss of 

daytime heating.  



Tonight...



Pleasant night ahead as high pressure crests over New England. Lows 

will range from the mid 50s to lower 60s. Expecting mainly dry 

conditions outside of patchy fog in typically fog-prone areas.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT/...

* Less smoke and Haze Thurs

* Seasonably cool temps and dry weather continue 



High pres remains in control over New Eng which will bring another 

dry day with high clouds giving way to increasing sunshine in the 

afternoon. A spot shower is possible again across NW MA in the 

afternoon but better chance will be north and west of SNE where 

marginal instability is located. Not much change in low level temps 

so expect similar high temps in the mid 70s to around 80, coolest 

east coastal MA.



Cool and comfortable night ahead Thursday night with somewhat 

favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Temps should fall into 

the middle to upper 50s with a few areas across the northern 

interior perhaps falling into the lower 50s.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Key Messages...



* Dry through at least Sunday. Low confidence in details, but some 

  showers/storms may return during the first half of next week. 



* Temperatures steadily climb each day; into the 90s next week with 

  dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s so it will be very humid 

  once again. 



High pressure in control essentially through the weekend before the 

pattern begins to shift with a trough swinging overhead next week. 

Subsidence beneath this surface high and mid level ridge will keep 

sunny skies around with no rain expected through at least Sunday. 

This pattern should also preclude a potential tropical system from 

making its way too close; ensemble guidance continues to indicate it 

remains well offshore but we will continue to monitor for any 

significant changes. Guidance has slowed the exit of the ridge a bit 

so looking more likely that Monday remains dry while the probability 

increases for some afternoon thunderstorms Tuesday and especially 

Wednesday as the trough moves overhead while dewpoints are in the 

upper 60s and low 70s. 



These elevated dewpoints will also serve to make it feel even hotter 

than the 90+ temperatures which are expected to a limited extent on 

Sunday but more widespread Mon-Wed. Yet to been seen if we'll need 

any heat headlines, but the big picture is that we'll be going from 

a period of below average temps this week to above average next 

week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Forecaster Confidence Levels...



Low - less than 30 percent. 

Moderate - 30 to 60 percent. 

High - greater than 60 percent.



00z TAF Update:



Tonight...Moderate confidence. 



VFR. Low chance for patchy radiation fog in low lying areas.

Moderate confidence on areal and temporal extent of stratus over

east coast/BOS. Also watching for potential stratus deck 

formation over SE MA if winds turn more southerly, otherwise 

light and variable winds.



Thursday...High confidence.



VFR. E-SE wind 5-10 kt. 



KBOS terminal...Low confidence in TAF.



Main concern is a marine stratus deck moving east towards the

terminal. Best guess is that it arrives around 23z and

dissipates after 01z, but confidence is low. Otherwise VFR for 

the TAF period with E to NE winds at 5-10 knots.



KBDL terminal...High confidence in TAF.



Light SSE winds for much of the TAF period.



Light southerly winds



Outlook /Friday through Monday/... 



VFR.



&&



.MARINE...

Forecaster Confidence Levels...



Low - less than 30 percent. 

Medium - 30 to 60 percent. 

High - greater than 60 percent.



Through Thursday Night...High confidence.



Tranquil boating conditions expected to continue into Thu. NE winds 

10-20 kt today, diminishing tonight, then E-SE winds 5-15 kt Thu. 

Seas 3 ft or less all waters.



Outlook /Friday through Monday/...



Friday: Winds less than 25 kt. 



Friday Night through Saturday: Winds less than 25 kt. Seas

locally approaching 5 ft. 



Saturday Night: Winds less than 25 kt. Areas of seas

approaching 5 ft. 



Sunday: Winds less than 25 kt. Seas up to 5 ft. 



Sunday Night through Monday: Winds less than 25 kt. Areas of

seas approaching 5 ft.



&&



