662

FXUS62 KFFC 062324

AFDFFC



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Peachtree City GA

724 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New 00Z Aviation Discussion...



.SHORT TERM...

(This afternoon through Thursday)

Issued at 251 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Key Messages:



   - Drier air is filtering into GA from the north, decreasing the 

     potential for precipitation, though the risk lingers over 

     central GA.



   - Additional drying tomorrow results in even lower risk for 

     rain, except over N GA, where showers/thunderstorms are 

     possible, particularly near the Appalachians.



   - Trend towards higher temperatures continues. Daytime highs 

     tomorrow will be in the mid 70s to upper 80s.



Discussion:



Drier air has filtered in over GA, particularly over N GA and the 

ATL metro. Clearing has been seen in those places, though replaced 

by afternoon cumulus for some parts. The upper-level trough has 

shifted eastward slightly, and is now in the vicinity of the AL/GA 

border. The 250 hPa winds have slowed, overall, with the remnants of 

the jet having moved up over the Carolinas into the MidAtlantic and 

up into Atlantic Canada. The lower levels of ventilation 

decreasing the support for convective development. Furthermore, 

the 12Z FFC RAOB shows significant drying above 700 hPa/10k ft. 

This will inhibit any development taller than that, and therefore 

also limit the development of thunderstorms. That being said, 

moisture remains in place over parts of central GA, fueling the 

development/development potential there, which will decrease after

around 8 PM EDT. 



Tomorrow, drier air will be in place across GA. Model guidance 

suggests precipitable water values on the order of 1.4-1.5 inches, 

near or just below seasonal normals. With the erosion of the wedge, 

additional heating is expected tomorrow. Daytime highs tomorrow are 

forecast to be in the mid 70s to upper 80s, including in the mid 80s 

for metro Atlanta. With this additional energy and decreased 

stability, as the influence of the wedge wanes, popup thunderstorms 

will be possible during the afternoon and evening hours. The highest 

risk will be in central GA into south GA, as well as around the 

Appalachians over N GA, owing to the aid from the terrain to 

convective development, providing lift. PoPs over N GA are in the 20-

50% range for tomorrow afternoon, and around 30% over southern 

portions of central GA. Isolated showers, or even a thunderstorm or 

two, cannot be wholly ruled out over far eastern GA, advected in 

from over AL, where convection is somewhat more likely; that being 

said, generally dry conditions are likely for the Atlanta metro area 

tomorrow into northern central GA. 



CRS



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Friday morning through next Tuesday)

Issued at 251 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



At a glance:



- Less rain, but still generally unsettled weather through the long 

term.



- Temperatures will gradually warm back towards normal.



Heading into the long term will see an easing of some of the heavy 

rain that we've seen over the past few days and a gradual return to 

temperatures that we'd normally expect for this time of year. Upper 

level RH values remain on the wet side and a southerly flow means 

afternoon thunderstorms will be the trend through the first half of 

the long term. 



By Tuesday, the models start to diverge. The Euro is brining another 

cold front into the area, and with it more rain while the GFS keeps 

things on the drier side for north and central Georgia. A more 

active Tropical Atlantic makes resolving some of these features more 

difficult as a named storm well off the Atlantic coast can introduce 

drier air on the back side. The NHC is currently tracking a pair of 

low pressure systems that they expect to develop. At this time, the 

forecast shows now interaction with out CWA but the long reaching 

effects of the storms will likely play a roll on determining the 

type of weather we see.



Temps will gradually return to normal during the course of the long 

term as well with highs in the low to mid 80s by Friday and a good 

signal that highs near 90 will return towards the middle of next 

week. 



Vaughn



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 722 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR through daytime periods with MVFR and temporary IFR late 

overnight to early morning Thursday. Winds E to NE at 4 to 9 kts.

Light showers in area possible along GA AL border may impact CSG

Thursday afternoon.





//ATL Confidence...00Z Update...

Medium confidence cigs overnight.

High confidence all other elements.



SM



