[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8204","issue":"2025-08-06T23:26:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KILX","awipsid":"AFDILX"}]
=================================================



684

FXUS63 KILX 062326

AFDILX



Area Forecast Discussion...Updated Aviation

National Weather Service Lincoln IL

626 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A slight (15-25%) chance for showers and thunderstorms exists

  late this evening in west central IL. Severe weather is not

  anticipated. Similar chances exist tomorrow afternoon but with 

  the very marginal risk of severe winds/hail. 



- Dry and seasonably hot weather will be in place this weekend

  before rain chances (20-40%) return early next week ahead of a

  cold front.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 241 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Today has been quite pleasant thus far. Highs in the 80s this 

afternoon will cool into the upper 60s tonight. There is the hint 

for another foggy morning tomorrow. Today will remain generally dry, 

but there has been a slight shift in what the CAMs are showing in 

terms of precipitation this evening.  



The CAMs (NAMNest, HRRR, HRW ARW...) are showing new development 

along the outflow boundary of the decaying MCS over Iowa/Missouri 

this evening in central IL. Timing is depicting the new convection 

to develop over eastern Iowa this afternoon and entering our western 

CWA by 00z. We have increased pops for this evening for areas mainly 

west of the I-55 corridor. Severe weather is not expected with this 

but lightning and heavy rain are. PWATs of 1.7-2.0 are forecast as 

this complex travels through to the south-southeast. This complex 

will be in southeastern IL after midnight, lingering into the 

morning. 



Tomorrow, after the showers and storms end in the morning, most of 

the day looks to be dry for most people in our forecast area. On the 

flip side, SPC is thinking we could see a marginal risk for severe 

weather. Most models have us being dry tomorrow, but a small MCV 

could develop out of the overnight convection in the Iowa area. That 

MCV **possibly** could spark off afternoon convection in west 

central IL. The probability currently is very low (< 20%) but by the 

off chance we get development, we could see a marginal risk for 

damaging winds and/or marginally severe hail. 



The weekend will be seasonably hot as a high pressure moves in. A 

weak front will start to move into the region late Sunday into 

monday. This will bring additional rain chances into the forecast 

for the new week. 



Copple



&&



.AVIATION...  (For the 00z TAFs through 00z Thursday Evening)

Issued at 626 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions will prevail at all TAF sites next 24hrs. Few CU

remains from earlier this afternoon but mid clouds around 15kft

are advecting over the sites. Will make this broken as it seems to

be increasing based on satellite loops. This AC will remain

overnight but be scattered. By morning, it will be gone and few to

scattered cirrus will remain. If any showers or storms develop

this evening, they will be very isolated, so not going to have any

mention of convection with the TAF issuance. If something does

develop and threatens the sites, will amend. Winds will be

south-southeast this evening but become light and variable

overnight. Southerly winds are expected tomorrow at less than

10kts.



Auten



&&



.ILX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.

&&



$$



