2025-08-06T23-27-18-958Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8209","issue":"2025-08-06T23:27:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KICT","awipsid":"AFDICT"}]
=================================================



689

FXUS63 KICT 062327

AFDICT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Wichita KS

627 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot Thursday and Friday



- Cold front arrives this weekend. Set to bring low rain chances

  and a moderation in temperatures late this weekend into early

  next week.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 156 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Early this afternoon, a strong mid/upper ridge was centered over the 

southwestern CONUS with weak troughing present across the Great 

Lakes and Ohio River Valley. The remnants of a powerful overnight 

MCS across the Missouri River Valley have continued into far

eastern/southeastern Kansas this afternoon. Brief heavy

rainfall and gusty winds are possible along and east of a Yates

Center-Coffeyville line over the next few hours. Additionally,

these showers and storms are helping to cool temperatures off

across far eastern Kansas, albeit briefly. Storms should move

off to the southeast by this evening, and quiet conditions are

generally expected today. With that said, with some mid-level

WAA Thursday morning, we can't rule out a couple of showers and

storms across the Flint Hills around sunrise. Anything that

develops is unlikely to be strong or severe, with occasional

cloud-to-ground lightning strikes and brief heavy rainfall being

the main concerns.



Over the next couple of days, much of the central plains will 

really begin to feel the influence of the southwest CONUS ridge.

While 500 mb heights will be somewhat hampered by an upper 

trough digging into the northern plains later this week, mid-

level temperatures suggest much of the central and south-central

Kansas could see their hottest days of the year both Thursday 

and Friday. Both the GEFS and EPS show 850 mb temps solidly at

or above the 99.5th percentile along and west of the I-135 

corridor. Record highs are currently not being forecast, but 

this will likely be the best opportunity for sites like Wichita 

to see their first triple digit temperature of the year (a deeper

dive into climo will be under the Climate portion of this 

discussion). Additionally, there's still going to be just 

enough moisture to drive heat indices up around 105 to 109 

degrees both Thursday and Friday afternoons, especially along 

and west of I-135. A heat advisory was issued for Thursday 

afternoon, and another one is likely to be needed for Friday 

afternoon, especially with hotter temperatures forecast for 

Friday.



The central plains will begin to feel the influence of the 

aforementioned trough that will progress across the northern plains 

this weekend. This will help to usher in a cold front that is 

forecast to sweep across the region late Saturday or early

Sunday. The expectation is for periodic storm chances 

throughout the latter half of the weekend and into the 

beginning of next week along with a moderation in temperatures 

across the area. There are still some questions about the 

coverage of storms across the area, and it's possible some areas

may miss out on rainfall entirely. At this time, widespread 

severe weather appears unlikely, but we can't rule out a few 

strong storms given decent instability and shear that will be 

present across the region. Temperature-wise, afternoon highs and

overnight lows will be close to, if not slightly below normal 

for this time of year by the beginning of next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 623 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions are expected through this TAF period. Low-level

wind shear is possible at all sites except CNU tonight into 

early morning as the low-level jet ramps up. Breezy southerly 

winds will continue with gusts up to 25 kts.



&&



.CLIMATE...

Issued at 156 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Since records began in 1889, Wichita has only had 7 calendar 

years without a 100-degree temperature. The most recent year 

without a 100-degree temperatures was 1927. 



Currently, the forecast is for Wichita to hit 99 on Thursday, 101 on 

Friday, and 99 on Saturday. If Wichita were to hit 100 on Thursday, 

it would be the latest first 100-degree day since 2007. If Wichita 

were to hit 100 on Friday, it would be the latest first 100-degree 

day since 1928. With decreased moisture across the area along with 

it being, climatologically, the warmest part of the year, the next 3 

days or so is likely the best opportunity for Wichita to hit 100 

degrees this year, especially on Friday.



&&



.ICT WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Heat Advisory from 1 PM to 8 PM CDT Thursday for KSZ032-033-

047>051-067-068-082-083-091-092.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...JC

AVIATION...GC

CLIMATE...JC



