[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8212","issue":"2025-08-06T23:27:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KAPX","awipsid":"AFDAPX"}]
=================================================



692

FXUS63 KAPX 062327

AFDAPX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Gaylord MI

727 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Hot and stormy/unsettled weather ahead...



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Issued at 351 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



PATTERN SYNOPSIS/FORECAST:



Split flow continues across the eastern US...but starting to 

slightly shift eastward...as troughing moves through western 

Canada...and ridging continues to strengthen over the Desert SW. 

Slight eastward progression of the pattern also lending eastward 

progression of hot/humid air across the central Plains (20C and 

hotter at 850mb)...with eastward extent of plume of EMLs sneaking 

into the MS Valley along better return flow...as 1034mb surface high 

tracks over Quebec and northern New England. Now that return flow is 

a bit closer to us...seeing an increase in moisture across the area 

(dewpoints in the 60s today vs generally in the 50s yesterday)...and 

even a touch warmer with a little less in the way of smoke in spots 

overhead as that air mass slowly starts to head out. We even started 

out the day with a little elevated instability per our 12z sounding, 

which is something to bear in mind as diurnal cu pops up this 

afternoon. 



Pattern to continue to progress eastward. Subtle trough to swing by 

to our north tonight into Thursday as the northern stream ridge 

departs. Appears this will drag some semblance of a BCZ across the 

area, which could stall out over the region Thursday night into 

Friday. Increasing southwesterly flow aloft should advect 

increasingly unstable air into the region, allowing for shower/storm 

chances to start, perhaps even as early as Thursday morning. Will 

also look for it to become warmer and more humid Thursday...though 

the one bonus is that we should have less smoke around. 



Primary Forecast Concerns through Thursday: 



Showers/storms late tonight into Thursday: Based on the last couple 

mornings walking in to see convective complexes upstream along the 

EML plume...do have some concerns for this idea to impact parts of 

our region tonight. Setup tonight may not be quite as good as points 

westward have been, as the EML plume and LLJ will likely be getting 

stretched out over the region, such that we may not get quite the 

same focus as points upstream have...and there are some signals in 

the guidance (of the LLJ pointing much more to our north) that 

activity may try to track north of us where the better warm 

advection should focus. However...if we do get an MCS to our 

northwest tonight...thickness gradient could support it tracking 

near or into part of our area. 



Better chances for activity will come on Thursday during the day 

with the more unstable environment in the region. Flow should remain 

largely weak again...with potential for lake breezes to focus 

convergence and thunderstorm development, and with a bit of synoptic 

assistance as a subtle wave tracks by to our northwest. Do have some 

concerns this could lay out a boundary across our region going into 

Thursday night, which, as moisture increases, could leave parts of 

our area open for a heavier rain event. 



Heat Thursday...Considering we are already warming into the mid and 

upper 80s across much of the interior today...and with the 

expectation that we should be warmer tonight (lows around 60 or so) 

think a lack of smoke to obscure the sun, combined with increasing 

warm advection, supports temperatures rising into the upper 80s. 

Would not be out of the question for a couple spots to hit 90 

(especially where downsloping on southerly winds will enhance 

warming/drying). Potential for deeper mixing could help mix down 

somewhat drier air that could slow initial convective development.



&&



.LONG TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 351 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Day 2-3 (Friday-Saturday)...



Hot/sticky with chances for storms...Prevailing southwesterly flow 

ahead of a strong upper low crossing south-central Canada will keep 

the heat cranked up going into the end of the week. Plume of 20+C 

850mb temps will start to creep in Friday, and should largely be 

overhead Saturday...supporting the potential for highs into the 

upper 80s and lower 90s by the weekend. Potential wrench in this 

plan is that any convective activity or convective debris (i.e., 

cloud cover) could limit diurnal heating and keep us from maxing out 

temps (for better or worse, depending on how much you love summer 

heat). It will also be muggy again, with dewpoints likely to reach 

and/or exceed the 70 degree mark. Will have to watch for periods of 

showers/storms at times; some question of how much capping plays a 

role in convective development with the warm air aloft (potential 

for 700mb temps to reach or exceed 12C...toasty)...but with that hot 

air/EML plume approaching the region, think we will need to keep an 

eye out for nocturnal MCS development somewhere in the region. 



Days 4-5 (Sunday-Monday)... 



Overall system/front may be slow to move through...which could leave 

things a bit on the warm side Sunday across parts of the area. This 

will keep the threat for showers/storms in the forecast into the 

start of next week, particularly noting signals for the broader 

trough axis to try to hang out to our west into Monday...which could 

keep parts of our region in a more unstable environment. If the 

boundary hangs out over the region for a few days and several waves 

ride along it...not impossible we could be looking at some heavy 

rain concerns as well. 



Days 6-7 (Tuesday-Wednesday)...



Upper low and attendant trough axis finally swing eastward toward 

the middle of next week...which suggests we should return to a 

cooler environment at least for a time...though potentially also 

smokier again...but still quite a bit of uncertainty to contend with 

for the end of the period and beyond. Longer range signals have been 

pointing toward a more active and warmer trend for the middle of 

August...so stay tuned.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 727 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Mainly VFR, but we may see SHRA/TSRA Thursday afternoon. Lots of

high clouds across the area tonight and into Thursday. Muggier

air Thursday will result in a developing cu field, and

eventually a better chance for showers/storms in the afternoon.

For now am keeping precip out of the TAFs (the best chance is in

the interior of northern lower MI, inland from all of our TAF

sites). 



Winds lighten tonight. Southerly breezes develop Thursday.



&&



.APX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MI...None.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...FEF

LONG TERM...FEF

AVIATION...JZ



