853

FXUS63 KLMK 062328

AFDLMK



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Louisville KY

728 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...Updated Aviation Discussion...



.KEY MESSAGES...



* Isolated to scattered showers and storms expected this afternoon.



* Drier conditions with warming temperatures Thursday through the 

  weekend.



* Storm chances increase again early next week as cold front 

  approaches the region.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 303 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Partly sunny skies were noted across the region this afternoon.  A 

diurnal Cu field was present with a few showers and storms 

stretching in an arc from Bowling Green eastward to Lake Cumberland 

and then back northward along the I-75 corridor.  Temperatures were 

mainly in the middle to upper 80s.  The hot spots in KY were 

generally down across south-central KY down in the Campbellsville 

and Columbia area.



For the remainder of the afternoon, scattered showers and storms 

will be possible in any one area.  However, the most likely corridor 

for storms will generally be across the Bluegrass region of central 

Kentucky.  Brief heavy rainfall, gusty winds, and lightning will be 

the main concerns with this activity.  A quick quarter to a half 

inch of rainfall is possible, with some locally higher amounts of 1-

2 inches in isolated spots. Highs on the day will top out in the mid-

upper 80s, though a few spots could touch 90 here and there.



For this evening and overnight, scattered convection will wind down 

with the setting sun and skies are expected to clear out with winds 

becoming light.  Patchy fog development will be possible in the 

valleys and in areas that see appreciable rainfall this afternoon. 

Lows will be in the upper 60s to around 70.



Thursday will feature another partly sunny day.  With the upper 

trough axis moving further away from the region, we're not going to 

have a lot focused lifting across the region.  A few isolated storms 

will be possible down near the Lake Cumberland region, but PoPs will 

be generally less than 20%.  Highs on the day will be in the 84-89 

degree range.  Mostly clear skies are expected for Thursday night 

with lows in the upper 60s to around 70.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 303 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Friday through Sunday Night...



Moving into the late week and weekend, the forecast thinking here 

has not changed all that much in the last 24 hours.  Heat ridge 

across the four corners region is forecast to expand northeastward 

into the southern Plains and into the western Ohio Valley.  A 

general baggy trough axis is expected to remain just to our east 

along the Mid-Atlantic region.  Our local weather is expected to 

feature partly to mostly sunny days with mostly clear nights.  There 

could be some isolated showers/storms down in the Lake Cumberland 

region over into the hills of SE KY.  



Highs during the period will see temperatures near normal with highs 

in the upper 80s.  Several spots could top out in the lower 90s. 

Overnight lows will be in the upper 60s to the lower 70s.



Monday through Wednesday...



Moving into next week, the upper ridge axis will push eastward and 

settle over the Mid-Atlantic area.  As this occurs, a pretty healthy 

upper level trough axis will transverse the northern US and head 

into the Midwest.  This will produce a period of active weather over 

the northern high Plains and into the northern Midwest (ND/SD/MN/WI).



This upper trough axis will slowly push eastward into the Great 

Lakes and will attempt to drive a cold front into the Ohio Valley in 

the Tuesday/Wednesday time frame.  Earlier runs were a little faster 

with the frontal passage, but have slowed down a bit in the latest 

runs.  



Local weather will feature a continued warm/dry pattern for Monday 

with highs in the upper 80s to around 90.  Showers and thunderstorms 

look to become a bit more widespread in the Tues/Wed period as the 

frontal boundary pushes in from the northwest.  Model soundings show 

a bit more instability here and a little more shear than what we've 

seen of late.  So some strong storms can't be ruled out in the 

Tue/Wed period.  Temperatures here will remain near seasonal norms 

with highs in the upper 80s to around 90 and overnight lows in the 

upper 60s to the lower 70s.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 06Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 727 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



SCT cu continues to diminish this evening, leaving us with mostly 

clear skies and light and variable winds to end the day. Mainly VFR 

conditions likely through 06Z Thu. 



Areas of fog are likely across the region between 07-13Z Thu, and 

MVFR or lower vsbys appear most likely at LEX, RGA, and HNB. Brief 

IFR/LIFR vsbys possible at those three terminals, especially between 

08-12Z. VFR likely across the board after 13Z Thu, with light winds 

and another round of afternoon cu.



&&



.LMK WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

KY...None.

IN...None.



&&



$$



