2025-08-06T23-29-46-033Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8385","issue":"2025-08-06T23:29:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KBUF","awipsid":"AFDBUF"}]
=================================================



865

FXUS61 KBUF 062329

AFDBUF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Buffalo NY

729 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

A large area of surface high pressure continues to settle from 

New England to the eastern seaboard through the end of the work

week. A southerly flow on the back side of this surface high 

will allow for a modest increase in moisture that will nudge the

afternoon humidity up, while also allowing for a spot shower or

thunderstorm today, and then east of Lake Ontario on Thursday. 

As surface high pressure remains near our region into the 

weekend, the dry spell is expected to persist through the 

weekend, with modest humidity values early edging higher through

the weekend.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Currently across Western and North Central New York, the last of the 

shower and thunderstorm activity from earlier in the afternoon are 

gradually petering out this evening with the main areas of activity 

lying across the lake plains from Niagara Falls to Rochester. A few 

showers are lingering across the western Southern Tier in Chautauqua 

and Cattaraugus counties. Additionally a few showers continue to pop 

up along outflow boundaries across the Buffalo Metro area.



Showers and thunderstorms will gradually dissipate headed towards 

midnight. Localized valley fog overnight across the Southern Tier 

looks possible once again for Thursday morning. Scattered rain 

showers will develop again Thursday for areas east of Lake Ontario 

as the weak shortwave continues to slowly progress underneath the 

general mid-level ridging pattern across eastern Canada and the 

northeast US. Precipitation totals will generally be light, but 

there could be a brief heavy downpour in the vicinity of Lewis Co 

where the terrain may aid in convective development Thursday 

afternoon.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

Headed into Thursday night, any lingering precipitation across the 

north-country should clear up with drier air aloft moving overhead 

for Friday. Afternoon temperatures Friday will climb into the mid-

80s for most across western NY. A weak frontal passage to the north, 

associated with a surface low well to the north moving across Hudson 

Bay, may bring a few isolated showers towards the Niagara Peninsula, 

but more likely will only lead to some increasing cloudiness in the 

afternoon. Height rises will begin late Friday afternoon with 

generally southerly low-level flow on the western edge of the 

surface high off the Atlantic Coast. This will commence the warmup 

headed into this weekend.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

An amplifying longwave pattern expected over the weekend with a 

strong 594mb ridge slowly building off the Southeast coast as a 

trough digs into the northern Plains. Sfc high pressure under the 

ridge will extend into the eastern Great Lakes region from the 

western Atlantic, providing very warm but dry weather through early 

next week.



Heat: While Saturday will likely be a few degrees warmer than the 

preceding days, 850mb temperatures are expected to climb to around 

+18C Sunday and Monday which will translate to widespread afternoon 

high temps in the low 90s across the Lake Plains. While dewpoints 

will also creep up several degrees, given the dry antecedent 

conditions humidity levels are not expected to be too oppressive. 

While not out of the question that a few spots may reach low-end 

Heat Advisory criteria, more widespread or higher end headline 

potential appears unlikely. Heat may partially linger into Tuesday 

with increased humidity depending on the timing of an approaching 

cold front next week.



Precipitation: The trough over the northern Plains will inevitably 

cross into the Great Lakes region next week. This will send the 

aforementioned cold front into the region from the northwest and 

bring us more appreciable chances for some needed rainfall in the 

form of showers and thunderstorms. Timing remains uncertain as 

mentioned previously though dry conditions are expected to persist 

through at least early Tuesday, with greater chances then lasting 

through Wednesday.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

VFR conditions expected across area terminals for much of the 

forecast period with times of visibility dropping to MVFR conditions 

with smoke/haze. Isolated showers and thunderstorms that developed 

across the region this afternoon will linger into the early evening 

hours, with much of the activity lying across the lake plains 

between Niagara Falls and Rochester. A few showers continue to 

develop along outflow boundaries across the Buffalo Metro as well. 

Storms will dissipate this evening.



Similar to the nights prior, river valley fog will be possible again 

tonight across the western Southern Tier supporting MVFR to IFR 

visibilities.



Outlook...



Thursday...Mainly VFR. A small chance of showers and thunderstorms 

east of Lake Ontario.



Thursday night...Mainly VFR. Localized fog and IFR possible across 

the river valleys of the Southern Tier.



Friday through Monday...VFR



&&



.MARINE...

Surface high pressure continues to slide southward across New 

England through tonight, with winds of an easterly component becoming

more southerly tonight. On the west end of Lake Ontario these 

speeds have reached 10-15 knots with a light chop to the 

waters.



Otherwise with surface high pressure anchored along the eastern 

seaboard through the remainder of the week winds will remain light 

fluctuating between southerly and easterly.



&&



.BUF WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

NY...Air Quality Alert until midnight EDT tonight for NYZ001>007-

     010>014-019>021-085.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...Brothers/Thomas

NEAR TERM...Brothers/EAJ

SHORT TERM...Brothers

LONG TERM...PP

AVIATION...Brothers/EAJ

MARINE...Brothers/Thomas



