869

FXUS62 KMLB 062329

AFDMLB



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Melbourne FL

729 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Issued at 238 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



- Highest rain chances (up to 60-70%) will be across the interior

  late this afternoon and evening. A few stronger storms will 

  likely result, producing strong wind gusts to 45-55 mph, 

  frequent lightning and locally heavy rainfall of 2-4 inches. 



- Shower and storm chances will be above normal (up to 60-80%) 

  from late week into the weekend, with greatest coverage in the 

  afternoon and evening. 



- The higher rain chances will offer some respite from the heat,

  but also continue a risk of strong storms capable of gusty

  winds, deadly lightning, and locally heavy rain.



&&



.PREVIOUS DISCUSSION...

Issued at 238 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Currently-Tonight...Convective development a little slow to start 

again this afternoon as the east coast sea breeze moves inland. 

However, a moist airmass remains across the area, with 15Z XMR 

sounding showing PW values around 2.1 inches. Therefore, still 

expecting isolated to scattered showers and storms to develop 

through mid to late afternoon, becoming scattered to numerous near 

to west of Orlando metro area late day/early evening, as boundaries 

collide over the interior. DCAPE values not quite as high as 

yesterday, but are still around 800-1000 J/kg, which will still 

support some stronger storms capable of producing strong gusts up to 

45-55 mph and frequent lightning. However, can't completely rule out 

an isolated severe storm or two, producing locally damaging wind 

gusts up to 60 mph. Additionally, there will be a very low potential 

for funnel clouds or a brief tornado given all the boundary 

interactions. Weak steering winds will again lead to slow and 

variable storm motion, with locally heavy rainfall up to 2-4 inches 

and minor flooding also a concern. Convection will gradually 

diminish through late evening with some areas of light debris cloud 

rainfall persisting. 



Mostly dry conditions then forecast overnight, but an approaching 

wave currently over the Bahamas, and a weak onshore flow may 

transport a few showers or an isolated storm or two onshore through 

late tonight. Remaining warm with lows in the mid to upper 70s most 

locations.  



Thursday-Friday...Weak frontal boundary across north Florida is 

forecast to drop slowly southward into north central FL into late 

week. Moisture will continue to increase with PW values around 2.0-

2.2 inches. The presence of this boundary and deeper moisture will 

lead to above normal shower and storm coverage, with rain chances up 

to 60-80 percent Thursday, and 70-80 percent on Friday. A weak 

onshore flow should still focus greatest coverage of showers and 

storms inland, but with deeper moisture in play, coastal areas 

should still see at least scattered coverage of showers and storms 

as sea breeze initially forms. A few stronger storms will continue 

to be possible, producing strong wind gusts and frequent lightning. 

The very moist airmass and weak steering winds will also continue 

the threat of locally heavy rainfall of 2-4" and minor flooding for 

any slow moving or repeated rounds of heavier showers and storms. 



Increasing convection and cloud cover should keep max temps a little 

closer to normal with highs in the low 90s for most spots on 

Thursday and upper 80s to low 90s on Friday. Humid conditions will 

still produce peak heat index values up to around 100-105 degrees. 



Saturday-Tuesday...(Modified Previous Discussion) Weak forcing 

leaves some questions marks in the details of the forecast in the 

extended term. Aloft broad, weak ridging builds across the eastern 

US, and remains in place over the Subtropical Atlantic towards 

Florida. Without any large scale forcing the front continues its 

extended residency somewhere near Central or North Florida, 

depending if and where additional disturbances develop along the 

boundary. Either way, even ensembles keep very high moisture (PWATs 

2+ inches) in place over Central Florida, through the weekend, then 

moisture begins to gradually decrease early next week. Very high 

rain chances (up to 70-80 percent) continue through the weekend, 

then gradually decrease to 60-70 percent on Monday and 50-60 percent 

on Tuesday as we progress into early next week. Temperatures follow 

the same trend, remaining near to slightly below normal Saturday and 

Sunday with the highest rain chances, then trending back up next 

week as the rain chances decrease. 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 238 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Tonight-Monday...A weak frontal boundary will linger across the 

Florida peninsula over the next several days. A weak area of low 

pressure along this boundary well east-northeast of the coastal 

waters currently has low chance (30%) chance for tropical 

development over the next 7 days. However, regardless of 

development, this system is not forecast to not have any direct 

impacts to the east central FL waters as it lifts N/NE and farther 

away of the region into the weekend. 



Winds are forecast to remain mostly light and variable through 

tonight into Thursday morning, with speeds around 5 knots, and then 

becoming onshore into Thursday afternoon around 5-10 knots as sea 

breeze forms and shifts inland. Winds then predominately remain 

onshore, generally out of the southeast Friday and into the weekend, 

and then becoming more easterly into Monday, with wind speeds 

remaining below 15 knots. Seas are currently forecast to remain 

around 2-3 feet through Saturday, and then building to 3-4 feet 

Sunday into Monday. 



Above normal coverage of showers and storms is forecast over the 

waters from late week and into the weekend as frontal boundary 

lingers across the area and deeper moisture remains in place. Rain 

chances then may trend closer to normal into early next week as 

deeper moisture shifts west-northwest.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFs)

Issued at 725 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A fairly early end to convection near the terminals this

afternoon. However, some VCSH will continue to linger near

MCO/ISM/SFB/DAB through around 1Z. Then, dry conditions look to

prevail, through the remainder of the overnight hours. CAMs

suggest a few showers along the Treasure Coast early in the

morning, though this is low confidence. Instead, higher confidence

in VCTS developing along the sea breeze around 17Z, then moving

inland through the afternoon hours. Light and variable winds

overnight will become northeast/east Thursday and increase to

around 8-10kts behind the sea breeze.



&&



