[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8457","issue":"2025-08-06T23:33:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KABQ","awipsid":"AFDABQ"}]
=================================================



938

FXUS65 KABQ 062333 AAA

AFDABQ



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service Albuquerque NM

533 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Issued at 1236 AM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025

 

- There is high confidence in widespread moderate to major heat

  risk impacts today through Friday, which may cause health 

  issues for individuals without adequate cooling and hydration. 

  Some areas will approach record highs.



- Gusty and potentially severe winds across western and northern 

  NM today through Friday from isolated to widely scattered virga 

  showers and dry thunderstorms.



- Temperatures not as hot along with better shower and storm

  coverage later this weekend into early next week.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This afternoon through Thursday night) 

Issued at 1121 AM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Today and Thursday will be the hottest days of the work week, as 

the upper high strengthens to near 601dam at 500mb directly over 

our area. The record 500mb height for KABQ for the month of August

is 599dam, so we may break that record and challenge the all-time

500mb height record of 601dam. Mid level moisture has increased 

over western and northern NM compared to 24hrs ago and will be 

more than sufficient for a round of scattered and high-based 

showers and storms this afternoon/evening and again on Thursday. 

The wetting rain footprint from these rounds of convection will be

extremely small, with most areas receiving a trace to 0.05" and 

strong/erratic wind gusts that may reach to near 60mph. Pressure

heights trend down by around 1-2dam Thursday, correlating to a 1-2

degree downtrend in temperatures but still high enough to warrant

another Heat Advisory for the Farmington area, ABQ Metro and

Roswell area. 



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Friday through Tuesday)

Issued at 1121 AM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The upper high is forecast to weaken to near 596dam at 500mb over

NM on Friday as an upper level trough swings east through the

northern/central Rockies. Temperatures will trend down as a

result, but we have a good chance to hit advisory criteria once

again around Roswell and in the ABQ Metro. Another round of mostly

dry storms and virga showers is forecast Friday afternoon, with

more late day strong/erratic wind gusts. More of the same on

Saturday, but with no areas hitting Heat Advisory criteria. The

upper high is then forecast to recenter over the lower Great Basin

as the aforementioned upper level trough ejects out into the

northern/central US plains. The upper high is then forecast to

shift south and center over AZ and expand a ridge axis back over

NM on Tue/Wed. This progression will hold-off any potential 

Monsoon moisture plumes and will favor northeast NM for daily 

rounds of storms due to weak backdoor fronts aided by convective 

outflows. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 527 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Isolated showers and storms continue to slowly trek eastward

across central and northern NM this evening. Gusty and erratic

outflow winds to around 30KT have been observed today, although

locally higher winds to around 40KT cannot be ruled out.

Convection will decrease in coverage after 02Z, with a few storms

lingering until around 05Z in the east. Isolated gusty virga 

showers and storms will once again be a concern around much of the

area tomorrow afternoon. VFR conditions will continue to prevail 

around the region through the TAF period.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 1121 AM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Critical fire weather conditions are not forecast through at least

the next seven days. However, elevated to near-critical fire

weather conditions are forecast across northwest NM through

Saturday, especially near the CO border where afternoon gustiness

is expected. Otherwise, unusually hot and dry conditions will

persist through the work-week under the influence of strong high

pressure aloft. Sufficient moisture in the mid levels of the

atmosphere will fuel rounds of mostly dry showers and storms each

afternoon through Friday, but with very low chances for wetting

rain and high chances for strong/erratic wind gusts. As high

pressure aloft recenters west of the area, a downtrend in 

temperatures and uptrend in humidity and chances for storms are 

forecast late in the weekend through the middle of next week, with

wetting rains favoring areas along/east of the central mountain 

chain.



&&



