=================================================



951

FXUS61 KRNK 062334

AFDRNK



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Blacksburg VA

734 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure over the northeast U.S. will keep a wedge of

cooler air in place across the Mid-Atlantic states through the 

end of the work week. A stalled frontal boundary along the 

coast of the Carolinas will bring additional rain chances late 

in the work week and into the weekend depending on the 

eventual track of any tropical moisture currently draped across

this boundary.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

As of 715 PM EDT Wednesday...



Key Message:



1) Temperatures cool through Thursday.



2) Rainfall about gone, but some light rain still possible

tonight, some drizzle around the Blue Ridge this evening.



Noticeable convection in east KY spilling over into the

Cumberland mountains of VA/KY. At this time, models start to

show this activity waning as it approaches our far southwest 

CWA. Meanwhile, light rain/drizzle in patches from the NRV to 

the Piedmont but mostly a non measurable or very low amount 

expected. May see some uptick in patchy rain in the piedmont 

overnight as an area of deeper convergence starts to shift west 

coincident with some upper support.





Previous discussion...



Updated forecast as radar trend shows drying across most of the

forecast area. Some residual light rain/drizzle possible in the

New River Valley south to the Blue Ridge. Also removed thunder 

from most of the forecast area as main instability is outside 

the wedge over far SW VA and southeast of the NC Triangle.



Previous discussion...



Patchy drizzle along with light to moderate rain continued to

fall, mainly east of the Blue Ridge this afternoon. An area of 

short wave energy/mean troughing aloft will continue to 

interact with a high pressure wedge at the surface to bring 

periods of light to moderate precipitation through tonight. 



Any additional rainfall through tonight will be light to nil

west of the Blue Ridge. Areas to the east will see rainfall 

amounts through morning totaling less than a quarter inch in the

foothills to as high as an inch as you go farther SE. QPF 

amounts increase markedly towards Rockingham and Stokes Counties

in NC, and Pittsylvania and Halifax Counties in SW VA. The 

higher amounts of rain are falling farther southeast towards 

Raleigh. At this time ensemble probabilities of reaching more 

than 1.5 and even 1 inch in our area (aforementioned counties) 

are quite low. FFG values for 6 hours are around 3 to 4 inches 

in that area, so not expecting to reach FFG. Rain gradually 

tapers off as the upper level disturbance shifts east. 



Overnight, lows will be in the upper 50s to low 60s, with  

mostly cloudy skies and some fog. Expect the occasional shower 

for Southside VA and NW NC through Thursday. Tomorrow will 

still be cloudy and below normal for highs, which will reach the 

upper 60s to mid 70s. 



Confidence in the near term is moderate to high.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY NIGHT/...

As of 100 PM EDT WEDNESDAY...



Key Messages:



1) Light rain and cool temperatures into the weekend



High pressure centered over New England continues to hold us in 

a wedge the second half of the week. Cool moist air will remain 

situated firmly overhead. The cold air damming will keep the sky

almost entirely overcast during this period, blocking the sun 

from doing much to warm things up. As such, temperatures will 

run about 5- 10 degrees cooler than usual, with highs in the 

upper 60s west of the Blue Ridge, and 70s east. Along with the 

overcast skies, weak isentropic lift over the wedge will provide

for low chances of rain throughout the forecast area. 

Accumulation amounts would be light with any rain that does 

fall.



Come Saturday the wedge will begin to falter as an area of low 

pressure off the southeast coast, which is being monitored to 

potentially become the next tropical system, will move 

northward, starting the process of breaking down the wedge. Some 

convection will be possible in NC, and temperatures will begin 

to warm as the sun finally breaks through the cloud cover.



&&



.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

As of 115 PM EDT WEDNESDAY...



Key Messages:



1) More summery pattern returns, temperatures warm up



After the wedge is displaced, a subtropical ridge will set up

over most of the southeastern CONUS. We will be located 

northwest of the ridge, and southwesterly flow will move off the 

Gulf and start to warm things back up. In addition, the overcast

skies will break and the sun will have an opportunity to warm 

things up again. Temperatures will be back around normal early 

next week, and headed to slightly above normal by the middle of 

next week. Along with this more typical summery pattern, 

afternoon diurnal convection will be a daily occurrence, placing

scattered thunderstorms back in the forecast for much of next 

week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

As of 720 PM EDT Wednesday...



MVFR to IFR CIGs continue across most of the area, though some

VFR at LYH. At this time anticipate mainly sub-VFR at all

terminals overnight, most MVFR in the 1-3kft range with vsbys

>3sm.



Enough of some clearing may allow for fog to become a problem

at LWB, possibly BLF/BCB late, with LIFR forecast at times. 



May not see much improvement til after 18z at most sites to VFR

some MVFR east at LYH/DAN.



With rainfall looking sparse kept it out of tafs for the most

part through the period. Winds should remain around 5 knots or 

less out of the east/northeast through tonight. On Thursday, 

winds may be slightly gusty to 15 kts in the Piedmont.





.EXTENDED AVIATION OUTLOOK...



Trends pointing toward drier weather but with residual wedge,

may be a case of VFR during the late morning til early evening,

then some lower cigs/fog at night into the early morning. Rain



This pattern looks to weaken over the weekend but still some

potential for rain showers if a low off the southeast coast

develops further west. At this time, looking mainly VFR outside

of late night fog/stratus into Monday.



&&



.RNK WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

VA...None.

NC...None.

WV...None.



&&



$$



SYNOPSIS...SH/WP

NEAR TERM...SH/WP

SHORT TERM...VFJ

LONG TERM...VFJ

AVIATION...SH/WP



