[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8499","issue":"2025-08-06T23:34:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KGRB","awipsid":"AFDGRB"}]
=================================================



981

FXUS63 KGRB 062334

AFDGRB



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Green Bay WI

634 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Updated aviation portion for 00Z TAF issuance



.KEY MESSAGES...



- There is a Marginal Risk of severe thunderstorms on Thursday

  afternoon and Thursday night. The strongest storms will be

  capable of damaging wind gusts, torrential downpours, and small

  hail.



- Heat and humidity will build into the region on Friday and

  Saturday when heat indices reach the lower to middle 90s.



- There will be a chance of thunderstorms on Saturday afternoon

  through Monday. Strong storms will be possible at times,

  particularly on Saturday afternoon and evening, and possibly on

  Sunday afternoon as well.



- Increasing southerly winds and building waves may result in

  hazardous boating and swimming conditions on Lake Michigan on 

  Saturday.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 236 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Short Term...Tonight and Thursday



A high-pressure system is currently centered over northeast 

Quebec, while a trough of low pressure is situated over the 

Dakotas. Warm advection is taking place over the Plains, and a 

remnant Mesoscale Convective Vortex (MCV) is moving over northeast

Wisconsin. There is widespread cloud cover over the northern 

Mississippi Valley, and reduced visibilities of 6 to 7 miles are

becoming more scattered in nature. Forecast concerns primarily 

involve the potential for thunderstorms, with some discussion 

also dedicated to lingering smoke.



Thunderstorm Potential:  Flattened troughing is expected to move 

across the northern Great Lakes through Thursday afternoon. Weak 

warm advection will continue at the low to mid levels. A moisture 

channel with precipitable water values up to 1.6 inches will move 

into Wisconsin, and a more unstable airmass will follow. Most 

unstable CAPE values are expected to reach up to 1500 J/kg in 

north- central Wisconsin late tonight and 1500 to 2500 J/kg across

the rest of the region on Thursday. Convective allowing models 

show relatively sparse development of thunderstorms tonight, but 

chances may increase after 4 AM in far northern Wisconsin, closer 

to the shortwave energy. On Thursday, additional thunderstorm 

development is possible, mainly over central and east-central 

Wisconsin as a warm front approaches. The confidence in these 

storms is low due to a cap around 6,000 to 8,000 feet and weak 

forcing. Any storms that do develop could produce heavy downpours,

gusty winds, and small hail. The chance for severe weather is low

due to weak deep-layer shear of 20-25 kts.



Smoke:  The latest High-Resolution Rapid Refresh (HRRR) model 

indicates that near-surface smoke and reduced visibility (around 6

statute miles) are possible through the evening before southerly 

winds push the smoke north of the state. But in general, air 

quality seems to be improving judging by improving visibilities 

across the region.



Long Term...Thursday Night Through Wednesday



A fairly typical late-summer weather pattern is expected over the

next week. A shortwave trough moving across the US-Canada border 

and central Canada will bring subtropical ridging into the region 

on Friday and Saturday. This will lead to a warming trend, higher 

humidity, and greater instability. 



Thunderstorm Potential: The leading edge of this unstable airmass

will arrive on Thursday night as a warm front lifts northeast. 

With a modest low-level jet of 25-30 kts and most unstable CAPE of

1000-2000 J/kg, scattered showers and storms are possible in a 

moist airmass with precipitable water values at 140-160% of 

normal. A few strong storms are possible due to deep- layer shear 

up to 25 kts. The Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has placed 

northeast Wisconsin in a marginal risk, with the most favorable 

timing from Thursday afternoon through late Thursday night. 



Most storms are expected to end by Friday morning as a mid- level

cap builds into the region. The next chance for storms will be 

from Saturday afternoon through Sunday as a slow- moving cold 

front crosses the area. An instability axis of 2000-3000 J/kg will

be present ahead of the front, along with high precipitable water

values (150-170% of normal, or around 2 inches) during peak 

heating on Saturday and Sunday. Effective shear will be relatively

weak (around 20 kts), but the combination of high instability and

ample moisture could lead to a threat of wind and flash flooding 

from slow-moving, training storms. 



Another cold front will move into the area around Monday night 

into Tuesday. Instability around 1000-2000 J/kg will bring another

chance for thunderstorms, with the potential for some strong 

storms.



Dangerous Heat: Very warm temperatures and humidity will build

into the region on Friday and Saturday. Heat indices will approach

the middle 90s over central and east-central Wisconsin. While

these values look to fall shy of Heat Advisory criteria, anyone

with prolonged outdoor plans should plan for very warm

temperatures and muggy conditions.



Marine: A brief period of conditions hazardous to small craft will

be possible on Lake Michigan on Saturday and Saturday evening.

Conditions will also be favorable for rip currents, and a Beach

Hazard Statement may be needed.



&&



.AVIATION...for 00Z TAF Issuance

Issued at 634 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Remnant cloud cover from an MCV will continue to track through the

region early this afternoon as the daytime cumulus clouds

dissipate after sunset. 



Weak warm advection will increase late tonight that will likely

lead to the arrival of MVFR stratus into north-central WI late

tonight into Thu morning. Scattered showers and possibly a

thunderstorm will also be possible late tonight into early

Thursday morning as indicated by the CAMs models. Will keep a

PROB30 in at KRHI and KAUW/KCWA as the models dissipate the line

as it heads southeast.



The convective allowing models (CAMS) are showing another round of 

scattered showers and storms moving across central and east- 

central WI late Thu morning and into Thu afternoon. The best

chance appears to be east-central Wisconsin Thursday afternoon;

therefore, will include a PROB30 group in the KGRB/KATW/KMTW TAF

sites.



Patchy ground fog is possible over north-central WI late tonight.

Cloud cover could prohibit fog from forming, but the boundary

layer will remain moist so added a short tempo for MVFR vsbys at

RHI.



&&



.GRB WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION.....MPC

AVIATION.......Kurimski



