Northern Alaska Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Fairbanks AK

335 PM AKDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

The Interior is warm and dry today and tomorrow as a transient

ridge moves through. The Alaska Range will be windy on Thursday as

a front moves overhead. Rain will be gradually moving into the

West Coast and Western Interior tomorrow through Friday, then

become widespread periods of rain this weekend. Widespread rain is

expected to develop over the Brooks Range on Saturday as well, 

then spread to the North Slope on Sunday. Temperatures will be

much cooler than we've been lately across most of Northern Alaska.  



&&



.KEY WEATHER MESSAGES...

Central and Eastern Interior...

- Warm and dry today and tomorrow with highs in the 70s and min

  RHs in the 20s and 30s, lowest in the Tanana Valley and Yukon

  Flats. 

Thursday Hazards:



- Wind Advisory for southerly gusts to 50 mph in Isabel Pass.

- Red Flag Warning for Delta Junction/Isabel Pass for wind gusts

   to 50 mph and RHs as low as 25%. 



- Cooler weather returns this weekend with highs in the 60s and

  increasing chances for rain, especially in the Northern 

  Interior. 



- Isolated to scattered showers in the Southern and Southeastern

  Interior. 



West Coast and Western Interior...

- Warm today and tomorrow, especially in the Western Interior,

  with gradually cooling temps along the Coast. 



- Rain moves into SW Alaska tomorrow then spreads north through

  Friday and into this weekend. 



- Widespread rain expected this weekend with 0.25 to 0.50" in most

  spots. Some areas may exceed 0.50". 



North Slope and Brooks Range..

- Showers in the Brooks Range today and tomorrow will become

  widespread rain this weekend. 



- Mostly dry conditions along the Arctic Coast through Friday with

  rain showers moving in on Saturday and Sunday. 



- Temperatures will be in the 40s and 50s north of the Brooks

  Range, and in the 50s/60s in the Brooks Range. Warmest 

  temperatures will be near Coldfoot 



&&



.FORECAST ANALYSIS AND DISCUSSION...

Transient ridging over the Interior and West Coast is bringing

warm and dry conditions with mostly sunny skies today and

tomorrow. A low over the Alaska Peninsula will bring showers to SW

AK today and tomorrow. This low weakens but a piece of its energy

moves north and interacts with a digging trough over the West

Coast on Saturday. This interaction will cause the trough to turn

negative and undergo cyclogenesis over the Western Interior.

Expect widespread rain over the Western Alaska this weekend. The

Northern Interior, Brooks Range and North Slope will also see 

some rain from this system. In the Southern and SE Interior, 

expect a couple of showers with the highest chance for rain coming

on Sunday as the low slides across Alaska from west to east.

Nonetheless, temperatures will be dropping below normal with highs

widespread in the 50s and 60s this weekend into next week.  



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

The most important thing right now is a Red Flag Advisory in Delta

Junction and Isabel Pass for sustained south winds of 20-30 mph 

with gusts to 50 mph and min RHs around 25% on Thursday. 



Otherwise, warm and dry elsewhere today and tomorrow with highs

in the 70s across the area and min RHs around 35 to 45%. Rain

moves back into the forecast on Friday and Saturday in the Western

Interior, then Sunday in the Central Interior. There is some

uncertainty in the expanse of the rain, but our highest confidence

is in the Northern Central and Eastern Interior seeing rain while

it's a bit more spotty in the Southern Interior. Nonetheless,

temperatures drop into the 50s and 60s this weekend for most of

the Coast and Interior and that persists through most of next 

week.



&&



.HYDROLOGY...

No concerns at this time. 



&&



.EXTENDED FORECAST DAYS 4-7...

Cool and wet weather arriving on Friday will continue through the 

weekend. On Saturday/D4, a trough aloft moving inland from the AK

Peninsula will weaken as it is absorbed into the surrounding 

flow, while a stronger trough/upper low over the Bering Sea 

bringing extensive rainfall to Western Alaska (including the 

Western North Slope) shifts eastward. While the heaviest rain will

likely remain over the West Coast and Western Interior, this 

eastward shift will yield some showers across much of the Central 

and Eastern Interior and North Slope through Sunday. From Monday 

onward, there is lower confidence on features, but as general low 

pressure/troughing moves inland, some heavier rain and showers 

will be possible across the far eastern Interior, with up to a few

tenths of an inch of rainfall. Additional rain is likely on the 

West Coast Tuesday onward as even more troughing moves in from 

over the Bering and Chukchi Seas.



Coastal Hazard Potential Days 3 and 4...None





&&



.AFG WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

AK...Red Flag Warning for AKZ937.

PK...Small Craft Advisory for PKZ811.

     Small Craft Advisory for PKZ850.

     Small Craft Advisory for PKZ851.

     Small Craft Advisory for PKZ854.

     Small Craft Advisory for PKZ857.

&&



$$



Bianco

Satlzman - Extended



