AFDLZK



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Little Rock AR

636 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 1250 AM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



All was quiet across AR early this Wednesday morning. Skies were 

mostly clear, winds were calm or nearly calm, and temperatures 

were in the lower 70s to lower 80s. Sunrise temperatures should be

in the 60s and 70s. These 70s are evidence of a slow warming 

trend now that low temperatures are no longer falling into the 60s

statewide.



Not much new information to add from yesterdays forecast discussion. 

Aloft, an upper ridge situated over portions of the Four Corners 

region will meander Ewrd into the Srn Plains. Here locally, NW flow 

will be in place through at least Friday. Beginning Sunday, an upper 

level trough will move into the Nrn CONUS with the base of the 

trough slowly pivoting across the Cntrl Plains. The main change in 

this forecast compared to 24 hours ago is now the lack of large 

scale lift across portions of Nrn AR. Thus PoP chances have 

decreased accordingly through the weekend/Monday. If rain does 

occur this week/weekend, it will most likely be the result of 

diurnal heating and remain widely scattered in nature. Overall, 

most locations will experience net loss of soil moisture through 

evapotranspiration.



A leeward Rockies surface low is anticipated to spur over the 

plains today, this will switch winds around to a S/SErly direction

across the region. Low level flow should increase through the 

weekend, becoming S/SWrly, in response to a Cntrl Plains deepening

low. Increasing Srly flow will ramp up dew points as well as 

atmospheric precipitable water values. 



Temperatures will continue to moderate through the week into the 

weekend. Today, highs will climb into the upper 80s to mid 90s. 

Thursday through Tuesday, highs should top out in the lower to upper 

90s. Heat index values will approach or exceed 100 degrees across a 

large portion of AR starting Thursday and continue through the 

period. If rain chances do not increase again on Monday and 

Tuesday, then heat headlines could return.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 635 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



High pressure will keep calm and dry conditions across the area

through the period. VFR conditions are expected with light and

variable winds overnight.



&&



