Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Pueblo CO

536 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Isolated thunderstorms (mostly dry) over mainly the mountains into

  early this evening.



- Even hotter Thursday (potentially the hottest day of the year

  so far) with winds increasing. 



- Red Flag Warnings in effect for the the Central Mountains and

  portions of the upper Arkansas Valley Thursday, with Heat 

  Advisories for much of the plains. 

 

- Hot again Friday, with near record temperatures and elevated

  to critical fire weather conditions.  



- Cooler northwest flow pattern with better chances of showers

  and storms for the weekend into next week.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Issued at 201 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Currently, hotter today across the region as high level cloudiness 

has decreased, and a few spots on the plains were at/slightly above 

100f as of 2 pm. High-based convection developing over the mountains 

at mid-afternoon has produced a few cloud to ground lightning 

strikes, though doubtful much rain is reaching the ground given

surface dewpoints in the 20s/low 30s. More concerned about 

lightning/gusty winds and potential for fire starts, especially 

back toward the Continental Divide where fuels remain very dry. 

Tonight, loss of heating will end any convection quickly this 

evening, with clearing skies and decreasing winds by midnight. 



Thursday looks like the hottest day of the season so far, as 

mid/upper level temps remain very warm (18-20C at 700mb), while w-sw 

flow at the surface and aloft increases, leading to deep mixing 

across the region. Nudged up blended guidance temps a couple degf, 

which gets us to record tying levels at COS/PUB, with ALS just a 

degf off. Will note that at least some MOS guidance is 1-3 degf 

hotter than current forecast, though would like to see 700mb temps 

solidly above 20C before going with more extreme values. Current 

heat advisory looks for the El Paso/Fremont/Pueblo counties and 

portions of the sern plains looks on track and didn't make any 

changes. Red Flag Warnings for the central mountains/upper Arkansas 

Valley also look good, with wind/humidity farther south in the San 

Juans just under critical fire thresholds. Another round of high-

based convection possible, mainly over the mountains, though cut

back pops a bit from previous forecast given rather sparse 

reflectivity Thu afternoon in many CAMs.



&&



.LONG TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 201 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Updated the longer term forecast for Friday to add a Heat

Advisory for Pueblo/El Paso/Fremont Counties, where temps remain

within a degf or two of Thursday's readings. Didn't expand heat

highlights on the plains yet, where readings may drift down just

a couple degf below the 105f threshold, though we'll be very 

close. Also added a fire weather watch for the Central Mountains

and San Luis Valley Friday afternoon/evening, where winds will 

be strong enough (gusts over 25 mph) to create critical 

conditions. Considered adding the San Juans to the watch as 

well, though held off at this point as winds are lighter here 

and RH slightly above the 15 percent threshold. Remainder of the

forecast on track with a trend toward cooler weather and 

increasing thunderstorm chances, especially from Sunday into 

next week. 



Previous discussion below...



Thursday night-Friday night...Latest model data continues to indicate 

a modest increase in westerly flow aloft, as a broad upper trough 

translates across the Intermountain West and Northern Rockies through 

the day on Friday. Models continue to support drier air within the 

westerly flow aloft moving into the Central Rockies on Friday, with 

only a few high based showers and storms expected across the higher 

terrain Friday afternoon and evening. The increasing westerly flow and 

lee troughing across the plains will bring breezy westerly winds of 

15 to 30 mph across the area Friday afternoon, strongest across the 

higher terrain, where critical fire weather conditions may be met once 

again across the Central Mtns and into the San Luis Valley. The downslope 

flow will also help warm temperatures into the 90s and lower 100s across 

the plains, with highs in the 60s to lower 80s across the higher terrain 

on Friday. With the well above seasonal, near record temperatures once 

again on Friday, will need to monitor for the potential for another heat 

advisory for portions of the plains.    



Friday night-Tuesday...Models remain consistent with a pattern change 

as passing Upper Midwest wave Friday night helps to develop a cooler

northwest flow pattern into the weekend, as upper level ridging builds

back across the Desert Southwest and into the Great Basin through early 

next week. NBM data supports this cooler (temperatures back to around 

seasonal levels) and wetter pattern, mainly across the eastern mountains 

into the southeast Plains where the best available moisture will be in 

place. Further west, ensemble data supports drier air within the flow 

keeping temperatures at to slightly above normal, and very little chance

of measurable precipitation.  



Wednesday and beyond...Upper ridging starts to flatten across the Rockies 

once again, with temperatures warming back to above seasonal levels area

wide. There is some support within the ensemble means of increasing 

available moisture into late next week, which would bring better 

chances of daily showers and storms, especially over and near the higher

terrain. Time will tell.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 536 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions are expected at KALS, KCOS, and KPUB through 24 

hours. 



KALS: Showers and virga in the area will keep intermittent gusts 25-

30 knots for another few hours. Winds will weaken overnight before 

turning westerly again Thursday afternoon.



KCOS: Showers in the area may bring intermittent outflow around 35 

knots through 02Z or so. Chances for precipitation at the terminal 

is low. Winds lighten overnight as mid-level clouds linger, with 

gusty southwest winds setting in Thursday afternoon.



KPUB: An outflow boundary is moving in from the east, and should 

impact the terminal between 01-03Z. Gusts will be intermittent from 

the east around 30 knots, but should not last long. Winds turn more 

westerly overnight with increasing mid-level clouds. For Thursday 

afternoon, gusty west winds will return to the area. 



There is another chance for scattered showers across southeast 

Colorado Thursday afternoon and evening, but current chances for 

precipitation at the terminals are too low to include in this round 

of TAFs.



