=================================================



023

FXUS63 KOAX 062338

AFDOAX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Omaha/Valley NE

638 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Showers and thunderstorms will be possible overnight into

  Thursday morning (30% chance). Severe weather potential

  remains low, though locally heavy rainfall will be possible.



- Heat returns Thursday and Friday with highs expected to reach

  into the 90s with heat index values of 100 to 105.



- Precipitation chances return Friday into the weekend, with

  periods of strong to severe storms possible.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 244 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Today and Tomorrow...



The broad-scale synoptic pattern this afternoon features a mid- to 

upper-level ridge centered over New Mexico, with a trough pushing 

onshore across the Pacific Northwest. An over-performing MCS moved 

through the area this morning, leaving lingering cloud cover

into the early afternoon, keeping temperatures a few degrees 

cooler than expected, with highs this afternoon reaching the low

to mid-80s.



Scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected to develop 

overnight into Thursday morning with varying solutions in coverage 

from CAMs. Both the HRRR and NAM Nest develop isolated to scattered 

convection along the Missouri River, while the NSSL-WRF appears to 

be the outlier in initiating yet another large MCS. With the lack of 

any strong wave approaching, the bulk of the convection looks to be 

fired along the nose of a 35-45 kt southwesterly LLJ. The extent of 

the activity should remain near and east of the Missouri River, 

where PoPs range from 15-30%. Low confidence is present in storm 

coverage for areas west of the Missouri River. The severe weather 

potential remains low given bulk shear generally under 20 kts, 

though a few stronger updrafts could produce gusty winds or small 

hail. The SPC maintains a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) for severe 

weather. With long, skinny CAPE profiles yielding around 1000-1250 

J/kg of MLCAPE and PWAT values near 1.5", efficient rainfall 

processes and locally heavy downpours will be possible, but the 

progressive and scattered nature of the storms should help to limit 

flooding concerns. However, this will still be something to monitor 

given the saturated conditions across much of the area. 



By late Thursday morning, showers should diminish, allowing sunshine 

to return and temperatures to climb. As the ridge expands into the 

central Plains, highs Thursday afternoon are expected to reach the 

upper 80s to low 90s. Dewpoints in the low 70s will push Heat Index 

values into the 95 to 103 degree range, creating an uncomfortable 

and humid air mass.



Friday...



By the end of the work week, the mid-level trough currently over the 

Pacific Northwest will progress eastward into the northern Plains. 

This system is expected to spin up a surface low tracking across the 

Dakotas/Nebraska and trailing a cold front to its south. Ahead of 

this system, afternoon highs will climb into the low to mid-90s, 

with heat index values of 101 to 105 degrees and isolated spots 

potentially exceeding 105. A Heat Advisory may be needed for a 

portion of the area in the coming days. 



Severe weather potential returns Friday as the frontal boundary 

pushes into the region. Model soundings indicate a moderately 

unstable but capped airmass; if the cap breaks, more isolated strong 

to severe storms could develop into the evening. The SPC currently 

highlights northeast NE under a slight risk (level 2 of 5) for 

severe weather and the remainder of the area under a marginal risk 

(level 1 of 5). Current PoPs peak near 30%.



Saturday and Beyond...



Saturday could bring another round of strong to severe storms as the 

mid-level trough pushes east across the northern Plains and the 

previously mentioned cold front settles over the region. Highs will 

likely ease back into the upper 80s to low 90s, with exact 

temperatures depending on how far south the front advances before 

peak daytime heating. Long-range guidance suggests a narrow corridor 

of steep lapse rates and moderate instability ahead of the boundary, 

supporting the potential for strong to severe storm development

along it. However, confidence is timing and severity of any 

storms remain low in this extended time frame. PoPs currently 

peak at 30-50%.



A similar setup is possible on Sunday as the front pushes near the 

southern edge of the CWA. Highs will again range from the mid to 

upper 80s behind the front, while another unstable airmass develops 

ahead of it. The primary uncertainty is whether the front will push 

far enough south to exit the CWA or linger across its southern half. 

PoPs are currently forecast at 40-60%. Several GEFS-based machine 

learning guidance highlights the area with a 5-15% probability of 

severe weather both Saturday and Sunday. 



Light PoPs (around 30%) may persist into Monday as the boundary 

remains nearby, helping to hold highs in the low to mid-80s. Zonal 

to northwesterly flow aloft will then continue into the first half 

of next week as the mid-level ridge shifts into the northeastern 

CONUS, with highs generally remaining in the 80s with occasional 

precipitation chances.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 638 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions favored through much of the period with passing

clouds around 3500-6000 ft agl overnight. Guidance is trending

toward shower and storm development near the MO river somewhere

around 5-7Z and lingering through around 12Z. Highest chances

appear to be at OMA, so included TEMPO mention with MVFR for 

now, but may need to adjust timing and also eventually may need 

a mention at LNK. Otherwise, expect some low level wind shear at

LNK with 40 kt southerly winds at 2000 ft agl. Surface winds

will remain southeasterly to southerly on either side of 10 kts.



&&



.OAX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

NE...None.

IA...None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Wood

AVIATION...CA



