2025-08-06T23-40-37-095Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8563","issue":"2025-08-06T23:40:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS64","cccc":"KEPZ","awipsid":"AFDEPZ"}]
=================================================



046

FXUS64 KEPZ 062340

AFDEPZ



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service El Paso TX/Santa Teresa NM

540 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 534 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



-  Dry and hot weather pattern through the rest of the week, with

   triple digit lowland high temperatures. Heat Advisories remain

   in effect for El Paso and lower Rio Grande valley through 

   Saturday.



-  Scattered showers and thunderstorms returning next week,

   focused mainly over area mountain and western New Mexico. 



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 1100 AM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



600 dam upper high remains fixed over New Mexico today. The

complete cutoff of monsoonal flow and local precipitable water 

values around 0.8" will result in dry conditions area wide and 

only a stray weak shower or two over the high terrain this 

afternoon. Temperatures in the desert lowland will climb above the

triple-digit mark, reaching 104-108 degrees along the I-10

corridor. Heat Advisory is in effect for El Paso, Las Cruces,

Deming, and Lordsburg zones through sunset tonight.



No major changes in the weather pattern through Saturday. Upper

high weakens slightly, but remains fixed over New Mexico the rest

of the week. Lack of available moisture will result in only

isolated showers and thunderstorms over the mountain zones,

remaining dry for most lowland locations. Triple-digit lowland

highs continue. El Paso will still need Heat Advisories through

Saturday with highs 102-106 degrees. 



Mid-latitude trough will fully erode the upper high by early next

week, resulting in more normal moisture values (PW 1.1-1.3") and

better thunderstorm coverage starting on Sunday. Scattered (30-50%

coverage) thunderstorms over area mountains and isolated (10-30%

coverage) across the desert lowlands will be most common, with

localized flooding and gusty outflow winds being the primary

hazards. Forecast QPF favors mountains and western NM next week,

with lesser chances for the Rio Grande and far west Texas areas.

Temperatures will also cool closer to seasonal normals next week,

with lowland highs in the upper 90s.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 534 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions persist through the period with calm winds

overnight and light afternoon breezes out of the south tomorrow.

Storms and showers look to stay confined to the Sacramento Mtns 

and Gila region the remainder of tonight before fizzling out later

this evening but not expecting storms at terminals. 



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 1100 AM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Elevated fire danger through the rest of the week due to recent 

dryness and above normal temperatures. Very low storm coverage 

next few days, limited to the high terrain and minimal flood risk.

Min RH 15-25% next few days, with light west winds resulting in 

Good ventilation.



Thunderstorms chances increasing again early next week as the

upper high erodes and allows for the return of better moisture.

Coverage will be scattered (30-50%) over LNF/GNF and area

mountains, with localized flooding possible. Gusty outflow winds

near storms may impact fire planning.



&&



