=================================================



060

FXUS63 KBIS 062341

AFDBIS



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Bismarck ND

641 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Scattered to numerous severe storms are possible Thursday

  afternoon and evening, with the possibility of significant 

  hail, damaging winds, and a few tornados. Another threat for 

  severe weather (similar hazards) is expected across eastern 

  North Dakota on Friday. 



- Hot and humid conditions will impact North Dakota Thursday,

  with heat indices forecast to approach 100 degrees across the

  south.



- Cooler and less humid weather can be expected Friday and

  through this coming weekend.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 640 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Weak reflectivities have started to develop along a weak 

inverted surface trough in far southwestern ND as the cap

continues to erode. With a moderate convective environment 

here, with MLCAPE values around 1500 J/KG and good bulk shear 

values around 35-45 knots, we will continue to monitor possible 

storm development this evening, though the late dissipation of 

the cap and weak forcing is expected to help damped some of this

potential. For this update, have added patchy fog in the 

southern James River Valley late tonight through early Thursday 

morning. Otherwise, the forecast remains broadly on track. 



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 300 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Currently, low amplitude upper level ridge building into the

High Plains region with a mid level low over south central

Canada. Resultant flow across the Dakotas is zonal, with weak

surface high pressure over our region. Resultant weather is a 

rarity for our region (at least recently), with a mostly sunny 

sky across western and central North Dakota this afternoon. 



There remains a possibility for a few showers and thunderstorms

over southwest North Dakota later this afternoon and evening,

where CAMs want to generate some reflectivity near a sfc 

boundary across southeastern Montana. 



Main highlight in the forecast period will be heat and the 

severe weather potential on Thursday. Aforementioned upper ridge

continues to amplify and will develop east across the Northern 

Plains Thu morning, with southwest flow aloft overspreading the 

Dakotas by Thu afternoon. This is in response to a vigorous 

upper level wave moving through the Pacific NW and into the 

Northern Rockies during the day Thu. The above mentioned sfc 

boundary now to our southwest will also be advancing east across

the Dakotas during the day Thursday, allowing for WAA and 

moisture advection into the state, with forecast highs tomorrow 

from the 80s north to lower 90s south, and sfc dewpoint 

temperatures in the mid 60s to mid 70s. Heat indices south 

central Thu afternoon will approach 100 degrees (heat advisory 

criteria) with the warm and humid air. Opted to not issue heat 

headlines at this time and let the mid shift take a closer look.

Regardless, it will be a sultry Thursday most areas. 



Second and more impactful concern for Thursday is the elevated 

severe weather potential. Ample instability (MUCAPE greater than

4K J/KG), bulk shear of 40-55kts, coupled with a mid level lead

S/WV impulse and the passage of both a warm and cold front, 

will all lead to an active and impactful weather event across 

the Northern Plains Thu afternoon and evening. CAMs are still 

fluctuating, so won't get into the weeds with details, other 

than we're expecting all modes of storms (depending on when they

form) and all hazards (large hail, damaging winds, heavy rain, 

a few tornados). Storms are likely to initiate early to mid 

afternoon, and continue into the evening, with all of western 

and central ND under this threat. SPC did upgrade our southern 

two thirds to an Enhanced Risk for severe weather. 



For Friday, aforementioned upper low will continue east across

the Rockies, before moving east-northeast into far south central

Canada Fri night. Depending on the frontal boundary placement on

Friday, there will be another elevated risk for severe weather

along and east of this boundary. Currently, the southern James

River Valley looks to see the best chance, but this could shift

either west or east. In the wake of said sfc boundary, cooler

and less humid air will move into western and much of central

North Dakota Fri afternoon. With cooler (highs 70-80) and less 

humid conditions (sfc dewpoints in the 50s) persisting through 

this coming weekend.



The upper low will remain quasi-stationary across far south

central Canada through the weekend, and will generate chances

for showers and a few thunderstorms across the north both

Saturday and to some extent Sunday (starts to move farther

east). 



Next week, models favor a more zonal flow pattern overall,

supporting a gradual warming trend and only slight/spotty

chances for precipitation at this time.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 640 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Mainly VFR ceilings and visibility is anticipated throughout 

the 00Z TAF period. Late tonight into early Thursday morning, 

chances for showers and a few thunderstorms increase across the 

southwest. Have opted to include a PROB30 group for -SHRA at 

KDIK with this update. Later in the morning through the 

afternoon, a more widespread round of showers and scattered 

thunderstorms is expected to move into the northwest and move 

eastward. With this update, have added prevailing rain at KXWA 

and KMOT, and a PROB30 group for -TSRA in the afternoon at KDIK 

when the confidence is highest. Some thunderstorms with this 

initial round may become strong to severe. Chances for showers 

and thunderstorms will expand across much of the forecast area 

through the late afternoon, becoming widespread by the end of 

the TAF period. Have included a PROB30 group for -SHRA at KBIS 

with this update. Beyond the end of the TAF period, numerous 

strong to severe thunderstorms are anticipated across much of 

western and central North Dakota. The main hazards anticipated 

with any severe thunderstorm would be very large hail and very 

damaging wind gusts. A few tornadoes are also possible tomorrow.

MVFR ceilings and visibility are anticipated with any 

thunderstorm that does develop. Patchy fog is also possible 

Thursday morning across portions of the far southern James 

River Valley, though confidence is too low to mention at any 

given TAF site at this time. Winds this evening are expected to 

continue diminishing, backing to become south southeasterly by 

Thursday morning. Winds are expected to become somewhat gusty 

through the late morning and afternoon, with speeds up to 15 

knots and gust up to 25 knots. Where thunderstorms do develop, 

gusty and erratic winds should be anticipated. 



&&



.BIS WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



UPDATE...Adam

DISCUSSION...NH

AVIATION...Adam



