[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8589","issue":"2025-08-06T23:41:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KDMX","awipsid":"AFDDMX"}]
=================================================



074

FXUS63 KDMX 062341

AFDDMX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Des Moines IA

641 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



 ...Updated for the 00z Aviation Discussion...



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Marginal Risk for severe weather tonight into Thursday

  morning. Main severe threat will be after midnight. Damaging

  winds are the main concern.



- Heat indices near 100 degrees possible on Friday (60%

  confidence).



- Thunderstorms possible (~40% chance) Saturday morning, then

  becoming likely after sunset Saturday night (>75% chance).

  Rain will remain in the forecast through at least Tuesday.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 157 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Complex setup in the short term. Objective analysis shows a passing 

shortwave across northern Iowa, this being a culprit for the MCS 

this morning. Another feature of note is a vorticity max at the apex 

of the upper ridge, seen better by a lee cyclone, or by the wave 

feature seen at 700mb. This feature will make its way towards Iowa 

through the day, with more certainty placed towards having 

consequential CI tonight. Convection will take place along the 

gradient of the EML with uncertainty in how much the EML will 

retreat in the wake of the MCS. Highest confidence resides along and 

north of Highway 20. A southward traversal is possible along the 

CAPE gradient. Similar to last night, backbuilding will be possible 

if this happens because of the cold pool orienting Northwest to 

Southeast. Locally heavy rainfall exceeding 2 inches will be 

possible in the northeast.



A trough will eject off the coast and across the PNW, but not before 

ridging will be allowed to advance into the region Thursday and 

Friday. Friday looks to be the warmest and driest day for the 

next 5 days. Highs will be in the low 90s and building humidity 

will push the heat indices near the 100 degree mark. That same 

trough will close off and tilt negatively, the upper-level jet 

rounding the trough base. The main complex will move east 

towards Iowa late Friday night into Saturday with the 

possibility of storms diving towards the state in the morning. 

The Marginal Risk over the area covers the damaging wind 

potential, given that the region of favorable shear will follow 

the storms. The better deep-layer shear orients west-to-east 

Saturday as the front pivots and stalls. Due to capping, the 

front may not initiate until after dark when the LLJ converges. 

There will also be over 2,000 J/kg of CAPE to work with, making 

this setup favorable for hail and damaging winds. 2 inch PWATs 

will pool along the boundary, making heavy rain possible as 

well.



When looking at the long term, an upper ridge in the eastern Pacific 

will continue to build northward. What this means is that the 

northern stream of energy we've been seeing will continue to be 

favored. The CONUS upper ridge will be contained in the Rockies. 

That means the EML will be kept in the Central Plains and Southwest 

US. That ridge may be held there until late next week. Until we get 

there, the longwave trough from the weekend will be slow to vacate, 

with an extension of it lagging back into the CONUS late this 

weekend. Then more northern stream waves will replace the 

aforementioned trough into next week. While discrepancies exist on 

what sort of intensity of synoptic lift will reach Iowa, the mean 

flow at 500mb is northwest through at least midweek. Pattern 

recognition would say there will be subtle shortwaves that will 

propagate along the flow, energizing the stalled front that 

will bounce around the nearby region. This is why >40% chance of

rain can be found in the overnight hours from Friday to 

Tuesday. There will be heavy rainfall wherever the front ignites

each night. It's worth monitoring the forecast for trends on 

precipitation placement into next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 641 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Areas of cumulus are expected to fade through the evening, with

otherwise VFR conditions are expected to remain through the

period. The potential remains for storms to move into northern 

Iowa into Thursday morning, though confidence on exact timing 

and area of development remains low, therefore keeping PROB30 

mentions at only the northern terminals. Close monitoring will 

remain over the next several hours, with adjustments made as 

needed. A low signal for patchy fog leading to slight visibility

reductions is present from model guidance that could impact 

most sites Thursday morning, so will be monitoring how 

conditions set up into the early morning hours.



&&



.DMX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Jimenez

AVIATION...Bury



