2025-08-06T23-42-43-070Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8599","issue":"2025-08-06T23:42:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KBTV","awipsid":"AFDBTV"}]
=================================================



084

FXUS61 KBTV 062342

AFDBTV



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Burlington VT

742 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure remains the dominant feature this week. Isolated

to scattered showers and thunderstorms may deliver brief 

downpours near mountain ridges over the next couple of days, but

much of the area will continue to see dry weather. Hazy 

conditions will continue over the next couple of days as well, 

with some gradual improvement expected. Temperatures will be 

near to slightly above normal through mid week, then we'll see a

warming trend through the weekend.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

As of 145 AM EDT Wednesday...Today and Thursday will be a near rinse

and repeat of yesterday as we remain under the influence of high

pressure. A weak surface trough/boundary will waver across the area

over the next couple of days, and this along with diurnal

heating/instability will allow isolated to scattered showers and

thunderstorms to develop each afternoon, particularly over the

higher terrain. Like yesterday, flow is weak, so any

showers/thunderstorms will be slow moving. Both moisture and forcing

are relatively meager, so the threat of any flooding or severe

weather is very low. However, heavy downpours will be possible due

to slow movement of storm cells. Highs will be similar both today

and Thursday, in the upper 70s to low/mid 80s. Dewpoints should

remain fairly comfortable in the upper 50s to low/mid 60s, and

tonight's lows will be in the mid 50s to mid 60s.



Unfortunately, wildfire smoke will continue to linger across the

region over the next couple of days, as well. The aforementioned

surface boundary remains to our south today, and this has allowed

improved air quality across northern areas for the time being.

However, with high pressure overhead and weak flow, expect smoke

will meander across the region both today and Thursday, though

concentrations should remain highest across central and southern

areas. Rutland, Windsor, Orange, and Addison Counties remain under

an Air Quality Alert through midnight Wednesday night.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...

As of 145 AM EDT Wednesday...Thursday night is expected to be quiet

and dry as daytime heating and instability end. Temperatures are

anticipated to drop into the lower 50s to mid 60s by the end of the

night. Skies will likely be a mix of partly and mostly clear. Winds

will be mainly light but just persistent enough aloft out of the

south/southwest to make valley radiation fog forecasting tricky.

Thinking chances of fog are fairly good in valley areas that get

clearer skies and lighter winds.



South/southwesterly flow will help propel temperatures above normal

on Friday with highs in the 80s, and we are forecasting one more

afternoon of isolated diurnally driven and terrain driven showers

and thunderstorms that day, about 15-25% chance of measurable

precipitation for most.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

As of 145 AM EDT Wednesday...Strong upper level ridging is expected

to build across the region towards the weekend, which will continue

to bring a period of dry weather through early next week. The

anticipated warming temperature trend continues to look likely with

highs climbing from the 80s Saturday into the upper 80s/mid 90s by

Monday. Overnight lows are forecast to be in the mid 50s to lower

70s Friday night onwards.



Midweek next week, southwesterly return flow will continue to keep

temperatures in the 80s and lower 90s each day. Dew points will also

be on an upwards trend toward midweek, making the heat feel even

muggier. Precipitation chances will increase as well towards midweek

ahead of an approaching frontal boundary, but there remains a lot of

variability on how this feature impacts our forecast area. Currently

have 30-45% chance of precipitation Tuesday night/Wednesday.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Through 00Z Friday...VFR conditions should persist for much of the 

evening before patchy fog develops later in the night. It will 

likely reach MPV for some time while it is possible at SLK. 

Probabilities of fog at MPV are maybe 75 percent while they are 

maybe 40 percent at SLK, though the fog may go in and out a few 

times at SLK if it does form. Some low stratus is also possible

at MPV unrelated to the fog. Any fog lifts not long after 

sunrise and VFR conditions should prevail into the early 

afternoon. Winds should remain under 10 KTs, changing from 

variable and terrain driven tonight to southerly/southwesterly 

tomorrow.



Outlook...



Thursday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Friday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Friday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Saturday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Saturday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Sunday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Sunday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Monday: VFR. NO SIG WX.



&&



.EQUIPMENT...

KCXX will be down until at least Thursday afternoon due to a

power supply failure. The part has been ordered and should

arrive by late Thursday morning. Our outstanding ET staff is hoping

to have the part installed and the radar working by mid

afternoon on Thursday if all goes well.



&&



.BTV WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

VT...None.

NY...None.



&&



$$

SYNOPSIS...Hastings

NEAR TERM...Hastings

SHORT TERM...Storm

LONG TERM...Storm

AVIATION...Myskowski

EQUIPMENT...WFO BTV



