[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8776","issue":"2025-08-06T23:44:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KDLH","awipsid":"AFDDLH"}]
=================================================



261

FXUS63 KDLH 062344

AFDDLH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Duluth MN

644 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- On and off storm chances continue into the weekend.



- North central Minnesota has the highest severe weather chances

  Thursday night and Friday night. East-central Minnesota and

  northwest Wisconsin severe storm chances for Saturday daytime.



- Temperatures will continue to increase through Friday. Heat

  Advisories and limited coverage Extreme Heat Warnings may be 

  needed.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 228 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A weak vorticity lobe crosses over the Borderlands this evening

and keeps a limited thunderstorm chance in the forecast into

tonight. While small hail and gusty winds to 40 mph are possible

in the Iron Range northward this evening, severe weather is no

longer expected.



A nose of warm air sets up in north-central Minnesota Thursday 

afternoon as a ridge axis slides overhead. Minor to Moderate 

Heat Impacts are expected Thursday afternoon in parts of north- 

central Minnesota and maybe into northeast Minnesota tomorrow. A

limited-coverage Heat Advisory may be needed for zones in 

Brainerd Lakes northward to Koochiching County tomorrow. A low- 

level jet builds into North Dakota Thursday afternoon to create 

increasing severe thunderstorm chances moving into northwest 

Minnesota late Thursday evening. The likely mesoscale convective

complex in western Minnesota tomorrow evening may (10% chance) 

move into the Leech Lake to Brainerd Lakes region after 10 PM 

and last into the overnight hours. While gusty winds of 30-50 

mph are somewhat likely (30-50% chance) under and behind the

complex, the environment for supporting any severe weather (60+

mph) could be hard to sustain a few hours after sunset as the 

nocturnal and low-level warm layer inversion set up in the 

Northland.



The heat further increases for east-central Minnesota and inland

northwest Wisconsin Friday as deep moisture advection from the

south-southeast ramps up. While some lingering sky cover and

morning precipitation from the Thursday night mesoscale complex

could inhibit some warming for parts of northern Minnesota,

there remains higher chances for heat impacts in Pine County

eastward into Wisconsin. For this combo of air temperatures

forecast around 90 F and dewpoints around 70 F creating a Heat 

Index scraping 100 F and the resultant heat impacts that are

possible, an Extreme Heat Watch was issued Friday Noon to 9 PM

for limited counties.



Accompanying the heat is a large pool of instability that will

build over the Northland. Forecast model soundings show this

nose of warm air creating a stout stable layer from 875 mb to

700 mb which is expected to largely inhibit convection Friday

afternoon. The uncertainty though in the Friday evening severe

situation grows though as a low to mid-level boundary advances

into the MN/ND stateline region. This boundary in a primed

environment is likely to allow convective initiation at least

for eastern ND into parts of northwest Minnesota. Even though

storm initiation is expected there, whether any can progress

into the Northland (mainly north-central Minnesota) as strong to

severe is highly uncertain at this point. If storms do enter

north-central Minnesota as strong to severe, timing is expected

late evening (onset around 8-10 PM) and continuing eastward from

the Brainerd Lakes to Borderlands through early Saturday

morning. At this point the most likely hazards would be wind

gusts to 60 mph and large hail.



By Saturday late morning, convective initiation is a bit more

likely in the east-central Minnesota to Twin Ports to most of

northwest Wisconsin region with the boundary now into central

Minnesota. Thunderstorm chances for this second round of storms

Saturday lasts into Saturday afternoon before decreasing in the

evening hours with the frontal boundary then into central

Wisconsin. Pending Friday night convection, Minor to Moderate

heat impacts may linger Saturday for northwest Wisconsin.



After a seasonable temperature and mainly dry Sunday, the next

system drops southeastward from the Southern Canadian Prairies

to create another round of thunderstorms early next week. The

potential for more smoke transport will also have to be

monitored for Monday and Tuesday next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 643 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions for the moment, but fog and low stratus are

expected to develop later tonight with light winds and

increasing moisture overnight. Expect MVFR/IFR visibilities and

ceilings at times starting around 06Z and ending around 14Z.

There is a small chance for showers this evening, which could

affect INL in the next few hours as weak shortwaves pass by to

the north (20% chance). There could be some redevelopment of 

showers around HYR later this evening (30% chance). While

thunder can't be completely ruled out, thunder potential seems

to be decreasing (<10% chance). Low clouds and fog are expected

to scatter out Thursday morning, leading to VFR conditions the

rest of the period with light southerly winds.



&&



.MARINE /FOR NEAR SHORE WATERS OF WESTERN LAKE SUPERIOR/...

Issued at 228 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Easterly winds around 10 knots persist into tonight with light

rain showers in the far North Shore. Isolated thunderstorms

tonight may bring a lightning strike hazard. Light southeast

winds Thursday except southwesterly funneling between Grand

Portage and Isle Royale. Easterly winds return again Friday and

build waves back into the Twin Ports. There is a low-end threat

of severe weather late Friday night.



For the open water discussion, refer to the NWS Marquette Area

Forecast Discussion at weather.gov/mqt.



&&



.DLH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

MN...Extreme Heat Watch from Friday afternoon through Friday 

     evening for MNZ038.

WI...Extreme Heat Watch from Friday afternoon through Friday 

     evening for WIZ002-003-006>008.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...NLy

AVIATION...JDS

MARINE...NLy



