=================================================



354

FXUS63 KMQT 062346

AFDMQT



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Marquette MI

746 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Smoke from Canadian wildfires will continue impacting air

  quality and may locally reduce visibility at times the rest of

  today through this evening. An Air Quality Advisory remains in

  effect for all of Upper Michigan until midnight tonight.



- Episodic showers and thunderstorms will be possible again

  tonight through the weekend.



- Heat and humidity increase late this week into the weekend,

  with a 40-70% chance of heat indices exceeding 90 degrees

  Saturday over most areas and 90%+ chance for areas in the

  lower elevations near Lake Superior.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

Issued at 321 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



As the remnants of an MCS bring mid to upper level cloud cover over 

the western half of the U.P. this afternoon, diurnal cu is 

continuing to develop over the portions of the U.P. as seen on 

satellite imagery. While radar returns are being seen over the far 

west early this afternoon from the Duluth and Marquette radars, the 

latest sfc observations show that none of the precipitation is 

hitting the ground, even though RHs are currently in the 70s percent 

range. In addition, haze and sfc smoke from the wildfires in Canada 

are still impacting the air quality in the U.P., with Purple Air 

sensors across the west and central still being mainly in the 

Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups (USG, Orange AQI) range. However, we 

are seeing some improvement to air quality in the eastern U.P., with 

sensors showing the area from Seney eastward having already improved 

to Moderate (Yellow AQI). As high pressure ridging over the area 

continues to retreat to the east as the parent high moves to New 

England tonight to Thursday, more south to southwesterly flow 

developing over that same time period will begin to push the smoke 

out of area and replace it with much better air (as seen on the 

Purple Air sensors over Iowa and Minnesota right now). Thus, the Air 

Quality Advisory from Michigan's Environment, Great Lakes, and 

Energy department is still continuing until midnight tonight. 



We could see some showers and storms redevelop behind the virga over 

the western U.P. this evening and bring rainfall to the west early 

tonight, before continuing eastward into the central and east by 

Thursday morning. While not too much rainfall is expected with this 

round, it may be enough to wet (around 0.10 of an inch or more) the 

ground across scattered portions of the U.P. this evening through 

Thursday. Given the lack of shearing and energy, no severe weather 

is expected tonight through Thursday. 



With upper-level cloud cover and smoke hanging out across the area 

today, high temperatures are expected to be about what they were 

yesterday; the upper 70s to lower 80s across most of the area. With 

the cloud cover and potential rain chances tonight, lows look to 

generally be in the lower to mid 60s. As a warmer and more humid 

airmass begins to advect into the region on Thursday, expect the 

temperatures to increase (especially with the lack of smoke over the 

area); wouldn't be surprised if most of the area got into the 80s, 

with some spots in the downslopes near Lake Superior and in the 

interior west potentially flirting with 90F.



&&



.LONG TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 321 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



More active weather and very warm and humid conditions are in store 

for us as we move into this weekend, with south to southwesterly 

flow pumping Gulf air (and Iowa corn-sweat) towards us. As the U.P. 

remains settled between a low pressure lifting from the Plains into 

the Canadian Prairies and a high pressure moving over the Atlantic, 

shortwaves are projected to cycle through the region from the end of 

this week through potentially even mid-next week. Thus, we could see 

showers and storms across all of or at least portions of the U.P. 

from Thursday night onwards throughout the extended period, save for 

during the day Friday when guidance is somewhat agreeing on a break 

from precipitation chances. As high CAPE air builds into the region 

late this week, we could see some strong thunderstorms develop over 

the western U.P. by Saturday as some shearing up to 30 knots is 

possible. However, given the lack of shear over the central and 

eastern U.P., severe weather potential seems pretty limited at this 

time, save for maybe some 'popcorn' convection that has core 

collapses (strong winds and hail possible). As cooler air begins to 

advect into the area Sunday, expect thunderstorm chances to 

decrease. However, given the marginal instability and modest forcing 

continuing over the area, thunder chances look to remain through at 

least early next week. 



Given the increase in temperatures and dewpoints Friday and 

Saturday, expect the Heat Index to climb into the 90s across the 

area those days, with the worst 'heat' day expected Saturday. The 

LREF still shows a 40 to 70% for heat indices exceeding 90 degrees 

across mainly the interior areas on Saturday; I'm thinking it's 

about a 90+% chance that we will see heat indices in the 90s in the 

southerly-wind downslope areas near Lake Superior Saturday 

afternoon. While the LREF shows less than a 10% chance for a max 

Heat Index of 100 or more on Saturday, be sure to get in plenty of 

water throughout the day as you will likely sweat a lot more than on 

a typical summer day UP here. As cooler air begins advecting into 

the area Sunday, expect the temperatures and dewpoints to decrease, 

bringing some needed relief to the area. However, we can still 

expect highs in the lower 80s for early next week as strong lapse 

rates develop in the lower levels of the atmosphere.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 746 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Low-end VFR HZ from Canadian wildfires gradually lifts out from the 

UP through Thu morning. SHRA/-TSRA are anticipated tonight, bringing 

periods of MVFR vis to the sites from W to E into early Thu morning. 

In the wake of precip, some MVFR cigs are anticipated before VFR 

conditions return. 



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 321 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Light winds of 20 knots or less hold across Lake Superior the rest 

of the work-week as remnant high pressure ridging retreats and gives 

way to a more zonal pattern aloft. As a shortwave low moves through 

the Upper Great Lakes on Saturday, the winds could potentially 

increase from the south up to 25 knots before dropping back down to 

20 knots or less again early next week. 



As for thunder chances, some showers and storms could move into the 

western lake this evening before scooting into the eastern half by 

Thursday. The shower and storm chances continue over the eastern 

lake until Friday, when a quick reprieve looks to be in order. Once 

the shortwave lifting from the Plains moves just west of the region 

on Saturday, expect showers and storms to return late Friday night 

through Saturday, with some of the storms potentially being strong 

over the western lake. Thunder chances return over the eastern lake 

Sunday, with low (~20%) storm chances continuing across the lake 

through early next week.



&&



.MQT WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Upper Michigan...

None.

Lake Superior...

None.

Lake Michigan...

None.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...TAP

LONG TERM...TAP

AVIATION...Jablonski

MARINE...TAP



