364

FXUS66 KMFR 062347

AFDMFR



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Medford OR

447 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...Updated AVIATION discussion for 00Z TAFs...



.DISCUSSION...Upper level troughing continues to dominate our

weather today and will do so through Thursday. An upper level 

trough is moving southward out of British Columbia and will swing 

through Washington State on Thursday. This pattern is bringing 

cloud cover to the region, most concentrated north of the Rogue- 

Umpqua Divide and along the coast. A weak front passed through the

area this morning. Most areas were dry, but a few clicks were 

registered along the coast north of Coos Bay. Weak instability 

behind that front is maintaining light shower activity in the 

Umpqua Basin and along the coast this afternoon. We don't expect 

much accumulation from these showers, and shower activity should 

wind down after sunset. Otherwise, mostly sunny skies are present 

for the rest of the area with another afternoon of below normal 

temperatures expected today.



Conditions on Thursday will be quite similar to those of today. 

Temperatures will again be below normal, and some spotty shower 

activity is possible along the coast and in the Umpqua Basin. There 

will be a bit of an uptick in afternoon breezes as the main trough 

exits to the east Thursday afternoon. Meanwhile, a thermal trough 

will develop along the coast Thursday night in the wake of the upper 

trough departure. This will mark the start of a significant pattern 

change that will continue through the weekend and into early next 

week as high pressure over the eastern Pacific nudges closer to the 

Pacific Northwest.



Dry conditions are expected for the remainder of the forecast period 

after Thursday, and temperatures will trend warmer on Friday 

reaching values more typical for this time of year. The more notable 

warm up will be along the coast south of Cape Blanco where the 

Chetco Effect will be in full swing. We expect hot temperatures in 

the Brookings/Gold Beach area where current forecasts show low to 

mid 80s, with upper 80s certainly in the realm of possibilities. The 

Chetco Effect is expected to persist into Saturday, maintaining hot 

temperatures along the coast south of Cape Blanco. For inland areas, 

temperatures will trend warmer by about 5 degrees each day Friday 

and Saturday. 



Sunday through Tuesday is when temperatures really heat up for 

inland areas. As far as the Chetco Effect, there's some uncertainty 

regarding if that will persist into Sunday with some guidance 

showing onshore flow returning at the coast and the thermal trough 

pushing inland. So it's possible for another day of hot temperatures 

along the southern coast. For inland areas though, expect triple 

digits for the West Side Valleys on Sunday with low to mid 90s for 

the East Side. Temperatures trend hotter on Monday, and this 

currently looks like the peak of the heat for inland locations. 

Currently we have 106 degrees in the forecast for Medford on Monday, 

but there is a 40% chance of reaching 110 degrees. The coast north 

of Cape Blanco should miss out on the hottest of the hot, but there 

are some indications that temperatures in North Bend could reach 

into the 80s on Sunday and Monday. The heat starts to ebb on Tuesday 

with a very subtle cooling trend. Triple digits remain in the 

forecast for many West Side Valleys on Tuesday with low to mid 90s 

continuing across the East Side. 



These upcoming temperatures put the region under a moderate to major 

risk for heat related illnesses. On Monday, much of the area west of 

the Cascades is forecast to see a major heat risk, meaning that this 

level of heat will impact anyone without cooling or hydration. Take 

steps now to prepare for this heat wave, and be sure to know the 

signs of heat illnesses. /BR-y



&&



.AVIATION...07/00Z TAFs...The TAF period starts with VFR levels 

across northern California and southern Oregon, with some changes 

expected overnight. MVFR to IFR ceilings are forecast along the 

Oregon coast, with chances of MVFR ceilings filling into the Umpqua 

Valley. Guidance also shows clouds banking in the southern end of the 

Rogue Valley overnight. There are slight chances of MVFR ceilings 

reaching the Medford terminal, but those chances are not high enough 

to support inclusion in the TAFs. Any overnight development looks to 

clear out late Thursday morning.



Other areas look to remain at VFR through the TAF period under clear 

skies or high ceilings. -TAD



&&



.MARINE...Updated 200 PM PDT Wednesday, August 6, 2025...Sub- 

advisory conditions are expected through early Thursday. A northwest 

swell will build into the waters later today ahead of a weak front, 

but seas will remain at or below 7 ft through early Thursday.



Conditions deteriorate Thursday afternoon as a thermal trough builds 

along the coast. This will bring moderate to strong north winds 

across the waters, with winds strongest south of Cape Blanco. Wind 

gusts could push towards gales Thursday night into the weekend in 

the southern waters, even so the main threat will be very steep win-

driven seas. Meanwhile Small Craft conditions will set up over the 

south waters late Thursday morning, then spreading over the rest of 

the waters Thursday night. The southern Waters will likely have 

Hazardous Seas Warning conditions and as such, the Hazardous Seas 

Watch has been upgraded to a warning.  



The thermal trough is expected to weaken late Sunday afternoon and 

night into Monday at which point we'll see conditions gradually 

improve. -Petrucelli



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...Updated 200 PM PDT Wednesday, August 6, 2025...Dry 

and stable weather will continue for the next several days.



Weak upper troughing will remain over the area today with afternoon 

temperatures below seasonal norms for the interior. 



A stonger upper trough will drop southeast and swing through 

Washington State Thursday. This will result in a thermal trough 

developing along the coast along with warmer afternoon temperatures 

for the interior, but still slightly below normal. Gusty afternoon 

and early evening breezes are likely east of the Cascades Thursday 

afternoon into early Thursday evening, but relative humidities will 

be high enough to keep conditions from getting critical. 



Meanwhile, breezy east to northeast winds will set up near and at 

the ridges in southwest Oregon and western Fire Zone 280 resulting 

in moderate to locally poor overnight recoveries Thursday night.  



Strong upper ridging builds into the area Friday through early next 

week. Meanwhile the thermal trough will remain in place. The net 

result will be continued gusty east to northeast breezes near and at 

the ridges in southwest Oregon and western Siskiyou County lasting 

into Saturday night. East winds are then expected to weaken Sunday 

night with improved overnight recoveries near and at the ridges in 

southwest Oregon and western fire zone 280. 



Temperatures near and at the ridges at night will be warm along with 

warm afternoon temperatures at Brookings and Red Mound in the 

afternoons starting Thursday and lasting into the weekend. Highs in 

Brookings Friday into the weekend could get into the upper 80s or 

low 90s. 



At the same time, afternoon temperatures will heat up for the 

interior westside valleys Friday into at least the middle of next 

week. Afternoon temperatures for the interior westside valleys will 

likely get into triple digit territory Saturday into Next Wednesday 

and low to mid 90s east of the Cascades.



At the same time, we'll have very low relative humidities in the 

afternoon for the interior with minimum relative humidities for 

portions of Fire zones 624, 625 and 285 getting close to single 

digits Friday into early next week -Petrucelli



&&



.MFR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

OR...CA...None.



PACIFIC COASTAL WATERS...Small Craft Advisory from 5 PM Thursday to 5 

