FXUS64 KEWX 062347

AFDEWX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Austin/San Antonio TX

647 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Seasonably hot with isolated afternoon coastal plains showers

  through early next week



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Tonight through Friday) 

Issued at 1258 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A strong upper level ridge will remain centered over New Mexico and 

northwest Texas through Friday, with most of south-central Texas 

under its influence. Across the inland coastal plains, low chances 

(10-20%) exist for isolated sea-breeze showers across Lavaca, 

DeWitt, and Karnes counties, being where the ridge is weakest. 

Otherwise, seasonably hot conditions continue through Friday, with a

slight increase in high temperatures.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Friday Night through Wednesday)

Issued at 1258 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



An inverted upper level trough over the northern Gulf on Friday is 

forecast to slide westward Saturday and Sunday, into the northwest 

Gulf and Texas coast, weakening the eastern extent of the upper level

ridge. This may allow for a slightly farther progression inland of 

afternoon sea-breeze showers Sunday, but still east of the I-35 

corridor which will be in the subsident region.



The upper level ridge to the west is forecast to retrograde farther 

west in Arizona early next week. This allows the aforementioned 

inverted trough to link into the broader northern stream trough, 

pinching off over Texas between the ridge to the west and subtropical

ridge to the east. The main impact from this will be a slight 

downward trend in high temperatures a few degrees, but dew points

trending slightly upward. There may also be slightly better 

opportunities for daily showers inland into the I-35 corridor and 

Hill Country on Monday and Tuesday, isolated in coverage and 

diurnally driven. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 644 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Southeast winds weaken at I-35 sites overnight while southeast flow 

persists at DRT. Low clouds spread northwest overnight with good

agreement in guidance, so TEMPOs for MVFR maintained for SAT and SSF

between 12Z-15Z. Some models indicate IFR CIGs may also be briefly 

possible at these sites, mainly around 12Z. Can't rule out similar 

CIGs developing at AUS, but higher chances remain to the south. CIGs 

break after sunrise, yielding VFR with light southeast winds.



&&



.PRELIMINARY POINT TEMPS/POPS...

