416

FXUS66 KOTX 062351

AFDOTX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Spokane WA

451 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Cooler with showers and thunderstorms Wednesday into Thursday.

  Stronger thunderstorms possible on Thursday over southeast WA

  and central/southern ID panhandle.



- Drying and warming trend this weekend into next week.



&&



.SYNOPSIS...

A couple systems move by the region Wednesday and Thursday, 

bringing showers and thunderstorms and cooler temperatures, 

with highs mainly in the 70s.  More typical summer weather 

returns this weekend into early next week, with temperatures 

rising back into the 80s and 90s.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Today through tonight: A weak shortwave has brought increased cloud 

cover and light rain this morning and early afternoon. The band will 

slowly move into north Idaho in the next couple of hours and drop up 

to a few hundreths of rain with locally higher amounts in the 

northeast mountains. A round of weak nocturnal convection is 

expected to move through the region tonight. Isolated fire starts 

will be the greatest concern with this round of showers and 

thunderstorms. 



Tomorrow: A stronger wave will drop southeast from Vancouver Island 

tonight and into Thursday morning bringing a better chance for 

thunderstorms Thursday afternoon. With 500-1000 J/kg of surface 

based cape and 15-30 kt of surface to 6 km shear (up to 40 in

southeast WA/southern ID Panhandle), storms could be on the 

stronger side. The best shear/cape overlap is in southeastern 

WA/southern ID Panhandle around 3-8 pm Thursday just downstream 

the base of the trough. These stronger cells would be capable of

hail size up to an inch, gusty outflow winds around 30-50 mph, 

and frequent lightning. By Friday, the trough quickly departs 

into western MT ushering in more stable northerly flow. There is

still a small chance (15%) for thunderstorms in far NE WA/north

Idaho but these cells will not have the strength of Thursday's 

cells. 



Friday through Tuesday: Friday brings the start of a warming and 

drying trend as a high pressure ridge builds off the WA coast. By 

Sunday, temperatures will return to near/above average in the upper 

80s to mid 90s with dry conditions. DB



&&



.AVIATION...

18Z TAFS: Primarily VFR conditions expected through the period,

but localized MVFR conditions possible with steadier 

precipitation and by early Thursday near GEG/SFF/COE/PUW.

This steadier rain will move into KGEG/KSFF eastward in a 

couple hours. Some night thunderstorms are possible beginning 

02Z at KCOE, around 06Z for KGEG/KSFF, and around 10Z for KPUW. 

There will be a short break in steady rain around 18Z, but it 

will be brief and pick up again near the end of the TAF period. 

Thunderstorm chances will be higher tomorrow after 21Z.



FORECAST CONFIDENCE AND/OR ALTERNATE SCENARIOS:

High confidence for VFR conditions. Low confidence in MVFR

conditions. Low confidence in overnight thunderstorms, but

moderate confidence in thunder tomorrow near the end of this TAF

period. /AS



-----------------------



Confidence descriptors:

Low - Less than a 30 percent chance

Moderate - 30 to 70 percent chance

High - Greater than a 70 percent chance



For additional probabilistic information for NWS Spokane

airports, please refer to the Aviation Dashboard on our webpage:

https:/weather.gov/otx/avndashboard



&&



