429

FXUS64 KMAF 062352

AFDMAF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Midland/Odessa TX

652 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 634 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



- Hot temperatures with Heat Advisories for exceptionally

  hazardous heat and air temperatures/apparent temperatures up to

  105 F forecast this afternoon through evening and again Friday 

  and Saturday.



- Little to no rain chances (<5%) under ridging into this weekend,

  then cooler temperatures as an upper troughing pattern and an

  increased probability (15%-30%) of showers/storms over western

  higher terrain forecast each afternoon into evening return to

  the area.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(This afternoon through Thursday night)

Issued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Today and tomorrow will be characterized by continuing hot 

temperatures. A Heat Advisory is in effect for Eddy County Plains 

into Culberson County, Marfa Plateau, and Brewster County into 

this evening for highs up to 106F for Eddy County Plains into Big 

Bend. Dew point temperatures remain in the 40s F western higher 

terrain, 50s F and below 60 F elsewhere as a dryline largely stays

east of the area. This will mean that while conditions will not 

feel as humid as if dew point temperatures were higher, Heat Risk 

in the moderate to critical 2 to 3 category is still expected. We 

are expecting the greatest risk of heat risk this afternoon, where

values rise to the critical 3 range for Eddy County Plains. Mid 

to upper ridging over the central CONUS and absence of any 

significant surface features keeps rain chances below 10% 

areawide, so apart from a stray shower or storm from heating of 

elevated terrain and terrain induced circulations, not expecting 

impacts from showers/storms today. Tonight is forecast to be a few

degrees cooler than last night over the Marfa Plateau, with lows 

falling into the lower 60s F instead of the mid 60s F, along with 

mid to upper 60s F in surrounding lower elevations. Otherwise, 

expect lows to fall into the upper 60s to mid 70s F range, mid to 

upper 70s F along the Rio Grande. 



The mid to upper ridging weakens slightly by tomorrow, so the 

heat is not expected to rival today. However, Heat Risk near 2 

will still pose health risks for anyone outdoors or without air 

conditioning. Highs still rise into the upper 90s to triple digits

northeast of the Marfa Plateau (with triple digits along the 

Pecos River and over northeast Permian Basin), with upper 80s to 

lower 90s in higher elevations, upper 90s to triple digits over 

basins of Culberson County and along the Rio Grande, and highs 

again up to 110F for the Big Bend. Dew point temperatures are 

forecast to remain below 60 F during peak heating hours, except 

for northeasternmost and southeasternmost portions of the forecast

area as the dryline meanders farther west than today. 

Additionally, Heat Risk near critical 3 value is only present for 

northeasternmost Permian Basin and air temperatures/apparent 

temperatures remain below 105 F threshold for exceptionally 

hazardous heat criteria. Therefore, despite continued hot weather,

apart from messaging heat impacts, we have not issued any Heat 

Advisory products. Lows Thursday night remain similar to Wednesday

night under mostly clear skies.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Friday through Tuesday)

Issued at 1224 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The astronomical "dog days of summer" into early next week will 

be characterized by what we typically expect during that time of 

year: hot temperatures. Friday features widespread triple digits 

northeast of Marfa Plateau, upper 80s to mid 90s higher 

elevations, triple digits along Rio Grande into Terrell County, 

and reading up to 105 F to 110 F for the Presidio Valley and the 

southernmost Rio Grande basins. Heat Risk values up to 3 increase 

in coverage compared to Thursday, but leaving issuance of any heat

products after Thursday to subsequent forecast shifts as we get 

closer to this next heatwave and the details get more ironed out. 

Hot temperatures again abate a few degrees at the start of the 

weekend but remain warmer than Thursday, with Heat Risk values up 

to 3 persisting over usual locations of southern Rio Grande 

basins, Upper Trans Pecos, Eddy County Plains, and northeast 

Permian Basin. The return of more seasonable hot temperatures only

comes into view Sunday into early next week as surface lee 

troughing over SE NM into W TX redevelops and rain chances and 

cloud cover increase, accompanied by cooling air columns as 

ridging lessens over the central CONUS and a troughing pattern 

more favorable to progression of weak cold fronts into the area 

redevelops. Highs Sunday through the middle part of next week 

feature highs in the lower to mid 90s northeast of Upper Trans 

Pecos, mid to upper 90s Upper Trans Pecos and Terrell County into 

Rio Grande basins, mid to upper 80s higher elevations, and triple 

digits along the Rio Grande from Presidio Valley into the Big 

Bend. Lows stay in the mid 60s to mid 70s F range, lower to mid 

60s F range higher elevations. Apart from highest percentile 

ensembles however, forecast grids and ensembles show little to no 

rainfall. 



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 634 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions are expected to continue through Thursday

afternoon. Gusty S/SE winds will persist through the early evening

hours before winds generally diminish to 5-10 knots for the most

part after sunset. S/SE winds will increase to 10-15 knots with

some gusts over 20 knots late Thursday morning into Thursday

afternoon. 



&&



