[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8945","issue":"2025-08-06T23:52:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS64","cccc":"KLIX","awipsid":"AFDLIX"}]
=================================================



432

FXUS64 KLIX 062352

AFDLIX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service New Orleans LA

652 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.SHORT TERM...

(This evening through Thursday night)

Issued at 1245 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



We are in a fairly stagnant upper level weather pattern with 

high pressure over the Four Corners, a very weak trough extending

from the Great Lakes into the northcentral Gulf, and the Bermuda

high extending into the eastern Gulf. All of this is combining to

produce a weak, relatively stationary front along the the coast,

drifting north and south through our area. Over the next 36 to 48

hours these features, plus some sea/lake breeze boundaries are 

providing a focus for afternoon thunderstorms. Note that some of

these storms could produce enough rain to cause some localized

flash flooding. The rain will balance with partly cloudy skies to

give us fairly normal high temperatures this afternoon and 

tomorrow in the low 90s. Winds will tend toward calm and variable,

except in the area of thunderstorms where they will be gusty and 

vary in direction.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Friday through Tuesday night)

Issued at 1245 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Beyond the short term, the upper level pattern will stay about the

same as it is now, except the trough and frontal boundary will

wash out into the weekend, but a weakness between the two high

pressure domes will remain in place and still provide a driver for

thunderstorm growth. The daytime highs, though, will remain about

the same as we are currently seeing.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 635 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Most terminals are seeing VFR conditions though there are still a

few lingering afternoon storms moving westward just north of HDC

and BTR which have prompted the addition of short-fused TEMPOs. 

Gusty winds up to 25-30kt and brief VIS/CIG reduction are the 

primary impacts. SH/TS will dissipate after sunset leaving the 

area with VFR conditions and light, variable winds through the 

morning hours. The only exception is noted at BTR and MCB where 

some fuzziness to VIS could occur with some intermittent MVFR 

fog/low stratus due to rain-soaked grounds from these evening 

storms. Very rinse- repeat for convective development beginning 

around 9-12Z along the coast and in the Gulf Waters and popping 

along the lake/sea breezes by 17-20Z. Have added PROB30s to 

reflect best general timing of TSRA impacts at terminals from 

latest guidance.



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 1245 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Main concern for marine operations will once again be the threat of 

thunderstorms, with the higher probabilities being in the offshore 

waters during the overnight hours. If there's going to be a 

day/night with the least activity it will probably be Friday. 

Outside of convective impacts, wind/wave conditions will remain 

benign across the coastal waters. A few strong storms will be 

possible each day/night, and would result in gusty winds, 

waterspouts, and locally higher waves/seas. The threat for 

waterspouts will be highest during the morning hours, especially 

when they can anchor near boundary intersections such as frontal 

boundaries, lake and sea breeze boundaries. 



&&



