[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8946","issue":"2025-08-06T23:53:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS63","cccc":"KFGF","awipsid":"AFDFGF"}]
=================================================



433

FXUS63 KFGF 062353

AFDFGF



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Grand Forks ND

653 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- A few strong to severe storms are possible tonight in the Red

  Lakes to Lake of the Woods area with hail to 1" and a few 

  wind gusts up to 60 mph.



- Severe chances increasing in severity and coverage each day 

  Thursday and Friday.



- With already primed soils in some areas localized flash 

  flooding may occur due to Thursday and Friday storms.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 652 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A few cumulus clouds across the northern Red River Valley, and a

few light radar returns as convection has tried to get going.

However, most of the forcing is to the north and what has tried

to develop has been short lived. Will continue to monitor our

northern tier for some isolated convection later this evening,

but for now the forecast is quiet and only made minor tweaks. 



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 219 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...Synopsis...



A dominant ridge over the desert southwest is currently leading to 

quasi zonal flow across the Northern Plains with a subtle southwest 

to northeast tilt. As this ridge breaks down through the end of the 

increasingly amplified shortwave activity will traverse this zonal 

flow impinging upon the dakotas and bring multiple rounds of severe 

weather with activity reaching a crescendo on Friday 

evening/overnight. By saturday afternoon one of there waves will 

develop into a closed upper low centered near the Lake Winnipeg 

region in south Central Manitoba. Associated with this upper low 

will be a cold front behind Fridays' severe with a cooler airmass 

arriving for the weekend as high drop into the upper 70s/low 80s. The 

cooler trend then looks to continue into next week as northwest flow 

becomes the dominant pattern once the upper low departs to the 

east late weekend.



Today (Wednesday)...



As weak impulse traverses westerly flow aloft it will retrograde 

north resulting in subtle rising heights at 500mb. MLCAPE around 

1500-2000 with eff shear near 25kt only support some marginally 

severe convection. Forecast currently support hail up to 1" or so 

and a few gusts upwards of 50 mph through the lack of flow below 

500mb over 35 kts and only modest amounts of dry air in the mid 

levels should limit downdraft potential but also limit dry air 

entrainment for hail production giving that threat a slight boost.



Thursday...



Thursday on its own right looks to have some problems with an 

afternoon upgrade today a day 2 enhanced (level 3 out of 5). The 

overall synoptic pattern looks to be driven by a stout 700mb wave 

with a closed low at 850mph ejecting out the eastern Montana area 

into ND by the early morning hours Friday. With this will be a broad 

sfc warm sector with dewpoints in the low 70s for much of ND. The 

effective warm front currently depicted across the HWY 2 corridor. 

Capping concerns becoming a lesser factor with 700mb temps only 

around 10-12C. Things look to initiate in eastern MT/Western ND and 

grow upscale thereafter leading to a primary wind threat across 

eastern ND and adjacent areas of MN. Tornadoes remain possible along 

with hail but these will likely be secondary threats compared to 

winds should things quickly grow upscale with eastward extent. 

MUCAPE of 3000+ and 0-3km shear of 20-25kts should at the very least 

support 70+ mph and if MLCIN is less than currently expected with 

eastward advance could support higher wind chances into northwest 

Minnesota that currently being advertised. Lastly some cams are 

depicting a "bow and arrow" setup which could favor flashing 

flooding behind the line with HREF PMM showing 3-5" across potions 

of eastern ND. Overall a flood watch may be needed by tomorrow 

afternoon but it is too early to tell where.



Friday...



A much more robust shortwave, leading to another enhanced (level 3 

out of 5 risk), will rotate into the northern plains Friday 

afternoon with a warm front draped across the HWY 2 corridor with a 

cold front back into central ND. Overall there are some doubts about 

the EML leading to capping and relatively weak deep layer shear 

around 30-35kts limiting the forcing. Vectors do look favorable for 

at least initially discrete storms which would mainly be a large 

(golf ball size ) hail threat and a few tornadoes. Eventual upscale 

growth would lead to a primary wind threat (80+ mph possible) with a 

decreasing hail threat and a maintained (QLCS type) tornado threat. 

Overall Friday seems to have the highest ceiling when considering 

the potential for discrete supercells though an analog such as June 

20th seems a little far fetched for the overall potential for the 

event. Flooding once again may be a problem but this will be very 

contingent on the prior nights convection.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 652 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions for all except KBJI, which has seen some 2500 ft

stratus hanging around this afternoon. Conditions at KBJI should

improve to VFR in the next few hours as cumulus dissipates. Some

patchy fog formation isn't out of the question overnight, but at

this point it seems like best chances are well south of the TAF

sites. Winds that are southwesterly at 8 to 12 kts will become

more southeasterly by tomorrow. 



&&



.FGF WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

ND...None.

MN...None.



&&



$$



UPDATE...JR

DISCUSSION...TT

AVIATION...JR



