[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.8953","issue":"2025-08-06T23:53:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KGYX","awipsid":"AFDGYX"}]
=================================================



440

FXUS61 KGYX 062353

AFDGYX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Gray ME

753 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

High pressure will remain over New England through the remainder

of the week with temperatures near seasonable averages.

Increasing heat and humidity is then expected this weekend

through early next week. Aside from some isolated showers and

storms, it will remain mostly dry across the region into early 

next week. Smoke and haze may try to return to the area Thursday

into Friday as winds become southwesterly.



&&



.NEAR TERM /UNTIL 7 AM THURSDAY MORNING/...

750 PM Update...Convection across western NH has waned and the

threat for slow moving storms has diminished. Have refined PoPs

to reflect these observed trends with no other significant

changes to the going forecast for tonight.



Previously...



Much like yesterday we will be dealing with isolated but slow

moving showers and storms across NH. Mainly anticipate these to

form in the Upper Valley and drift southward towards the

Monadnocks before weakening with the loss of daytime heating.

Like yesterday HREF suggests that 3 hr precip totals could

approach 2 to 3 inches under the strongest convection. I bring

PoP up to around 50 percent for southern Grafton into Sullivan

County before trending things down...but once radar shows trends

forecasts may have to update those numbers. 



Overnight with strong high pressure in place we should see temps

radiate nicely. Widespread lows in the 50s are likely with 40s

in the northern valleys. Radiation fog will also be possible. I

added patchy fog to most areas...recent dryness should be

mitigated somewhat by evapotranspiration...and patchy dense fog

to the major river valleys in the mtns.



&&



.SHORT TERM /7 AM THURSDAY MORNING THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/...

Thu will be very similar to today. Seasonable temps and weak

pressure gradient under high pressure will promote afternoon sea

breezes taking over. As high reorients slightly farther south

and east it will also move forcing for isolated showers slightly

north across NH. This will put the focus more into the White

Mtns vs the Upper Valley. Will also have to watch for haze/smoke

on the periphery of the sea breeze based on latest HRRR/RRFS

near-surface smoke forecasts. Thu night will be another coolish

night compared to recent temps...with valley fog a threat once 

again.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Pattern Overview: Surface high pressure stays locked in through

the majority of the long term forecast period. A 500 mb 

shortwave moves through Friday and Saturday which may help to 

pop some diurnally driven, terrain induced showers/storms. Deep 

layer ridging then takes over early next week which comes with 

more heat and humidity. The models diverge toward mid-week, with

some suggesting the ridging stays in place while others suggest

we may get a frontal passage and our best chance at somewhat 

widespread showers we have seen as of late.

  

  

Impacts and Key Messages: 

* Heat and humidity return early next week with heat indices 

  around 90-95F possible in some areas.

* The extended forecast continues to have little in the way of 

  precipitation, and with a signal for above normal 

  temperatures, an expansion of abnormally dry conditions in the

  region seems likely. 

                 

Details: Friday: High pressure becomes centered offshore Friday

with a 500 mb shortwave approaching from the west. The airmass 

remains plenty dry with no large scale synoptic forcing, so the 

result is likely diurnally driven, terrain induced clouds and 

maybe an isolated shower/storm. With little in the way of any 

kind of steering flow, I would expect any showers/storms that do

develop to stay confined to the terrain. Temperatures south of 

the mountains, where skies end up clearer, climb into the low to

mid-80s. North of the mountains where clouds develop, 

temperatures will be slightly stunted, topping out in the upper 

70s to low 80s. Clouds stay banked up in the mountains overnight

as the shortwave moves overhead resulting in low temperatures 

mid- to upper 50s areawide. 



Saturday and Sunday: Saturday looks very similar to Friday with

the shortwave starting overhead and sliding east offshore. 

Expect some clouds in and around the mountains, along with a 

chance for an isolated shower/storm, with clear skies prevailing

elsewhere. Temperatures continue to trend upward as high 

pressure remains in control, but only slightly so on Saturday. 

South of the mountains looks to top out in the low to mid-80s 

once again, with more in the way of low 80s to the north. 

Dewpoints trend upward as well, making for a slightly warmer 

night with temperatures bottoming out in the upper 50s to around

60. With the trough positioned offshore and potentially phasing

with a low pressure system, there will be enough of a northwest

flow to keep some clouds in the mountains Sunday, but chances 

of showers go away as deep layer ridging begins to build in. As 

the warming trend continues, high temperatures on Sunday stand 

to climb near 90 in many locations. Dewpoints stay rising as 

well, limiting low temperatures to the low to mid-60s Sunday 

night. 



Monday-Wednesday: Models are in fairly good agreement at the 

moment that ridging stays in place through the better part of 

Tuesday. This would continue to lend itself to rising heat and 

humidity with heat indices potentially rising near 95F in some 

locations by Tuesday. Models diverge after this, with the the 

Euro suggesting ridging stays in place through Wednesday, but 

this is the outlier. The GFS and Canadian suite suggest a 

frontal passage Tuesday night or Wednesday which would come with

some chances for showers. This will be a period to watch as we 

look for any kind of relief from the dry spell. It is also worth

noting that we are heading into the historically busy time for 

the tropics, so keeping tabs on that will be important as well.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Short Term...Widespread VFR conditions anticipated during the

daytime. Tonight and Thu night there will be some radiation fog

that develops. Local IFR or lower conditions are

possible...especially at HIE and LEB. Sea breezes are expected

during the day Thu as well...so coastal terminals will see winds

flip from offshore to onshore during the mid morning hours.



Long Term...VFR ceilings will prevail during the day through 

Monday as high pressure stays over the region. Restrictions may 

come during the overnight and early morning periods as 

increasing low level moisture will lend itself to fog formation.

Some very isolated thunderstorms are possible in the mountains 

on Friday and Saturday afternoon.



&&



.MARINE...

Short Term...Winds and seas are expected to remain below SCA

thresholds. Sea breezes will develop during the afternoon with a

weak pressure gradient across the region.



Long Term...Wind gusts and seas remain below SCA criteria 

through the day Sunday. Long period swell from a passing low 

pressure system may briefly bring seas up to around 5ft Sunday 

evening into early Monday morning. Winds look to be generally 

southerly with gusts up to 15kts.



&&



.GYX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

ME...None.

NH...Air Quality Alert until 11 PM EDT this evening for NHZ003-005-

     007-011.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$





NEAR TERM...Legro/Schroeter

SHORT TERM...Legro

LONG TERM...Baron



