[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9000","issue":"2025-08-06T23:57:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KPIH","awipsid":"AFDPIH"}]
=================================================



487

FXUS65 KPIH 062357

AFDPIH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Pocatello ID

557 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Isolated Showers & Storms Return Late Tonight Through Friday



- Red Flag Warning - Critical to Near Critical Fire Weather 

  Conditions



- Much Cooler Weather By Friday



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 254 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



An area of low pressure will move across Washington state, northern 

Idaho, and through Montana over the next few days which will bring a 

cold front through Eastern Idaho late Thursday into early Friday. 

This system will increase our rain/storm chances a bit, but for many 

this will be a dry cold frontal passage. There is about a 5 to 10 

percent chance for an isolated storm or two up in the northern 

portion of Custer, Clark, and Fremont counties. Wind gusts today 

will range from 20 to 30 mph for many. Gusts will increase a bit for 

Thursday, from 30 to 35 mph for many and closer to 40 mph out across 

the Arco desert. Storms on Thursday still look isolated, but it'll 

be closer to a 15 to 25 percent chance in parts of the Central 

Mountains and upper Snake River Plain and northern Eastern 

Highlands. This cold front will bring some relief from the heat as 

highs go from low to mid 90s in the Snake River Plain today to mid 

and upper 80s Thursday to upper 70s by Friday.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 254 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



By late Friday, Eastern Idaho will solidly be in northwest flow 

aloft with dry conditions expected through the weekend and early 

next week. After a quick cool down into the upper 70s and low 80s on 

Friday, the heat begins to build again with highs in the lower 

elevations back into mid and upper 80s by Sunday afternoon with a 

return to the 90s on Monday. Wind gusts stay in the 15 to 25 mph 

range through the first half of next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 548 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR conditions are expected through the period with winds still

breezy currently around 20 kts at all sites except BYI and PIH, 

where winds are lighter. Winds will diminish by early to mid 

evening for all sites, but pick up again tomorrow, Thursday, 

afternoon as a cold front approaches. Expect gusts of 20 to 25 

kts for all TAF sites tomorrow afternoon into the early evening 

tomorrow. All convection tomorrow afternoon/early evening should

remain north of all sites with the best convection over the 

Montana Divide just north of IDA and DIJ. Have held off on a 

Prob30 for thunderstorms for IDA and DIJ for tomorrow afternoon,

for now.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 254 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



An approaching low pressure shortwave trough that will brush across 

southeast Idaho from the north on Thursday will send a cold front 

across the region. This system will support some thunderstorm 

activity as far south as portions of Zones 475, 476, and 411, 

although coverage currently appears isolated with low confidence in 

scattered (critical) coverage. Meanwhile...this system will nudge RH 

values a bit higher Thursday afternoon compared to what we are 

seeing today, but it will also solidly increase the winds. In 

coordination with our regional fire weather partners...the bump in 

RH is not expected to be enough to make a significant difference in 

fire risk given very dry measured fuel moistures and the increase in 

winds (which may gust 30-40 MPH over Zone 410). Thus, it is very 

likely we will be issuing Red Flag Warnings for Thursday across 

Zones 410, 413, and 427, with non-zero potential that another zone 

or two may need to be tacked on as well (411?). RH values leaning 20 

percent or higher across Zones 422 and 425 may preclude them from 

inclusion. Overall, the driest conditions are expected south of the 

Snake Plain (413/427), while the strongest winds will focus across 

Zone 410. Given that we already have Red Flags out for a couple 

zones today, the final call on issuance will be made this evening or 

overnight tonight. Cooler temperatures are expected post-system from 

Friday into the weekend, with RHs still lingering near critical 

thresholds but winds steadily decreasing over the three days. No 

thunderstorms are currently expected beyond Thursday's system.



&&



.PIH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Red Flag Warning until 9 PM MDT this evening for IDZ413-422.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...AMM

LONG TERM...AMM

AVIATION...TW

FIRE WEATHER...01



