489

FXUS63 KABR 062357

AFDABR



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Aberdeen SD

657 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Patchy fog with visibility averaging around 3 miles expected 

around daybreak Thursday morning, lowest near and south of Watertown.



- Hot and humid Thursday with heat indices as high as 104 over 

portions of central South Dakota.



- An active pattern continues with showers and thunderstorms 

in the forecast through Friday night.



- There is a marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) for severe storms 

south of a line from McIntosh to Watertown this evening if isolated 

storms can form. There is a slight risk (level 2 out of 5) along and 

north of a line from Eagle Butte through Miller to Watertown on 

Thursday. Strong winds and large hail will be the primary threats.



- There is an enhanced risk (level 3 out of 5) for severe weather 

over portions of far northeastern SD into west central MN Friday 

afternoon into Friday night with a slight risk extending westward to 

a line from Eureka to Winner. All hazards are possible including 

tornadoes.



&&



.UPDATE...

Issued at 631 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The current forecast remains on track, with little to no

adjustments expected. The main change was to incorporate more fog

over eastern SD and west central MN, with visibilities down to

around 1-2SM near and south of ATY 10-12Z. We'll continue to

monitor the trends with fog development overnight. 



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 242 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



An unstable weather pattern continues through the end of the week 

along with the threat for severe weather. An increase to the heat 

and humidity is expected Thursday with heat indices possibly 

reaching as high as 104 degrees over portions of central SD. As of 

230pm, temperatures range in the upper 70s to the upper 80s and 

dewpoints in the upper 60s to around 70 with winds between 5 to 15 

mph. 



By this evening, the overall upper pattern consist of a trough over 

the western CONUS as a mid level low will move in off the northern 

Pacific and over western British Columbia with a ridge downwind of 

the trough and over the Rockies by 00Z. RAP/HREF indicate ongoing 

zonal flow over the region as a weak shortwave/embedded pulses will 

move in over central SD with these weak pulses pushing eastward over 

the state through the overnight. This weak upper level activity will 

combine with a surface lee trough (extending eastward from the lee 

low through central SD) to help with the possibility of isolated 

convection, however, surface convergence and lift aloft will be weak 

along with an ongoing cap in place. New run of the CAMs are less 

than thrilled on little to any convection (keeping the atmosphere 

capped). HREF has convection firing up from north central through 

south central and spreading southeastward through east central SD. 

So confidence remains low on the outcome and pops may need to be 

adjusted. If we can get convection there is the possibility of a few 

strong to severe storms as dewpoints will continue in the mid to 

upper 60s/70 along with marginal CAPE and shear and steep lapse 

rates of 7-8C. With this isolated threat, the SPC has a marginal 

risk (1/5), along and south of a line from McIntosh through 

Brookings as hail, up to the size of quarters, and 60 mph wind gusts 

are possible.



By Thursday, this ridge (and winds turning southwest aloft) will be 

over the Northern Plains as this mid level low, that was over BC, 

swings southeast and over the Pacific Northwest by 18Z with the axis 

of the trough extending southward. 850mb temps by peak heating will 

range from 23 to 30C with 700mb temps between 12 and 16C, with the 

warmest 700-850mb temps over central SD which is 1-2 standard 

deviations above climo, per NAEFS. Temps are forecasted to rise into 

the upper 80s east of the James River with temps ranging in the 90s 

to possibly 100 west of here. Add in dewpoints in the 70s, heat 

indices are forecasted to reach around 100-104 for portions of 

central SD to possibly Brown/Spink. A Heat headline will be needed, 

however, in collab we decided to hold off on the issuance on this 

shift. With this southwest flow, embedded shortwave energy will move 

in over north central SD through ND by the afternoon, with most of 

this activity staying north of our CWA. The lee low will also reside 

over the western Dakotas/MT/WY with the trough extending farther 

eastward over the CWA from this low. Once again, warm and moist air 

at the surface will cap the atmosphere through much of the day. 

SUfficient CAPE, shear, and lapse rates along with daytime heating 

should be enough to break the cap by the evening allowing for 

showers and thunderstorms to fire up over the area. RAP 1000-500mb 

does indicate drier air residing over south central SD as a dryline 

extends southwest from this lee low. NamNEST/NSSL WRF both indicate 

convection firing up over west central ND by late afternoon/evening 

and quickly forming into a line (bowing line aka wind event?) as it 

tracks southeast and through the CWA through Thursday night. This 

scenario allows more time for cold pooling and a southeastern storm 

motion leading to these storms to plunge southward into the higher 

instability. However the other scenario, HiRes ARW/FV3/HRRR/RAP 

keeps the storm threat in ND with the southern edge of the linear 

system skirting just along the ND/SD border as storm motion is more 

eastward. So low confidence on what scenario will play out at this 

time. However, SPC did expand the slight risk eastward given this 

linear threat hitting portions of the CWA and now extends from a 

line from Eagle Butte through Miller to Watertown and northward with 

a marginal risk (1/5) south of here.



By Friday, things could get interesting as the lee low is forecasted 

to be over ~north central SD by 12Z with the low and cold front to 

push southeast, mainly along and east of the Mo River, Friday 

afternoon and evening and the mid level low forecasted to be over 

MT/Saskatchewan border by 00Z Saturday and ongoing southwest flow 

(embedded shortwave pulses) on the PVA side of the trough. Add in 

daytime heating, steep low to mid lapse rates, and moderate 

instability/shear will lead to all modes of severe weather possible. 

With bulk shear becoming parallel to front this could enhance the 

convection to become more linear with embedded bows/QLCS possible 

east of the James River. Due to this threat there is an enhanced 

risk (3/5) over far northeastern SD into west central MN with a 

slight risk (2/5) extending westward from a line from Eureka to 

Winner. EC EFI is up to 0.9 for cape and shear with a shift of tails 

between zero and one.



By the weekend, this mid level low will swing northeast and track 

over Manitoba and becoming an open wave as it nears the Hudson Bay. 

The trough from this will continue to push eastward with winds 

turning northwest on the backside of it late Sunday into Monday as a 

large ridge builds over the western CONUS. Temps will be cooler back 

into the upper 70s to the upper 80s for the weekend and warming back 

up through next week as this ridge pushes eastward. It does look 

like we get a bit of a break in precip with generally 15% chance or 

less of pops here and there Sunday though the middle of next week.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z THURSDAY/...

Issued at 631 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Terminals KABR,KATY,KPIR,KMBG



VFR conditions are expected to continue over the next 24 hours.

The only exceptions would be for fog over mainly eastern South

Dakota (ABR/ATY), with the lowest visibilities around 1-2SM at ATY

from 10-12Z.





&&



.ABR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

SD...None.

MN...None.

&&



$$



