=================================================



648

FXPQ50 PGUM 062358

AFDGUM



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Tiyan GU

958 AM ChST Thu Aug 7 2025



.Tropical Systems Update...

A circulation, Invest 98W, is found just east of Pagan Island near

18N148E. This circulation has shown gradually improving organization

the past 24 hours as it has drifted westward. The Joint Typhoon

Warning Center has just rated it high with the issuance of a Tropical

Cyclone Formation Alert for the expected development into a tropical

cyclone in the next 24 hours. Latest deterministic and ensemble

model data show 98W continuing westward into the Philippine Sea as 

it continues to become become better organized. Impacts will largely

be limited to the far northern islands and consisting of very heavy

downpours, thunderstorms, increasingly gusty north winds shifting to

south tonight or early Friday. Seas will also become increasingly

choppy during 98W's slow passage. Inter-island travel is not

recommended until 98W is out of the area. For more information on

98W, see bulletins issued by the JTWC under WMO header WTPN21 PGTW.



&&



.Prev discussion... /issued 504 AM ChST Thu Aug 7 2025/ 



Marianas Update...

No meaningful changes were needed to the forecast tonight. Showers 

are expected to be scattered today. These showers are expected to 

diminish over the next 12 hours or so for Guam and Rota, and over the

next 24 hours for Tinian and Saipan. Pleasant weather is expected to

return once these showers pass. Altimetry shows combined seas of 2 

to 3 feet and the buoys confirm these heights. Winds are expected to 

be light to gentle. Overall, benign seas are expected through the 

weekend.



Eastern Micronesia...

Eastern Micronesia has two trade-wind troughs, one is north of Majuro

and the other is south of Pohnpei. The trough south of Pohnpei will

move westward heading out of the region. The one north of Majuro will

be in the region for a few days yet, but is expected to pass north of

Pohnpei and well north of Kosrae.



Majuro will have isolated showers through Saturday, then could have

scattered showers Sunday and Monday with the next minor disturbance.

Kosrae could have scattered showers through the period as a line of

convergence stretches out behind the receding trough. Pohnpei could

have scattered showers through Friday, then again on Saturday and

Monday, with isolated between, as a contained line from the trough

could flare up again near Pohnpei.



Within the Majuro Waters, winds will be capped at 10 to 15 knots

Friday and Friday night, and 5 to 10 knots at other time. Seas will

be 4 to 5 feet most of the period, the likely exception being 

Saturday, when they might be just 3 to 4 feet.



Within the Kosrae Waters, winds will be capped at 5 to 10 knots

through the period. Seas will be 3 to 4 feet most of the period, but

could be 4 to 5 ft tonight.



Within the Pohnpei Waters, winds will be capped at 5 to 10 knots

through the period, and seas will be 3 to 4 feet through the period

also.



Western Micronesia...

A trade-wind trough is pushing out of Chuuk and toward Yap, and

another trough is west of Koror, Palau. The trough west of Chuuk will

continue to affect the area over the next couple of days.



Chuuk could have scattered showers through Friday. It all depends on

what happens with that system from Eastern Micronesia and where it 

sets up. Yap could have scattered showers today, then isolated

showers through Saturday. Scattered showers could return Sunday and

Monday with the next system. Koror, Palau could have scattered

showers today, isolated for Friday night, then scattered for Saturday

through Monday.



Within the Chuuk Waters, winds will be 5 to 10 knots through the

period. Combined seas will be 2 to 4 foot.



Within the Yap Waters, winds will be capped at 5 knots, and seas will

be 2 to 3 feet.



Within the Koror, Palau Waters, winds will also mainly be capped at 5

knots. Seas will be 2 to 3 feet until Monday, when they could 

diminish to 2 feet.



Prev discussion... /issued 605 PM ChST Wed Aug 6 2025/ 



Marianas Synopsis...

Satellite and radar this afternoon show partly cloudy skies and

isolated showers across much of the Marianas, with more cloud and

shower coverage over Saipan waters from the passage of a northward-

moving outflow boundary. Further north, numerous showers and isolated

showers are seen covering much of the northern CNMI and eastward, 

along the western and southern flanks of a disorganized surface 

circulation. Buoy and altimetry data show combined seas of around 2 

to 4 feet.



Discussion...

A fairly small and weak surface circulation began to take shape 

northeast of the Marianas overnight, which was well captured by the 

models yesterday and later in the overnight ASCAT pass. Today's 

scatterometry analysis places it near 18.7N 149.2E, and it has been 

gradually shifting southwest toward the Marianas this afternoon. CIMSS

850-500 mb vorticity analysis shows a maximum in vorticity in this 

area as well, just to the east of Alamagan. The circulation center 

is now more difficult to make out on visible satellite with 

widespread cloudiness and numerous showers over the center, and 

convection has been persistent, mainly focused along the western and 

southern flanks. Models show little development with this feature 

over the next few days as it makes a slow westward turn across the 

northern CNMI. Current model guidance keeps the circulation north of

Saipan as it shifts west, moving somewhere to the south of Iwo To by

the weekend. GFS wants to develop this disturbance more strongly 

over the Philippine Sea early next week, but other models are not 

following suit for now. For the time being, this feature looks to 

increase showers over the Marianas tonight through at least Thursday 

as it helps to deepen surface troughing in the area. Tinian and 

Saipan will see most of the showers with a lesser increase seen over 

Guam and Rota waters. A quieter pattern looks to move in this weekend

as the feature and associated trough move west.



Marine/Surf...

Light to gentle southeast to south winds are expected to persist for

the next day or two, with more variable winds near Tinian and Saipan

as a col and then a disorganized circulation pass nearby. By the end

of the week, winds should increase to gentle to moderate out of the 

east to southeast. Combined seas of around 3 feet will continue into 

the weekend with a dominant trade swell and light wind waves. A very 

slight increase in trade swell may bump seas up around a foot by 

early next week. This may lead to a small increase in surf and 

increase the rip current risk to moderate for east facing reefs by

Monday, but in the meantime, a low risk of rip currents is expected

along all reefs.



Eastern Micronesia...

The pattern remains active as waves of surface troughs in the trade

winds traverse the region. The first said trough is located from 

near Kapingamarangi northeastward to Pohnpei, ending north of there. 

Most of the convection has been located south of Pohnpei in an area 

of stronger surface convergence and higher PWATs (Precipitable Water),

with above 2.5 inches common south of Pohnpei and less north of 

there.



Then, a longer trough stretches from east of Kosrae, northeast 

through Majuro, ending northeast of there near 13N176E. Here too the 

best moisture and convergence is found across the southern third of 

the trough, mainly southeast of Kosrae. However, near 10N and points

northward also have higher PWATs here, with 2.5 to 3 inch amounts 

common. This region is also benefiting from enhanced surface 

convergence and modest divergence aloft, helping maintain updraft 

vigor due to improved storm top ventilation.



Both of these features will move westward, with the models showing

additional troughs crossing the region from east to west in the

coming days, with each bringing renewed rounds of increasing 

rainfall potential.



For the mariners, light to gentle winds will prevail. Seas of 3 to 5

feet near Pohnpei and Kosrae, and 4 to 6 feet from between Kosrae and

Majuro eastward will continue through the end of the week, with seas

possibly dropping a foot or so to start next week.



Western Micronesia...

A broad area of convergence, between east-southeasterly trade winds 

and west-southwesterly flow, is generating widespread cloudiness, 

scattered to numerous showers, and isolated thunderstorms across 

Palau and Yap. As Invest 96W moves farther northwest towards the 

Philippines, this area of convergence will also shift and shower 

coverage is expected to gradually taper down to isolated to low-end 

scattered Thursday night through Friday night as a weak ridge builds 

in. Over the weekend and into early next week, models hint at another

period of scattered showers as convergence builds across far western

Micronesia. First, within the trade-wind pattern, then a possible 

disturbance to the north may begin to influence the weather pattern 

early next week. 



For Chuuk, generally scattered showers persist through the week due 

to a weak, convergent trade-wind pattern and a series of troughs that

will bring periodic upticks in showers and cloudiness. Although 

model GDI values were not all that supportive, added a slight chance 

of thunderstorms for tonight based on stray thunderstorms observed 

upstream, as well as an upper-level trough providing support for 

convection. 



Overall, benign marine conditions continue. Buoy and altimetry data 

show combined seas of 2 to 4 feet. Light and variable winds persist 

across Yap and Palau over the next several days, meanwhile, 

gentle to moderate trades near Chuuk, are expected to settle back 

down to light to gentle by Thursday night.



&&



.GUM WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

GU...None.

MP...None.

Marianas Waters...None.

&&



