AFDCHS



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Charleston SC

800 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

A front will linger near or just south of the area this week,

while high pressure remains centered well to the north. Low

pressure could develop off the Southeast U.S. coast on

Thursday, tracking to the northeast through this weekend. 



&&



.NEAR TERM /UNTIL 6 AM THURSDAY MORNING/...

Early this evening: Overall, shower and thunderstorm activity

has been pretty minimal this afternoon and into the evening. In

fact, there has been little to no lightning with what little

activity has developed. Current radar imagery continues to show

that the best chance for additional development is along the

immediate coast where the isolated to scattered coverage has

been so far. There have been some pockets of heavier rain, but

nothing that has produced an flooding concerns. There is still a

roughly 2 hour window of time where we could see some more

robust development, and then hi-res model guidance suggests that

the overnight will be relatively quiet. The setup supports a

strengthening wedge overnight with the parent high situated over

New England and a weak area of low pressure offshore which will

help to support the arrival of northerly low-level flow. This

should bring an area of low clouds in across the region, with

ceilings potentially being quite low. There could also be some

fog/mist, possibly reducing visibility down to 4-6 miles at

times. 



&&



.SHORT TERM /6 AM THURSDAY MORNING THROUGH SATURDAY/...

Thursday, 12Z GFS indicates that a broad H5 trough will ripple 

over the Carolinas and GA, with the axis remaining to the west 

before 0Z. At the sfc, a ridge will remain centered over the 

western to central Carolinas. To the east, a broad trough or 

spot low is expected to remain over the Gulf Stream or east. NHC

has highlighted this feature in their TWO, reducing the 

probability of formation to near 0 percent within the next 48 

hours. This pattern should keep deep instability restricted to 

the Coastal Plain Thursday afternoon. Recent runs of the HREF 

and HRRR keep scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms 

close to the coast, beginning near the Santee River around 18Z, 

zippering down the sea breeze during the rest of the afternoon 

and evening. High temperatures are forecast to range in the mid 

to upper 80s. Convection should dissipate by late evening, with 

some degree of coverage remaining across the Atlantic during the

overnight.



Friday and Saturday, high temperatures are forecast to range in

the mid 80s across the forecast area each afternoon. The CWA 

will remain below a broad weakness of H5 heights. At the sfc, a 

strong summer wedge is forecast to remain ridge across the 

Southeast U.S. The forecast will indicate generally diurnal 

showers and thunderstorms, initiating on the sea breeze each 

day.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Sunday, the sfc wedge will likely breakdown during the daylight

hours. GFS indicates a shortwave may track from SE to NW as the

ridge begins to build over the region. The combination of the 

mid-level disturbance and sea breeze may support likely to 

widespread showers and thunderstorms during the afternoon and 

evening. Forecast soundings indicate the PW are forecast to 

remain around 2 inches. Given deep moisture and moderate 

instability, it is possible that localized excessive rainfall is

possible. High temperatures should peak in the mid 80s.



Monday through the mid-week, h5 ridge should gradually increase

across the Southeast U.S., with heights building to around 595 

DM. As the ridge strengthens through early next week, 

temperatures will gradually warm each day, reaching the low to 

mid 90s by Wednesday. This pattern should yield a typical 

summer-time thunderstorm timing and coverage.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

The 00z TAF period begins with VFR conditions at KCHS, KJZI, and

KSAV. There are some lingering showers near KCHS and KJZI but

this activity should remain away from the terminals as it

dissipates. Also, there is an outflow boundary lingering just

south of KSAV which could provide some focus for shower and

thunderstorm development through the next few hours. However,

current thinking is that anything that does develop will stay

just south and southeast of the terminal. 



Of greater concern for the overnight is the increasing

likelihood for widespread IFR ceilings starting after midnight.

Model guidance is quite aggressive in spreading low ceilings in

as northerly flow arrives with high pressure building in from

the north and northeast. We have timed MVFR ceilings into KCHS

and KJZI by around 04z, lowering to IFR by around 06z. For KSAV,

arrival should be a bit later with IFR conditions timed into the

TAF by 08z. These low ceilings could linger for much of Thursday

morning, likely no improving to VFR until 16-17z. There could be

some fog reducing visibility to around 4-6 miles late tonight,

but the low stratus will be the main driver of flight

categories. Also, there could be shower and thunderstorm

concerns Thursday afternoon and evening, but confidence in

placement and timing is low at this time. 



Extended Aviation Forecast: As a front remains stalled nearby, 

periodic brief flight restrictions will be possible as showers 

and/or thunderstorms develop along this boundary into next week.



&&



.MARINE...

Through Tonight: Northeast/east winds this afternoon will back 

more to the north overnight. Speeds on average will be less than

15 knots, but could briefly be higher in any shower or 

thunderstorm that move offshore later this evening. Seas will be

1-3 feet.



Thursday through Monday: At the sfc, a ridge will remain 

centered over the western to central Carolinas. To the east, a 

broad trough or spot low is expected to remain over the Gulf 

Stream or east. NHC has highlighted this feature in their TWO, 

reducing the probability of formation to near 0 percent within 

the next 48 hours and 30 percent over the next 7 days. WPC sfc 

pressure maps indicate that the spot the low is expected to 

remain off shore off the GA/SC coast. The sfc pattern will 

support steady northeast winds between 10-15 kts, with 

occasional gusts to 20 kts. Winds may decrease and veer from the

west by Sunday. Wave heights should generally range between 2 

to 3 ft.



&&



.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...

We are approaching the next full moon (8/9) and the next lunar

perigee (8/14), which will help to drive astronomical tide

values higher in the coming days. Also, the evening high tide

cycle will be the higher of the 2 daily high tides. At

Charleston, the astronomical evening high tide will rise to just

over 6 ft MLLW starting with the Friday evening high tide.

Observed tide heights could approach the minor coastal flood

threshold as soon as the Thursday evening high tide (near 7 ft

MLLW), with a greater potential for exceeding 7 ft MLLW with the

Friday evening high tide. Coastal Flood Advisories could be

needed, mainly for the Charleston and Colleton county coasts. 



&&



.CHS WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

GA...None.

SC...None.

MARINE...None.



&&



$$



NEAR TERM...BSH

SHORT TERM...NED

LONG TERM...NED

AVIATION...BSH/NED

MARINE...ETM/NED



