2025-08-07T00-02-20-755Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9220","issue":"2025-08-07T00:02:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KFGZ","awipsid":"AFDFGZ"}]
=================================================



707

FXUS65 KFGZ 070002

AFDFGZ



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Flagstaff AZ

502 PM MST Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...High pressure will dominate the region through the

forecast period, resulting in hot daytime temperatures and mostly

dry conditions. Look for slight chances for showers and 

thunderstorms through the weekend. Otherwise, near-critical fire 

weather conditions will persist through Friday before winds begin 

to lighten up over the weekend.



&&



.DISCUSSION...A strong high pressure system aloft was centered 

over New Mexico with a trough over the west coast of the United

States during the afternoon. There will be little change in this 

pattern through Friday. The result will be continued breezy 

daytime winds from the west to southwest each day. The strongest 

winds will be along and north of a Prescott-Flagstaff-Four Corners

line where sustained winds of 10 to 20 mph gusting to 25 to 35 

mph are forecast. South of this line winds will be more like 5 to 

15 mph gusting to 20 mph. The air mass will remain on the dry side

with only slight chances (15% or lower) for showers and 

thunderstorms for most of Arizona. The only exception is the far 

eastern Mogollon Rim and White Mountains areas where showers and 

storms will be more scattered (30% coverage). Hot daytime 

temperatures will continue with an Extreme Heat Warning for the 

lower elevations of the Grand Canyon and Yavapai County.



From Saturday onward...The high center will shift westward and

weaken as the west coast trough heads inland across the Great 

Basin and Rocky Mountains. The high will eventually end up 

centered roughly over Nevada/Utah/northern Arizona. As a result, 

the flow from Saturday onward will be from the west to northwest 

which will maintain the drier air mass across our area. Not much 

changes with this pattern shift as far as precipitation, with 

slight chances continuing but potentially even a downturn in 

activity for the White Mountains(although that isn't reflected in

our current forecast package). Winds will become much lighter as 

the presence of the high centered over the area will result in 

much weaker pressure gradients. Daytime high temperatures may cool

some as the air mass source will be more from over the eastern 

Pacific, but only by about 5 degrees or so. In other words, it 

won't be quite as hot.



&&



.AVIATION...Thursday 07/00Z through Friday 08/00Z...VFR conditions.

Localized FU north of KGCN to KPGA. Isolated -SHRA/-TSRA primarily

in Navajo/Apache counties through 02Z this evening and again after

18Z Thursday. Winds are SW 10-15 kts gusting up to 25 kts, then 

light and VRB after 03Z. SW winds 10-15 kts gusting near 25 kts

will develop again after 17Z Thursday. Gusty and erratic winds 

can be expected near storms.



OUTLOOK...Friday 08/00Z through Sunday 10/00Z...VFR conditions. 

Localized FU north of KGCN to KPGA. Daily chances for -SHRA/-TSRA,

primarily across the higher terrain KFLG-KRQE southward. Winds 

are W-SW winds 10-20 kts gusting up to 30 kts. Winds are light and

VRB during the overnight periods. Gusty and erratic winds can be 

expected near storms.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...Thursday and Friday...Hot, breezy, and mostly dry. 

Near-Critical fire weather conditions towards western Coconino 

county where the winds are strongest. Isolated to scattered 

showers/storms expected primarily along the higher terrain Flagstaff 

and southward each day. Gusty/erratic winds and cloud-to-ground are 

the primary concerns near storms. Winds are southwest 10-20 mph 

gusting up to 30 mph each day. Minimum RH is 10-20% each afternoon.  



Saturday through Monday...Slightly better chances for showers and 

storms, but still expected to stay Flagstaff and southward. The 

primary hazards near storms will be cloud-to-ground lightning and 

gusty/erratic outflow winds. Minimum RH is generally 10-25%. Winds 

will be a touch weaker, west through southwest at 10-15 mph gusting 

to 20-25 mph each day.



&&



.FGZ WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ006-018-037.



&&



$$



PUBLIC...McCollum

AVIATION...JJ

FIRE WEATHER...Lewandowski



For Northern Arizona weather information visit

weather.gov/flagstaff



