FXUS65 KCYS 070002 AAA

AFDCYS



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service Cheyenne WY

602 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Largely hot and dry conditions are expected across the area

  through Friday. Areas of smoke from wildfires to our southwest

  are expected to produce periods of degraded air quality.



- An isolated strong to severe thunderstorm cannot be ruled out

  this afternoon/evening, mainly in the northern NE panhandle 

  and east central WY.



- Red Flag Warnings remain in effect through Friday due to low 

  afternoon relative humidity and poor overnight humidity 

  recoveries.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

Issued at 400 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The powerful ridge over Arizona and New Mexico continues to 

strengthen today, with the latest RAP analysis showing 500-mb 

heights exceeding 600-dm. Limited recycled monsoon moisture 

continues to circulate tightly around the ridge center, but a narrow 

band of very dry air remains stuck over southern Wyoming and western 

Nebraska. Along and west of the Laramie Range, the atmosphere is 

very well-mixed, supporting extremely dry surface conditions. 

Further east, a surface high well to our east nudged against the I-

25 corridor this morning, and remains fairly stubborn heading into 

the afternoon. East winds and higher surface moisture remain 

considerably west of earlier model forecasts, leading to 

temperatures running cooler and dewpoints running higher than 

previous forecasts. Mesoanalysis indicates plentiful instability and 

vertical wind shear in place over the High Plains, but a very strong 

cap is the primary inhibitor for convective activity. The result of 

this is low confidence in any storms developing at all, but if 

storms do manage to develop, they will be able to tap into a potent 

environment and become strong to severe. Storm coverage will not be 

very high, but isolated storms will be capable of developing into 

supercells with large hail and damaging winds possible. 



Meanwhile, satellite observations show the Lee and Elk fires near 

Meeker, Colorado becoming active once again this afternoon, putting 

out a growing smoke plume to the east-northeast. Expect to see 

increasing haze in the Laramie and Cheyenne areas as this plume 

moves into the area. Overnight, expect drainage flow to increase 

surface smoke concentrations across southern Wyoming. As a result, 

an Air Quality Alert has been issued for much of Carbon, Albany, and 

Laramie counties in coordination with the Wyoming DEQ. Thursday will 

be the hottest day of the week as 700-mb temperatures crest around 

+17 to +19C. The surface trough and associated dryline is expected 

to advance well into the Nebraska panhandle, allowing westerly 

downslope winds to spread eastward across the High Plains and boost 

the low-level warmth. Forecast highs are generally within 1 to 3F of 

daily record highs, although no record is explicitly forecast at 

this time. While the boundary layer will be quite dry for the entire 

area, we may see a few isolated high based showers and thunderstorms 

develop in the afternoon hours, mainly in the central and northern 

Nebraska panhandle. Low-end PoPs continue for this area. 



By Friday, an upper level shortwave will begin to encroach on the 

area and suppress the powerful ridge to the south. Cooler and more 

moist air will work into the High Plains (mostly along/north of the 

North Platte River Valley) during the morning hours while points 

around Cheyenne and westward will remain quite dry. The increased 

moisture and expected strong vertical wind shear will possibly allow 

for a few isolated strong to severe thunderstorms. This will also 

bring a little relief from the heat with highs along the US-20 10-

15F cooler than Thursday, while those further south will be slightly 

cooler, but only by perhaps 3-5F.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

Issued at 400 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



The long term period continues to advertise a more active synoptic 

pattern across the region through the weekend. The mid-/upper-levels 

will be characterized by longwave troughing with embedded 

shortwaves/disturbances. Moisture associated with these disturbances 

promote slight chance to chance PoPs (~15% to ~45%) Saturday 

afternoon through Sunday afternoon, mainly east of the Laramie 

Range. On that note, western portions of the County Warning Area 

(CWA) may not see any rain, as precipitation will have to overcome 

drier mid-/lower-level air to reach the ground. Wind speed 

maxima/wind shear associated with these disturbances aloft may 

organize and/or intensify convection, but at this time, the 

probability for widespread severe weather remains low. Subtle 

differences in the timing/evolution of the disturbances remain among 

the global models, but there is general agreement regarding the 

overall synoptic pattern. Temperatures will trend down slightly over 

the weekend -- forecast highs are generally in the mid-70s to mid-

80s and forecast lows are generally in the upper 40s to upper 50s.



The GFS and ECMWF prog the mid-/upper-level trough axis to shift 

east of the CWA on Sunday night/Monday morning, with northwesterly 

flow aloft setting up for Monday and Tuesday. As a result, overall 

drier conditions are expected at the start of next week (PoPs are 

less than ~25% and relegated to the eastern and southern portions of 

the CWA) and afternoon temperatures should return to the mid-80s to 

upper 80s.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 540 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Main forecast challenge for the TAF period will be potential 

for low ceilings/reduced visibility at the Nebraska terminals 

overnight/early tomorrow (Thursday) morning. For this TAF cycle,

have gone with SCT MVFR clouds at all four of these sites from 

09z/10z to 14z, with FEW005 and BR at SNY and AIA given CIG/VIS 

reductions at both sites this morning. VFR conditions are 

expected at the Wyoming terminals through the period, as well as

gusty conditions tomorrow -- winds are forecast to be 13-20 kts

with gusts 20-30 kts.





&&



.CYS WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

WY...Red Flag Warning from noon to 8 PM MDT Thursday for WYZ417-418.

     Red Flag Warning until 6 PM MDT Friday for WYZ419>429.

     Air Quality Alert from midnight tonight to noon MDT Thursday 

     for WYZ105-109>119.

NE...None.



&&



