[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.9258","issue":"2025-08-07T00:04:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS65","cccc":"KPSR","awipsid":"AFDPSR"}]
=================================================



745

FXUS65 KPSR 070004

AFDPSR



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Phoenix AZ

504 PM MST Wed Aug 6 2025



.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.



&&



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Extreme heat will persist the next couple of days with readings

  around 10 degrees above normal and challenging record levels.



- Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect through Friday across 

  the entire area with widespread major to extreme HeatRisk.



- Chances for showers and thunderstorms will gradually increase 

  across eastern Arizona high terrain with the best chances 

  arriving over the weekend.



&&



.SHORT TERM /Today through Friday/...

Early afternoon WV imagery depicts a pronounced subtropical anti-

cyclone stationed over western New Mexico with objective analysis 

suggesting H5 heights flirting with 600dm. Thermal profiles aloft 

are just as anomalous as heights with H7 readings nearing +16C and a 

bubble of H8 temperatures above +30C. With a drier airmass 

entrenched over the forecast area, mechanical mixing will ensure an 

impressively deep boundary layer (likely above 600mb) fully capable 

of tapping these anomalous temperatures aloft. As a result, 

confidence is very good that widespread high temperatures in excess 

of 115F will cover lower elevation communities through Thursday. And 

despite magnitudes solidly above daily records, ensemble guidance 

spread are remarkably narrow offering even more evidence of readings 

yielding widespread major to extreme HeatRisk. 



While near surface moisture remains rather scant by early August 

standards with only 5-6 g/kg mixing ratios, this moisture profile 

has been well mixed through the boundary layer depth such that the 

buoyant hot airmass lifted into higher terrain areas will be capable 

of producing a few deeper convective updrafts. Given the tremendous 

T/Td spreads, limited rainfall may actually reach the ground, but 

dry lightning strikes and gusty outflow winds may locally impact the 

far eastern CWA. 



Otherwise, more robust convection should be confined to northern 

Sonora where 10+ g/kg mixing ratios will be far more conducive for 

organized storms. The general SE steering flow should advect 

midlevel moisture into the CWA such that another round of morning 

ACCAS appears likely both Thursday and Friday morning with 

thermodynamic profiles and model evidence pointing to Friday as 

potentially supportive of a few isolated elevated showers. In fact, 

there are hints that the flow pattern juxtaposed with convective 

systems over Mexico will induce an impressive Gulf surge Thursday 

night resulting in 9-10 g/kg low level moisture influx into south-

central Arizona Friday. This would set the stage for afternoon 

mountain storms descending closer to lower elevation population 

centers, but still encountering notable CinH where multiple, deep 

outflow boundaries would be needed to break the inhibition in lower 

elevations. Nevertheless, the additional moisture influx from this 

convection could further aid boundary layer moistening and increase 

rainfall chances over the weekend. 



&&



.LONG TERM /Saturday through Tuesday/... 

As a trough passes by across Idaho and Montana Friday into Saturday, 

it will help to suppress the ridge over our region leading to H5 

heights between 592-595dm by Saturday, or more in line with around 

the 90th percentile of climatology instead of the record territory. 

The falling heights along with the marginally better moisture should 

bring most areas back into Moderate HeatRisk. However, with forecast 

temperatures still running 4-7 degrees above normal some areas may 

still see Major HeatRisk over the weekend. An extension of the Heat 

Warning may end up being needed for some locations. 



The weakening ridge and the better moisture should also allow for

improved rain chances over the weekend. Uncertainty in the amount

of available moisture is still present leading to somewhat low 

forecast confidence for the weekend PoPs. NBM PoPs seem to be 

overdone considering PWATs max out at 1.3-1.4", so we have lowered

PoPs by around 10-15% leaving 20-30% chances over the eastern 

Arizona higher terrain and 10-15% chances into the south-central 

Arizona lower deserts. 



By Monday, ensembles show the ridge rebuilding once again with the

center potentially setting up directly over Arizona. So far 

guidance shows H5 heights mostly holding between 594-596dm which 

may hold daytime highs mostly between 109-113 degrees early next 

week. Moisture is also expected to lower once again with this 

rebuilding of the high, likely cutting off any lower desert rain 

chances for at least a couple of days. Looking more toward the 

latter half of next week, guidance somewhat agrees on some sort 

of trough passing by to our north potentially lowering heights and

temperatures even further across our region. As far as moisture 

and rain potential later next week, models are a bit more 

optimistic but given how dry this monsoon season has been so far 

its hard to imagine any big changes happening anytime soon. 



&&



.AVIATION...Updated at 0004Z.



South Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; and 

Southeast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:



No major weather issues will exist through the TAF period under 

FEW to SCT passing mid to high cloud decks. Wind directions and 

speeds will continue favoring typical diurnal trends with 

occasional late afternoon/early evening gusts to 20 kts, and 

periods of variability during the mornings. There is potential for

winds to remain westerly through tonight at KPHX and not make the

easterly shift, but confidence is too low to remove the east shift

with this TAF package. Slight reductions in surface visibility 

due to smoke/haze are possible at KIPL, particularly late this 

evening into Thursday morning.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Very hot conditions with near record high temperatures will be 

common through Thursday with modest cooling, albeit still with above 

normal temperatures arriving later in the week. A gradual moisture 

increase may allow some isolated, weak afternoon storms to initially 

form over the high terrain of far eastern districts yielding slight 

chances (~10%) for dry lightning and new wildfire starts the next 

couple days. Winds will follow typical diurnal patterns with 

afternoon upslope gusts to 15-25 mph. Afternoon minRHs between 8-12% 

should increase closer to a 15-20% range later in the week with poor 

overnight recovery improving to fair to good thresholds. As even 

better moisture moves into the area over the weekend, thunderstorm 

coverage should increase with a few storms and gusty outflow 

boundaries potentially reaching into the south-central Arizona lower 

deserts. 



&&



.CLIMATE...



Record high temperatures this week:



Date        Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro

----        -------         ----         ---------

Aug 6     114 in 2023    115 in 1995    114 in 2018

Aug 7     112 in 2012    114 in 1962    117 in 1970

Aug 8     116 in 2012    115 in 1940    115 in 2012

 

&&



.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

AZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530>563.



CA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ560>570.



&&



$$



SHORT TERM...18

LONG TERM...Kuhlman

AVIATION...Benedict/Whittock 

FIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/18 

CLIMATE...18



