751

FXUS63 KARX 070005

AFDARX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service La Crosse WI

705 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...



- Shower and storm chances (20-50% chance) tonight and into Thursday.

  Additional chances for showers and storms for the weekend 

  with highest confidence (50-70% chance) Saturday evening and 

  overnight.



- Gradual warming and becoming increasingly more humid through

  Friday. Heat indices on Friday will reach into the middle to 

  upper 90s for most.



&&



.DISCUSSION...

Issued at 147 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Today - Friday: Gradual Warming with Shower & Storm Chances Thursday



GOES-19 water vapor imagery and 06.15z RAP 500mb heights depicts a 

quasi-zonal synoptic flow pattern over portions of the northern 

plains with an embedded shortwave/MCV over portions of northern IA. 

As we continue through the rest of the afternoon and evening, this 

will continue progressing southeast. Some of these showers 

associated with this system have been noted to have some gusty winds 

with them which may continue in isolated locations through early 

afternoon in northeast IA. 



By the time we head into the overnight hours, a weak axis of 850mb 

moisture transport will allow for dewpoints to increase resulting in 

increasing MLCAPE values of around 1500-2500 J/kg through mid-

morning on Thursday. Initially, convection shown in the CAMs 

develops along this moisture transport axis in southeast 

MN/northeast IA before progressing eastbound throughout the morning. 

The trouble with this is that there still remains substantial 

differences on exact location for where this occurs but confidence 

is fairly high showers/storms will be around with the recent 06.12z 

HREF having high probabilities (60-90% chance) for measurable 

precipitation. As diurnal heating occurs tomorrow, soundings in the 

RAP/HRRR would suggest that a cap that was initially in place would 

erode and allow for storms to trend more surface-based. As a result, 

noticing some increased coverage in CAMs as any convection that does 

develop moves eastbound. The environment for any convection tomorrow 

looks fairly unfavorable as far as severe potential is concerned 

with 0-6km bulk shear values in the 06.15z RAP under 30 kts. 

However, with MUCAPE values encroaching on 4000 J/kg and mid-level 

lapse rates around 7 C/km, would not rule out some small hail and 

gusty winds with any stronger cores, primarily along and east of the 

Mississippi River. Eventually, convective potential diminishes 

significantly towards sunset on Thursday as instability wanes. 



Friday will likely be our warmest day of the next few days as 

deterministic guidance generally agrees on pushing some upper-level 

ridging into the area, allowing for some subsidence and clearing our 

skies out. Consequently, temperatures will respond accordingly with 

median high temperatures in the NBM in the upper 80s to lower 90s 

for Friday. Additionally, with dewpoints still remaining in the 

upper 60s to lower 70s, heat indices will be in the middle to 

upper 90s, with perhaps a few spots reaching the 100 degree 

mark.



This Weekend: Increasing Storm Chances Saturday Evening & Overnight



As we head into the weekend, the next synoptic feature to watch will 

be a broader trough that sets up over the Northern Plains for the 

weekend. As this occurs, a surface frontal boundary pushes southeast 

across portions of the Upper Midwest. Overall, the environment out 

ahead of this boundary across the local area on Saturday evening is 

fairly unstable with modest probabilities (40-60%) in the recent EC 

ensemble for MUCAPE values over 3000 J/kg. The one question with 

this is bulk shear values seem uncertain with probabilities in the 

grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) for 0-500mb bulk shear of 

over 30 kts having a large spread across the local area (10-70% 

chance). This is likely tied to the disagreements on when the wave 

arrives across the deterministic guidance which would determine

how our shear profiles manifest. Regardless of how the severe 

potential manifests, confidence is fairly high that we will get 

some meaningful rainfall as the recent grand ensemble 

(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) has modest probabilities (40-70%) 

for rainfall amounts of 0.25" for Saturday evening and 

overnight. 



Early Next Week: 



Looking towards next work week, the aforementioned broader synoptic 

trough slowly meanders eastbound which correspondingly puts our area 

in zonal flow to start the week. As a result, current inter-quartile 

range for high temperatures in the NBM remain near-normal through 

Tuesday. Have some low-end probabilities for precipitation during 

this period as various ensemble members pivot some pieces of 

shortwave energy through the zonal flow pattern, however the 

resolvability of these features remains low at this time.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 629 PM CDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Potential aviation impacts for the immediate term in the 07.00Z

TAF issuance. Clear skies from central through western Iowa on 

satellite imagery expected to shift east, potentially allowing 

sufficient overnight cooling for low level stratus (IFR/LIFR) in

areas and/or decreased visibilities (MVFR/IFR). Limited 

temporal and spatial concern of 10Z-13Z in parts of southeast 

Minnesota through northeast Iowa into southwest Wisconsin. 

Increased off surface winds limits confidence (<15%) and 

therefore haven't included in TAF or aviation grids. 



Precipitation chances increase through the overnight through

Thursday morning, lingering through Thursday. Overall low

confidence locally due to overnight timing limiting coverage.

Higher confidence in central Wisconsin affecting locally smaller

airport of K82C. IFR visibilities likely where storms do form 

and impact.



VFR expected through Friday. Storm chances return Saturday,

increasing through the morning hours and lingering through the

weekend. Expect aviation impacts later in the weekend.



&&



.ARX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

WI...None.

MN...None.

IA...None.



&&



