=================================================



105

FXUS66 KMTR 070014 AAB

AFDMTR



Area Forecast Discussion...UPDATED

National Weather Service San Francisco CA

514 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New AVIATION...



.KEY MESSAGES...

Updated at 1248 AM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



 - Seasonally warm conditions through the early part of next week.



 - Patches of Moderate HeatRisk Friday through Sunday in the

   interior East and South Bays.



 - Elevated fire weather concerns given the warming and drying

   trend coupled with daily afternoon/evening breezes.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

Issued at 206 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025

(This evening through Thursday)



Your eyes aren't playing tricks on you, those are radar returns

passing through Lake/Colusa counties on KMUX radar this afternoon.

They're associated with the swath of clouds noted on the AM 

discussion that are still sweeping through NorCal. Despite being 

picked up on radar, no precip is reaching the ground per webcams.



The more noteworthy story this afternoon is the 24 hour

temperature change. We've been messaging a warm up for a few days

now and today it finally arrived. Any location outside of marine

influence has recorded warmer temps this afternoon. In fact,

downtown San Francisco hit 72 degrees. The last time the city

eclipsed 70 was July 10. There are other stations around the

Bay Area that haven't been this warm since July 10 as well.



This evening through Thursday: The longwave pattern this afternoon

continues to show big upper level high over anchored over NM and

another upper level high over the EPac. Sandwiched in between is

an upper level trough over the West Coast (parent low still over

BC). The upper level trough will deepen slightly over the region

tonight and Thursday. It will help to keep the marine layer along

the coast and locally inland. Some of the guidance actually 

deepens the marine layer a tad late tonight and early Thursday. 

The trough will also help pause the temperature warm up. While 

technically a few far interior locations will warm another 1-3 

degrees, but most of the forecast will be just as warm as today or

1-3 degrees cooler. Minor changes in the sensible weather.



As noted before, could still see some haze/smoke nudge into

interior San Benito from the Gifford Fire to the south on

Thursday.



&&



.LONG TERM...

Issued at 206 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025

(Thursday night through next Tuesday)



The "pause" quickly comes to an end by Friday as the parent low

and associated up trough move east. Temperatures will rebound on

Friday with maxes exceeding seasonal averages. The warmer weather

will persist through the weekend and into next Monday. Only subtle

day to day changes with overall sensible weather. Impacts to the 

overall population will be Minor to Moderate HeatRisk. Simply put,

be smart with the warmer weather: take breaks, hydrate, plan 

ahead if recreating, and look before you lock your vehicle.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 508 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



VFR prevails at all TAF sites as of this writing, however,

confidence is increasing (though still low to moderate) that 

we'll see more in the way of MVFR and IFR cigs compared to the 

last few mornings. Patchy IFR stratus currently lurks just 

offshore and I anticipate intermittent cigs will pester KHAF 

through late-evening before a more widespread stratus blankets

coastal aerodromes after midnight. North Bay terminals are 

anticipated to be infiltrated by a mix of MVFR and IFR cigs, with 

areas such as KSTS experiencing IFR vsby due to influences from 

the Russian River. It's quite possible that TEMPO groups may be 

more appropriate here, but confidence wanes in the exact duration 

of poor flying conditions. Confidence is a little higher at KOAK 

that a more prolonged MVFR stratus event near/just after sunrise. 

VFR is advertised at KLVK and KSJC, though there are some hints 

that there is a 2-3 hour window of potential MVFR/IFR at KSJC.

Elevated winds with gusts near 20 knots are forecast this

afternoon for areas across SF/San Pablo Bays. Onshore winds abate

some overnight, before ramping up again (and increasing in 

strength) on Thursday afternoon. 



Vicinity of SFO...VFR currently with low to medium confidence in

intermittent MVFR cigs around 12 UTC Thursday. In the meantime, 

westerly breezes are anticipated this afternoon, though peak gusts

are anticipated to remain around 25 knots. Winds may gusts closer

to 30 knots on Thursday afternoon. A slight minority of guidance 

offers an arrival of stratus closer to 10 UTC, but given the 

current satellite presentation and recent trends, the current TAF 

resides with the model consensus of 12 UTC. At any rate, loss of 

VAPS does seem possible during the morning push. Cigs are 

anticipated to erode by 16 UTC, though similar to the onset time, 

there are a few models that offer a later clearing time. At this 

time, confidence isn't terribly high that we're contending with an

all day event and by at least 18 UTC, a return to VAPS seems 

highly probable. Increased winds through the San Bruno Gap seem 

plausible on Thursday afternoon with the probability of nearing 30

knots gusts near 30-40% at KSFO. 



SFO Bridge Approach...Impacts or potential loss of VAPS around 12

UTC, with clearing anticipated around 16 UTC. Confidence is medium

due to timing uncertainty.



Monterey Bay Terminals...Low to medium confidence cig/visibility

forecast. Stratus is just offshore and is anticipated to build 

over the next 2 to 4 hours. Occasional surges in the stratus 

onshore are anticipated through at least 05/06 UTC before a more 

prolonged period of IFR and LIFR cigs impact both sites shortly 

thereafter. Because the marine layer remains slightly compressed,

IFR to LIFR visibility may also accompany the reduced cigs. 

Inclusion of FG may be needed depending on trends. Stratus will be

a little later to dissipate late Thursday morning, with VFR 

by the afternoon. Occasional periods of afternoon IFR/MVFR cannot

be discounted, however. 



&&



.MARINE...

(Tonight through next Monday)

Issued at 307 PM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Expect widespread fresh to strong northwesterly gusts tonight and

into Thursday. A coastal jet looks to develop off of Point Reyes

and off of the Big Sur coast which will bring near gale force

gusts. Winds diminish beginning Friday with a gentle to moderate

breeze prevailing through the weekend. Moderate seas persist

through the forecast period, with a brief period of 10 to 12 foot

wave heights in the northern outer waters on Friday.



&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 937 AM PDT Wed Aug 6 2025



After coordination calls this morning with fire partners we'll be

hoisting a headline for the afternoon fire weather forecast. The

headline will mainly focus on the increasing temperatures coupled

with the lower humidity Friday and over the weekend. The greatest

areas of concern will be the higher terrain and interior 

locations. Low elevations will see better humidity recovery, but 

higher terrain will be persistently dry. Daily sea breeze pushes 

occuring during peak heating will also elevate concerns.



Taking a look at the Hot Dry Windy index also highlights concerns

pushing values into the 95th percentile later in the weekend and

early next week.



