=================================================



210

FXUS65 KBOU 070021

AFDBOU



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Denver/Boulder CO

621 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.KEY MESSAGES...  

 

- Critical fire weather conditions for mountains and high valleys

  through at least Friday.



- Hot and dry conditions persist through Friday. Highs on Thursday

  should be near or above 100F across the lower elevations.



- Some relief this weekend with a chance of storms entering the

  forecast. 



&&



.DISCUSSION /Through Wednesday/...

Issued at 151 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Heat and fire weather continue to be the main headlines in the

forecast. Unsurprisingly, it's another hot one out there today,

despite some cloud cover across the I-25 corridor. Denver (or at

least KDEN) has already managed to hit a 97F high today, with

similar temperatures between Boulder-Greeley. Where cloud cover

and a few weak showers have developed in the southwest metro,

temperatures have largely held on to the low/mid 90s. No

significant changes are anticipated this afternoon with just a few

gusty showers continuing, mainly along the Palmer Divide.



Tomorrow will likely be the warmest day of the week, with

temperatures in the upper 90s to low 100s over the lower

elevations. There is some uncertainty with how much smoke/cloud

cover could impact temperatures. Still, statistical and

deterministic guidance is bullish on temperatures... and even

models that include smoke into their radiation scheme (HRRR) still

have temperatures near/above 100F. At this point, I'd give it

about a 50/50 shot of seeing another 100F day in Denver.



The upper level pattern won't change much for Friday, with above

normal temperatures and continued fire weather concerns. An

approaching trough axis should pass close enough to the region to

usher in a cool front by Friday evening or Friday night, though

this should only bring a little relief from the heat for this 

weekend. However, there should be a modest increase in moisture

across the plains... leading to a better chance of showers/storms

on Saturday and Sunday. There isn't much of a signal for

widespread wetting rainfall... though ensembles hint at Sunday

having better potential for precipitation.



The extended period doesn't offer much of a signal for moisture,

as ridging is expected to redevelop across the desert southwest.

This ridge should weaken and drift towards the forecast area by

mid/late next week, but ensemble guidance generally favors near/above

normal temperatures and below normal moisture until we get closer

to next weekend.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS through 00Z Friday/...

Issued at 540 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Early evening virga showers are clearing out across the plains,

with a few showers still present across the Palmer Divide. The

main threat for any additional microbursts passing over the TAF

sites is diminishing quickly, but outflow boundaries visible on

radar still leave a threat for some wind shifts until roughly

0Z/1Z this evening. 



There is ongoing potential for impacts to slant-range vis with

multiple fires continuing to burn in western Colorado, bringing

smoke into the urban corridor of the Front Range. Hi-res guidance

continues to keep the highest smoke concentrations to the north 

of all TAF sites through this evening, so any impacts would likely

be for any approaches from the north. For tomorrow morning,

guidance shows an increase in vertically integrated smoke over the

northeast plains but keeps the highest surface smoke 

concentrations generally over eastern Larimer and Weld Counties 

near sunrise. With surface smoke expected to stay to the north, 

went ahead and kept FU out of the TAF for now, but with the 

vertically integrated smoke increasing, there could be impacts to 

slant-range vis for the morning rush, especially for any 

approaches from the north and east. A swath of higher 

concentrations of smoke aloft will pass across the TAF sites 

through the late morning, but surface smoke should remain minimal. 



Westerly winds are expected for most of the day tomorrow across

the TAF sites, increasing between 18/19Z with some 20-25kt gusts

possible. Confidence is too low at this time to include any

mention in the TAF of gusty outflows from virga showers for the

afternoon, but this may need to be added to upcoming TAFs. 







&&



.FIRE WEATHER...

Issued at 151 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Elevated to critical fire weather conditions have developed across

the higher elevations in the last hour or two, and should continue

through the evening. Though there has been some spotty critical

conditions across portions of the mountains and foothills outside

of the Red Flag Warning, we elected to keep the RFW as is, with

Middle Park/North Park still the most likely area to see

widespread critical conditions.



Thursday will see similar conditions, with perhaps just a little

bit more moisture across the higher elevations. 500mb flow

approaches 35-45kt by the early afternoon hours, and there should

be widespread surface gusts of 25-40 mph across Middle/North Park.

A Red Flag Warning was issued for Fire Zones 211/213/217 for most

of the afternoon and evening hours.



Friday should again see more wind across the high country as the

flow aloft continues to strengthen. GEFS Hot Dry Windy Index

values approach the 95th percentile across the mountain valleys

into the Front Range foothills. Though there is some uncertainty

with fuel status there (RAWS ERC values are far lower than west 

of the divide), we went ahead with a Fire Weather Watch for the

foothills and the rest of the higher elevations for Friday given

the signal for both wind/RH to reach critical thresholds.



We should see gradual improvement this weekend into next week,

though there looks to be little to no moisture across the high

country... meaning a return to elevated/critical fire weather

conditions would be plausible, if not likely, if the flow aloft

restrengthens.



&&



.BOU WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Red Flag Warning until 8 PM MDT this evening for COZ211-213-217-

218.



Red Flag Warning from noon to 8 PM MDT Thursday for COZ211-213-

214-217.



Fire Weather Watch from Friday afternoon through Friday evening 

for COZ211>218.



Heat Advisory from 10 AM to 7 PM MDT Thursday for COZ038>040.



&&



$$



DISCUSSION...Hiris

AVIATION...Bonner

FIRE WEATHER...Hiris



