268

FXUS62 KRAH 070025

AFDRAH



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Raleigh NC

825 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Canadian high pressure that will extend across the Middle Atlantic 

this week will be dampened and dammed over the Carolinas by 

disturbances in very moist, southwesterly flow aloft.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH TONIGHT/...

As of 315 PM Wednesday...



Minor discussion update to expand the Flood Watch into Halifax

and Edgecombe counties. Also, the western Piedmont was trimmed 

from the Flood Watch as moderate to heavy rainfall has ended in 

this area and scattered to widespread flash flooding is no 

longer expected.



Previous forecast as of 145 PM follows.. 



*  Flood Watch in effect through midnight Thursday. There is a 

   Slight Risk for excessive rainfall across most of central NC. 

   Scattered flooding is possible, especially in urban, flood-prone 

   locations. 



Aloft, a trough over the TN/OH Valleys will continue to shift sewd 

to over the Appalachians, with a s/w embedded in the base of the 

trough crossing the Appalachians this eve/tonight. Another, weaker 

disturbance may track ewd across the Southeast US tonight. At the 

surface, Canadian high pressure will remain in place, extending swwd 

across the mid-Atlantic into central NC. A quasi-stationary wedge 

front near I-95 today, with a weak low analyzed near the NC/SC 

coastal border this afternoon should slide swwd out of the area this 

eve/tonight as the wedge front collapses sewd and toward the coast. 

This low may result in increased convective activity across the 

Sandhills and srn/cntl Coastal Plain through this evening. 

Otherwise, the area of moderate to heavy rainfall continues north of 

the wedge boundary, the heaviest rain associated with the 

convectively amplified disturbance aloft, which is lifting newd 

across the Foothills/Piedmont. This area of rain may will exacerbate 

flooding portions of the ern Piedmont near the Triangle which have 

already received a significant amount of rain and where flash 

flooding is occurring. While coverage and intensity may wane after 

the disturbances exit the area, patchy/scattered rain/showers may 

continue through tonight, especially as the s/w aloft moves across 

the mtns. 



Temperatures: Highs today similar to yesterday, ranging from 

mid/upper 60s north to low/mid 80s SE, with a very sharp gradient 

between the two ends, located generally along the i-95 corridor 

invof the wedge front. Lows tonight should also be similar to last, 

low 60s north to upper 60s SE.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/...

As of 1230 PM Wednesday...



* Rain chances continue mainly across the east. 

* Well below normal temperatures. 



Upper level trough will slowly shifts across the Mid-Atlantic region 

Thursday with an abundance of moisture along the coast. As it slowly 

shifts off coast by Friday, a strong upper level ridge is expected 

to build in with drier air and lower PWs. At the surface, At the 

surface, cool high pressure off the NE US coast will continue to 

bring northeast flow. Temperatures are expected to be in the mid 70s 

NW to low 80s SE. A week low pressure system off the coast of the SE 

US still has NHCs attention and they are monitoring for tropical 

depression development. The system is expected to stay offshore, but 

will influence another round of heavy showers and storms Thursday 

mainly across eastern portions of our region. WPC has much of the 

region (minus portions of the Triad) in a Marginal risk for heavy 

rainfall and flash flooding. The threat for heavy rainfall along 

with the amounts expected tomorrow, are significantly less than they 

are today. As the weak surface high helps push the trough offshore, 

and the low also shifts east, chance of precip is expected to wane 

off by late evening into the overnight hours.  As the atmosphere 

dries out and high pressure builds in, NE flow  will filter in 

overnight lows in the low to mid 60s.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

As of 100 PM Wednesday... 



* Below normal temperatures become near normal by early next 

  week.

* Expect mostly dry conditions over the weekend. 

* Increase in daily afternoon showers and storms next week.



Upper level ridge building in across the region over the weekend 

will weaken as a weak trough swings to the north early next week. At 

the surface, the low that NHC is still investigating off the coast 

(40% chance for development) will slowly shift NE on Friday into 

Saturday. NE flow from the system is expected to bring some 

afternoon showers and storms with the higher chance for precip along 

and east of US-1 corridor. WPC has much of the Coastal Plain in a 

Marginal Risk for flash flooding as a few heavy bands from the 

system could move onshore. By Saturday morning some drier air will 

move into the region but if the system lingers just off the coast we 

could expect another round of showers and storms especially along 

and east of I-95 corridor. For now have 20-40% PoPs over the Coastal 

Plain and Sandhills regions. Will have to keep looking and the 

trends of the location of the low for an upward or downward trend of 

PoPs for Saturday afternoon/evening. The general consensus is that 

high pressure will build in the region and the low will continuously 

shift to the northeast and a majority of Central NC will be dry 

Saturday and Sunday. Over the weekend high temperatures will be 

increasing into the low low 80s NW to upper 80s SE. 



Monday into the workweek the trough will slide north of the region 

as a weak ridge builds in from the south. Isolated showers and storm 

chances will increase each afternoon with diurnal heating. 

Temperatures will range from the mid to upper 80s with a few warmer 

spots in the low 90s by mid week. Lows will range from upper 60s to 

low 70s.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

As of 830 PM Wednesday...



The steadier rain has shifted into the far eastern counties. 

However, periods of light rain and drizzle will likely persist over 

overnight, resulting in deteriorating aviation conditions with 

ceilings lowering to LIFR/IFR. 



Gradual improvement is expected during the day Thursday, with 

ceilings lifting to MVFR by the afternoon. Scattered showers will

remain possible through the period.   



Outlook: Prolonged northeasterly flow will persist through at least 

Sat, during which time flight restrictions mostly from ceilings will 

be lowest each night-morning, with diurnal improvement to MVFR or 

low VFR each afternoon. A chance of rain/showers will also exist 

throughout the forecast period.



&&



.RAH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

Flood Watch until midnight EDT tonight for NCZ007>011-023>028-

040>043-075>078-084>086-088-089.



&&



$$



