[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.358","issue":"2025-08-07T00:28:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS62","cccc":"KJAX","awipsid":"AFDJAX"}]
=================================================



456

FXUS62 KJAX 070028

AFDJAX



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Jacksonville FL

828 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



...New UPDATE, AVIATION...



.UPDATE...

Issued at 825 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



For the latest NE FL and SE GA Daily Key Messages please visit:

https://www.weather.gov/media/jax/briefings/nws-jax-briefing.pdf



Much of the convection has diminished this evening with mainly 

scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms at 8 pm. Similar 

thinking as before, and appears that there could be scattered 

showers and perhaps a thunderstorm overnight due to the diffuse 

frontal boundary over far southeast GA and northeast FL. The best 

chance of additional precip after midnight will likely be over 

southeast GA, along the coastal areas north of St Augustine, and 

the coastal waters. Main updates for rest of tonight were to adjust

the POPs and cloud cover based latest data and forecasts. 



&&



.NEAR TERM...

Issued at 105 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



For the latest NE FL and SE GA Daily Key Messages please visit:

https://www.weather.gov/media/jax/briefings/nws-jax-briefing.pdf



Convection will continue through the afternoon, and dissipate 

during the evening. With a stalled frontal boundary in place, 

showers could linger through the night. Lows Tonight ranging from 

the lower 70s inland to the mid 70s along the coast.



&&



.SHORT TERM...

(Thursday through Friday night)

Issued at 105 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Diurnal bouts of showers and storms are expected to form on

Thursday and Friday as a mid level trough stretches ahead of the

mostly stationary frontal boundary over the region. Potential for

numerous to widespread storms building during this period with the

primary drivers for developments through the end of the week

occurring along the sea breeze boundaries and areas of

convergence. Higher rainfall amounts anticipated over inland areas

on Thursday. High temperatures through the end of the week will

rise into the mid to upper 80s primarily for areas along and north

of the I-10 corridor and in the lower 90s for north central

Florida.



&&



.LONG TERM...

(Saturday through next Wednesday)

Issued at 105 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Pattern of daily showers and thunderstorms will continue through

the weekend with rainfall amounts lessening through the course of

the forecast period with a south-easterly prevailing developing by

the beginning of next week as high pressure ridging extends

towards the Florida peninsula from out of the Atlantic.

Temperatures will experience a warming trend through the weekend

and into next week with daily high temps expected to rise into the

mid 90s before midweek.



&&



.AVIATION...

(00Z TAFS)

Issued at 825 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



Some VCSH in the area around GNV to SSI and points to the

northwest. Brief vsby restriction to 5-6SM possible over the next

1-3 hours. The showers should largely dissipate by about 05z. 

Some low clouds also in the area this evening with a brief MVFR 

cig possible through 06z. Patchy inland fog may produce restrictions

at KVQQ toward dawn Thursday. Low stratus is expected to develop 

over a good part of southeast GA overnight into Thursday morning, 

which could produce restrictions at IFR at KSSI. Convective 

chances will increase through the morning hours Thursday, with 

greater chances during the afternoon hours.



&&



.MARINE...

Issued at 105 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



A frontal boundary will remain over the region through Thursday. 

This front will then sink south across central FL Friday through the 

early part of next week. This boundary will result in unsettled 

weather throughout this period, with daily rounds of showers and 

thunderstorms.



The National Hurricane Center is monitoring an area that may become 

a tropical cyclone near the Southeastern US coast late this week 

into this weekend. Continue to monitor NHC forecasts through the 

week.



Rip Currents: Moderate through Thursday



&&



