Riverton Aviation Discussion

Issued at 2231 UTC Wednesday, August  6, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 420 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025

Gusty west-southwest winds of 15-25 kts will be decreasing by 

sunset for all TAF sites. Mostly clear skies will prevail 

overnight, with partly cloudy skies over some of northwest WY in

the mid-level southwest flow. VFR conditions are expected at 

all TAF sites through the forecast period though there is a very

small chance of showers across northwest WY (KJAC toward KCOD) 

Thursday afternoon. The surface pressure gradient tightens 

across WY Thursday as a system moves from western MT toward

southeastern MT Thursday into Friday morning. West-southwest 

winds will be stronger Thursday afternoon for all of the TAF 

sites, with 12-20 kt sustained winds and 20-30 kt gusts. 

Localized 25g35kt winds are possible. The stronger winds again 

will decrease by sunset Thursday evening, though breezy 

conditions may continue through 08/04Z. Clouds will increase 

from NW to SE late Thursday evening as the weather system drags 

a cold front across the area. KCOD will first see winds shift 

to the north before 08/06Z.

Please see the Aviation Weather Center and/or CWSU ZDV and ZLC for 

the latest information on smoke, icing, and turbulence

forecasts.



Cheyenne Aviation Discussion

Issued at 0003 UTC Thursday, August  7, 2025



.AVIATION /00Z TAFS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY/...

Issued at 540 PM MDT Wed Aug 6 2025

Main forecast challenge for the TAF period will be potential 

for low ceilings/reduced visibility at the Nebraska terminals 

overnight/early tomorrow (Thursday) morning. For this TAF cycle,

have gone with SCT MVFR clouds at all four of these sites from 

09z/10z to 14z, with FEW005 and BR at SNY and AIA given CIG/VIS 

reductions at both sites this morning. VFR conditions are 

expected at the Wyoming terminals through the period, as well as

gusty conditions tomorrow -- winds are forecast to be 13-20 kts

with gusts 20-30 kts.





