519

FXAK68 PAFC 070031

AFDAFC



Southcentral and Southwest Alaska Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Anchorage AK

431 PM AKDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SHORT TERM FORECAST SOUTHCENTRAL ALASKA (Days 1 through 3: 

Wednesday through Friday evening)...



The upper-level ridge continues its slow progression eastward 

this afternoon ahead of a potent front in the Southwestern Gulf. 

Kodiak Island continues to see moderate to heavy rainfall which 

looks to continue through tonight as the front progresses 

northwards through the western Gulf. The coastal ridge building 

ahead of the front coupled with a thermal trough over the Interior

has allowed for the development of southerly gap winds this 

afternoon and through this evening. Hot, dry, windy conditions are

expected to continue through this evening for the Copper Valley 

as temperatures climb into the upper 70s, relative humidities fall

into the 20 percent range, and southerly winds of 10 to 20 mph 

with gusts up to 30 mph. A Red Flag Warning remains in effect 

through 9 PM this evening for the Central Copper River Basin as 

rapid ignition, growth, and spread of fires remains possible. 



The forecast remains on track with the well-advertised frontal 

system moving from the western Gulf this evening to the northern 

Gulf Thursday morning. With previous connections to the tropics, 

this system continues to carry abundant moisture with Kodiak 

Island likely picking up another 1 to 1.5 inches of rain along the

eastern shores and 2 to 2.5 inches of rain in the mountains 

through Thursday morning in addition to the rainfall already seen

this afternoon. The Southcentral mainland is beginning to see the

effects of the front this afternoon as mainly clear skies 

throughout the day today across Cook Inlet and the Kenai Peninsula

are beginning to become overspread with increasing cloud cover 

as well as the first radar returns showing up over the southern 

Kenai Peninsula. As the front moves toward the Southcentral coast 

by Thursday morning, rain will overspread Cook Inlet and across 

the Anchorage Bowl and Mat-Su Valleys. A persistent southeasterly 

cross-barrier flow aloft will allow for some downsloping along the

lee of the Kenai and Chugach Mountains; however, weak waves 

embedded in the upper- levels and moving over these areas should 

allow for periods of very light rain through Friday from Kenai 

north to Palmer. The heaviest rain will be along the eastern 

Kenai Peninsula / western Prince William Sound as the 

southeasterly flow drives copious moisture into the coastal 

mountains. In addition to the steady rainfall, widespread easterly

gales are expected along the front as it lifts north through 

Thursday morning. High- end gale-force winds with occasional 

storm- force gusts are also expected through Kennedy Entrance and 

across the Barren Islands this afternoon and evening through 

Thursday morning. 



Steady rains look to continue into Saturday along the immediate 

coast as the front stalls and weakens. Scattered showers will 

remain possible for interior location as multiple upper-level 

waves continue to lift north over the region. Models do diverge by

Saturday with the timing and placement of a quick-moving north 

Pacific low entering the Gulf over the weekend, though conditions 

are likely to remain generally unsettled across Southcentral 

through the weekend.



-JH/TM



&&

.SHORT TERM FORECAST SOUTHWEST ALASKA/BERING SEA/ALEUTIANS 

(Days 1 through 3: Today through Friday night)...



Heavy rainfall associated with the remnants of ex-Typhoon Krosa 

has lifted across the Alaska Peninsula and into Bristol Bay and 

the Kuskokwim Delta. Largely light rainfall rates over Southwest 

with heavy rain showers over Bristol Bay are expected to continue

tonight into Thursday morning. Lightning activity in the form of 

isolated thunderstorms persists south of the Alaska Peninsula, in 

the maritime space south of Kodiak Island, but some may persist 

briefly over the southern extent of Kodiak Island. These isolated 

thunderstorms to diminish this evening.



Cool and wet conditions push north across Southwest Alaska

tonight, keeping temperatures capped in the upper 50s to mid 60s 

for Thursday as the front lifts slowly northward. By Thursday 

night, the front weakens and precipitation coverage begins to 

diminish significantly. The Bering Sea low exits to the North 

Pacific on Thursday and northerly winds overspread much of the 

Bering Sea. Northerly winds will not be accompanied by any 

meaningful push of cooler air initially, which will mean

widespread stratus and areas of fog will continue to persist 

across the region through late this workweek. Temperatures over 

Southwest Alaska rebound a bit on Friday as the influence of low 

pressure wanes and drier, sunnier conditions prevail. By Friday 

night, confidence is growing towards a wave dropping in from the 

north into the interior Kuskokwim Delta and/or Lower Kuskokwim

Valley. This may re-initiate rainfall over the area late Friday

night, and persisting across the region through Saturday. The

southern extent of this wave does not look as impressive,

suggesting Bristol Bay to remain relatively dry early this

weekend. 



-CL



&&





.LONG TERM FORECAST (Days 4 through 7: Sunday through Wednesday)...



An upper-level trough originating from the Arctic digs across the 

southern half of mainland Alaska on Sunday as a low moves 

northeastward across the Gulf of Alaska. The result of both of these 

systems is a day of widespread rain across much of southern AK.

Model spread and thus lack of forecast confidence increases 

significantly by and after late Sunday, with the ECMWF keeping the 

trough an open wave and having it lift quickly into Yukon. 

Meanwhile, GFS and Canadian models and ensemble means advertise a 

deeper, slower, and closed-off low for the area. Upper-level ridging 

then amplifies across Southcentral by Tuesday in wake of the trough. 

The ECMWF, the preferred model into next week, does have a closed 

low moving under the ridge and through the Gulf late Monday into 

Tuesday. 



High pressure prevails across much of the eastern Bering into the YK 

Delta from late Sunday into the start of the next work week. The 

ECMWF has a more significant low developing across the North Pacific 

and moving northward into the central Aleutians and into the eastern 

Bering, this system may bring strong winds to those locations should 

it materialize.



The bottom line with all these features is that an active weather 

pattern will continue from the Bering Sea through the Gulf and into 

Southcentral AK. Rain is likely across much of southern AK Sun/Mon 

before drying out with a ridge and then possibly returning to 

coastal locations by Tuesday, though uncertainty is large with 

timing given the spread in features. The Bering remains active, 

particularly the central and eastern Aleutians, as a series of 

troughs rotate across. Temperatures across much of southern AK will 

be on the cool side Sunday and Monday before warming to or above 

average Tuesday and Wednesday. 



-Brown



&&



.AVIATION...



PANC...VFR conditions will persist. Southerly Turnagain Arm winds 

with gusts up to 30 mph will continue through the rest of the

evening. Winds gradually decrease and become northerly after 10Z 

tonight. Rain is expected to move over the terminal tomorrow 

morning but southeasterly winds across the Chugach mountains,

downsloping into Anchorage, will help dry the surface Thursday

afternoon.





&&





