[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.451","issue":"2025-08-07T00:32:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KBTV","awipsid":"AFDBTV"}]
=================================================



550

FXUS61 KBTV 070032

AFDBTV



Area Forecast Discussion

National Weather Service Burlington VT

832 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Widely scattered showers and thunderstorms will dissipate this 

evening with areas of patchy fog possible after midnight in the 

protected valleys. Additional terrain driven showers and 

thunderstorms are expected again on Thursday afternoon with 

temperatures warming into the mid 70s to lower 80s. Much warmer 

temperatures return for the weekend into early next week with highs 

into the mid 80s to mid 90s anticipated by Sunday and continuing for 

early next week.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY NIGHT/... Near term challenge wl 

be fog potential tonight, followed by areal coverage of 

showers/storms on Thurs. GOES 19 water vapor shows mid lvl 

moisture advecting acrs central/southern VT, while llvl flow 

around sfc high pres is helping to enhance moisture along the 

Green Mtn spine. Any lingering showers/storms wl dissipate by 

00z this evening with loss of sfc heating/instability. Greatest 

probability of fog wl be in areas that receive rainfall this 

aftn, which include the valleys of the northern Dacks and parts 

of central/southern VT. Setup for fog is not ideal with sounding

data showing some 925mb to 700mb moisture and winds in the 

975mb to 925mb layer of 10 to 20 knots, which could limit areal 

coverage. Also, temps might not reach cross over values in areas

that don't receive rainfall. Lows near 50F SLK to l/m 60s CPV 

near Lake Champlain.



For Thursday very little change is anticipated with slightly more 

coverage in showers/storms anticipated. Once again upper lvl forcing 

is minimal, so activity wl be mostly trrn/lake breeze driven, but 

moisture/instability parameters are slightly better. Sfc based CAPE 

values are in the 1000 to 1500 J/kg, while PW values are btwn 1.40 

and 1.60". The flow with high pres overhead is very weak with 700mb 

winds of 5 knots and 500mb winds of only 10 to 15 knots, so storm 

movement wl be driven off outflow boundaries. Areas of localized 

heavy rainfall wl be possible given the very slow storm motions of 5 

to 10 knots. Progged 925mb temps btwn 17-19C support highs mid 70s 

to l/m 80s.



&&



.SHORT TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT/...

As of 831 PM EDT Wednesday...An isolated threat for showers/storms

are possible again on Friday aftn, but given minimal forcing the

areal coverage wl be limited and mainly confined to the trrn. Have

continued with 15 to 20% pops on Friday aftn. Progged 925mb temps

warm another 1 to 2 degrees with values in the 20 to 22C range,

supporting highs upper 70s to mid 80s. Lows generally in the mid 50s

to mid 60s with some patchy fog possible on Thurs night and less

likely on Fri night due to slightly stronger 975mb to 925mb flow of

10 to 20 knots.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...

As of 831 PM EDT Wednesday...Forecaster confidence is increasing for

an extended period of much above normal temps with another North

Country heat wave likely from Sunday thru Tues anticipated in the

valleys. NWS heat risk shows moderate to major category is expected

acrs our cwa, with greatest risk over the SLV/CPV. Large scale

trends support mid/upper lvl ridge building directly overhead with

comfortable humidity values to start, but as sfc high pres shifts to

our east, south/southwest winds develop and advect in higher sfc

dwpts with increasing humidity values. The greatest combination of

heat/humidity looks to be Monday/Tues of next week, where valley

locations wl come close to heat advisory criteria. NBM probabilistic

data shows 85% to 90% of max high >90F on Monday/Tues acrs the CPV

and parts of the SLV, while slightly cooler temps are expected in

the mtns. Early indications on apparent temps off the NBM show

values mostly in the upper 80s to l/m 90s with highest values in the

CPV urban area. In addition, this wl be a cumulative event over 3 to

4 days long with overnight lows only in the mid 60s to lower 70s,

especially by early next week. Unfortunately the threat for a

widespread wetting rainfall over the next 4 to 7 days is very low.

Chances for showers/storms increase late Tues into Weds of next week

associated with a decaying boundary approaching our region. Have

kept pops at NBM values of 20 to 35%.



&&



.AVIATION /01Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

Through 00Z Friday...VFR conditions should persist for much of the 

evening before patchy fog develops later in the night. It will 

likely reach MPV for some time while it is possible at SLK. 

Probabilities of fog at MPV are maybe 75 percent while they are 

maybe 40 percent at SLK, though the fog may go in and out a few 

times at SLK if it does form. Some low stratus is also possible

at MPV unrelated to the fog. Any fog lifts not long after 

sunrise and VFR conditions should prevail into the early 

afternoon. Winds should remain under 10 KTs, changing from 

variable and terrain driven tonight to southerly/southwesterly 

tomorrow.



Outlook...



Thursday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Friday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Friday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Saturday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Saturday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Sunday: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Sunday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.

Monday: VFR. NO SIG WX.



&&



.EQUIPMENT...

KCXX will be down until at least Thursday afternoon due to a

power supply failure. The part has been ordered and should

arrive by late Thursday morning. Our outstanding ET staff is hoping

to have the part installed and the radar working by mid

afternoon on Thursday if all goes well.



&&



.BTV WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...

VT...None.

NY...None.



&&



$$

SYNOPSIS...Taber

NEAR TERM...Taber

SHORT TERM...Taber

LONG TERM...Taber

AVIATION...Myskowski

EQUIPMENT...WFO BTV



