[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.534","issue":"2025-08-07T00:38:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KAKQ","awipsid":"AFDAKQ"}]
=================================================



634

FXUS61 KAKQ 070038

AFDAKQ



AREA FORECAST DISCUSSION

National Weather Service Wakefield VA

838 PM EDT Wed Aug 6 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Strong high pressure over Quebec will build east to southeast 

and become centered across New England by tonight and Thursday,

slowly drifting off the coast on Friday. Meanwhile, a trough of

low pressure remains to the lee of the southern Appalachians 

today, becoming absorbed by low pressure off the southeast coast

by Thursday. The low pressure system slowly lifts northeast, staying

well off the Carolina coast into the first part of the weekend.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

As of 340 PM EDT Wednesday...



Key Messages:



- Potential for locally heavy rainfall continues into tonight and 

Thursday morning. A Flood Watch is in effect for portions of 

southern VA through 8 AM Thursday.



- Higher coverage of showers persists through late Thursday morning, 

followed by a lower coverage by Thursday afternoon and evening. 



Anomalously strong high pressure (~1033 mb) remains positioned 

over eastern Quebec and New England this afternoon, with a weak 

coastal trough underneath the sfc high offshore of the NC/SC 

coast. An upper- level trough axis extends from the Great Lakes 

into the OH Valley. Cooler temperatures prevail with upper 60s 

to lower 70s for most of the area and upper 70s or lower 80s 

across coastal NE NC. A rather active wx day has transpired 

across the region today with our CWA favorably positioned in the

right entrance region of a jet streak aloft. Additionally, 

frontogenetic forcing from the nearby CAD front has served as a 

focus for conditional symmetric instability (CSI). All of this, 

combined with an anomalously moist airmass and saturated 

profiles (PWATs 1.6-2"), produced areas of moderate to heavy 

rainfall from this morning into this afternoon. This was 

especially the case over south-central VA where a rather large 

area of 3-5" of rain fell, with a few spots near 6". 0.5-1.5" 

fell further to the NE into interior SE VA, with lesser amounts 

elsewhere. 



The forecast into tonight is of rather low confidence. 

Compounding this has been the rather poor model performance, 

with models slow to pick up on the heavy rain potential. Still,

am seeing some emerging signals among the HRRR and respective 

ensemble guidance (particularly the REFS) to similar 

convective/rainfall behavior tonight. There is not a particular 

area to specifically mention, but the flooding threat is 

generally highest in the areas that saw significant rainfall 

earlier today. The earlier Flood Watch has been expanded NE into

interior portions of central and SE VA, along with extended in 

time until 8 AM Thursday morning. Aerial QPF is quite low (on 

the order of 0.25-0.75"), but there is potential for localized 

spots to again receive up to 2-3". Overnight lows will be milder

tonight given widespread cloudy skies and in the 60s inland to 

around 70 F at the coast. 



A shortwave will dislodge from the parent trough W of the area 

Thursday, leaving a weak cut-off low over the the higher 

terrain of NC/SC. Therefore, the forcing for convection will 

become increasingly nebulous by Thursday afternoon. Still, 

slightly warmer temperatures and slowly increasing humidity 

levels could lead to a few widely scattered showers and 

isolated storms. While instability levels may be a tad higher 

compared to the previous days, there is not support for anything

more than a slight chc of thunder. The coastal trough offshore 

will also sharpen during the day and NE winds could become gusty

to 20-25 mph, especially along the coast. High temps warm into 

the upper 70s to around 80 F.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...

As of 340 PM EDT Wednesday...



Key Messages: 



- Trending drier for Friday and the weekend with low pressure

  remaining well offshore. 



- A gradual warming trend is also expected with highs in the mid

  80s by Saturday. 



The high pressure system should begin sliding off the New

England coast Friday. However, latest model consensus continues

to be less aggressive with the nearshore moisture Friday as 

weak low pressure gradually develops. The best chc for 

showers/storms would be across far SE VA and NE NC and PoPs are 

30-50% in these areas and <20% elsewhere (where the high should

keep mainly dry conditions in place). The weekend continues to 

trend drier and would not be shocked if the entire area remains 

dry and mostly sunny. This is because the offshore low pressure 

now looks to remain well away from the area. While this low does

eventually have some chance at becoming tropical or 

subtropical, little to no impact is expected across our CWA. 



Highs Friday in the upper 70s to lower 80s and lower to mid 80s

Saturday. Lows Friday night in 60s inland to around 70 F at the

coast.



&&



.LONG TERM /SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

As of 350 PM EDT Wednesday...



Key Messages:



- Trending warmer to near to above normal levels with 

  increasing humidity Sunday into early next week. 



High pressure settles overhead Sunday into early next week.

Aloft, the flow remains quite weak with a trough over the 

Midwest. Highs trend to seasonal normals Sunday- Monday (back 

into the mid 80s to lower 90s) and potentially above normal by 

Tuesday. Overnight lows also warm back into the 70s. There is 

not much support at the sfc or aloft for convection, so PoPs are

generally 20% or less.



&&



.AVIATION /01Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

As of 835 PM EDT Wednesday...



Widespread light to locally moderate rainfall persists over the

region to begin the 00z TAF period. Predominate VFR conditions

prevail, with pockets of MVFR along the coast, including at KSBY

and ORF. Overall, the rain continues to decrease in coverage 

and intensity as it shifts east. Expect, MVFR to LCL IFR CIGs to

be the primary issue overnight with the best chances for rain 

and sub-VFR flight conditions expected at PHF, ORF and ECG

initially, spreading eastward to RIC after 06-08z lat tonight 

into Thu morning. Guidance is trending toward IFR conditions SW

of RIC where the heaviest rainfall occurred earlier in the day,

with MVFR developing at ORF-ECG-PHF and eventually RIC

overnight. E-NE winds will increase by Thu morning closer to 

the coast with some gusts to 18-20 kt possible.



Outlook: Rain chances remain elevated into Thu afternoon, 

highest across SE VA and NE NC, then tapering off and becoming

slightly lower on Friday (though again, highest in the SE). 

Some additional flight restrictions are possible tomorrow night

into Friday mainly for CIGs. Uncertainty is higher for the 

weekend with low pressure lingering along or just off the SE

coast, so there is still a chc for mainly diurnal showers and a 

few tstms Sat, again highest along the coast.



&&



.MARINE...

As of 300 PM EDT Wednesday... 



- Increasing winds and seas are expected through this week. 

  Small Craft Advisories are in effect for all waters. 



- High risk for rip Currents is in effect across all beaches

  through this week.



Strong pressure remains N of the region this afternoon, with a weak 

coastal trough offshore of the southeast CONUS coast. Winds are 

generally E-NE at 10-15 kt. However, a few spots in the western 

Chesapeake Bay are 15-20 kt with gusts to 25 kt. These winds appear 

to be enhanced by the effects of showers and convection W of the 

bay. Because of this, decided to start the Small Craft Advisory for 

the bay and lower James River a few hours early with the 1 PM 

forecast update. A brief lull in the winds is possible later this 

afternoon and evening before NE winds increase again tonight as the 

coastal trough to our S sharpens and weak low pressure develops 

offshore of SC. Elevated winds are expected across the entire marine 

area tonight through at least Friday as the gradient remains 

compressed with the high persisting N of the area. Regarding the 

headlines, the SCAs for the bay, lower James, and Currituck Sound 

have been extended through Friday morning, with SCAs for the ocean 

extended through Saturday morning due to high confidence in elevated 

seas. The developing low pressure system could also take on tropical 

characteristics as it begins to move out to sea Saturday, though it 

is looking less likely that this system will have any direct impacts 

to the area (outside of the continuing onshore flow). 



NE winds continue into Saturday though the gradient should be 

weakening by then. Therefore, marginal SCAs are possible and most 

probable in the lower bay and coastal waters (due to seas). High 

pressure then builds over the area Sunday into early next week and 

winds should finally be solidly sub-SCA for all of the waters and 

generally 10 kt or less. 



3-4 ft seas this afternoon should increase to 4-5 ft tonight and 

then 5-7 ft Thursday and Friday. There is some potential for higher 

seas (on the order of 8-9 ft) offshore of the NC coastal waters 

Thursday night. ~5 ft seas persist through Saturday before dropping 

to 3-4 ft early next week. Waves in the bay will also remain 

elevated to 2-4 ft at times, with 5 ft possible at the mouth 

Thursday and Friday. 



A HIGH rip current risk remains in effect through the end of the 

week and likely into the weekend as well. Nearshore breaking

waves of 3-5 ft will be likely, with 4-7 ft possible Friday.



&&



.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...

As of 300 PM EDT Wednesday... 



Increasing NE flow will allow for tides to build across the area 

through Friday. This may allow for stronger flood tides into the Bay 

and only weak ebbing out of the bay, possibly lead to nuisance to 

minor tidal flooding starting as soon as the Thursday late 

afternoon/evening high tide. With the persistent NE flow, it is 

possible that elevated tides will continue through Saturday. In 

addition, ocean tides will also become elevated, and combined with 

surf of up to 6 or 7 feet, some localized nuisance to minor coastal 

flooding is possible along the coast as well.



